    23	9. **Telling.** At the bell, on the first flight, into Lira's ear: "Twelfth. His. Lean." (placed). Brom and Karis are on the stair and give their word for it; Seln and Oryn are told afterward (Seln that night). Why Cleon may be told and Vastin still may not (Cael's own reckoning, on the page): the taking from Cleon goes on every hour that he kneels at that man's elbow, and the man has a rule that he is told what a thing is once he has looked; three of the five who own the secret are on that stair to ask. The eleventh sits unused, the man it came from was the office the six kept it from, and it cannot be told without the whole. The debt to Vastin gets heavier, not lighter, and is entered so.
    24	10. **At the board.** Cael tells Cleon alone, in the order he has it (reported, not recited, so that the notice is printed once). "You did what." and "That's not in the classification system. That's not *supposed* to be possible. That's — " are whole; in a two-person scene they can only be Cleon's: PLACED, and flagged. "No," … "No. I'll not say that one. Not down here. Not to you." is elided: MARKER. The cup of water (mine: he pours it from the jar and sets it on the table's bare corner; "for your eyes"). The warning (Tier A 10) is elided between its halves: MARKER. Then the plan's narration line, whole: "He went back to the carving." He looks away from the hand that held his lamp, on purpose.
    25	11. **The stair.** Cael and Vastin bring the case up together. The twelfth's lines are read there, slowly, in the plain words in which he will have to write them: the Tier A 13 unit, placed once, in italics, with the frame sentence, and after it *He looked away. Kept.* and *Not a man.* The eyes are paid on the last flight; the old man, above him and facing him, tells him the treads. Chapter 35 closes on the plan's sentence: "Cael did not tell anyone. He remembered." He does not write the warning in the log either: a thing in that book is a thing told to Lira.
    26	12. **The tail (night of the 291st into the 292nd; set piece; mine within the packet's limits).** Seln and Cael; Lira and Brom keep the loft, Karis and the stall. Seln teaches three rules. Route: the painted door; the open place and the trough (the watch's handcart and its lantern as cover); the brokers' street; the rope-walk, three hundred yards of open shed, where the hood stops half-way to see who comes round; the tar yard and the quay; the ring road under the second wall; the climbing street to the blue gate. Every fragment asks and is told no; Shadow hardest, since it is night. The hood warms his hands at the keepers' brazier with his back to the street, is let through the wicket, and turns in it for a breath. Cael walks up to the portcullis groove and stops: no card by night, no tier ever. A keeper says "First bell," kindly. No faces, none taken, nothing learned but patience. Cost: a night; tar on his hands; a small bill in the eyes for one look he did not mean (the rope-walk). Seln's finding: it was worth one night to learn that it is not worth two.
    27	13. **The 292nd below.** Cleon marks where each run ends (the lean gives the word-breaks). Karis: seventeen on the evening of the 291st; twenty-one at the bell of the 292nd; her first word that is not a number is *and*; she takes her glove off the six-sign word and it is not the word she wished for (mine; flagged: it is the plain word *set*, as a stone or a stake is set). Cleon does not look at the lamp's hand and now takes care not to. At the bell he says that the bundle will be untied in the morning.
    28	14. **The parcel (292nd evening).** Hesk's dividers come by the courier with the cut hat, who is lying the night at the inn on his way to the Lowmarch landing with the bag, and knows Brom's back from the western bank. Brom's thumb on his tally; no name. Hesk's note (Tier A 15): whole, placed.
    29	15. **The meal.** Brom's ruling: a parcel that has come that far is eaten on. The lower room; four marks; **130**. Two cups kept: Karis's (she is in the loft with her letters and will not come down until the twenty-first is found twice) and Oryn's. Seln's slip, read and burned, "Closed." (placed; what was on it is not given: FLAGGED). Vastin and the cups: "Out here you count who's owed a seat," … "It's a different ledger." is elided and its speaker unpinned: MARKER, written so that Vastin or Brom could own it. The table's joints (the twelfth; Brom reads the same table by eye).
    30	16. **The inventory (292nd night, the loft).** The first in the ledger form written inside walls. Entries: the count ("Eight days inside"); *Nineteen* and the twelve; Hesk's lines (the S14 log unit, placed); and fourth, the light. **The light:** the crown's light has come in beside the blanket every night as a pale line on the boards. For a count of one it is not steady. He feels the weight of an instrument turning toward him and says to himself that a needle swings to iron and nobody says the needle has seen it. He does not cross the loft; he does not lift the blanket; he does not look again. Entered in the form of the 168th (claim; evidence; ruling: hold). Then the unit "The promise stands. Not the steps. Not the square." (placed).
    31	17. **Oryn (cutaway; two scenes; the night of the 292nd and first light of the 293rd).** Her fourth gate, unnamed. A girl of the hold in her fourteenth year; the hold's old woman scratches a ring on the threshing floor at first light, as is done inside on a chalked board; no office, no plate, no clerk. Oryn is asked to stand by and does, with her hands in her sleeves. She watches the child's eyes go to the edge of her sight after a thing nobody else can see, and thinks of one that went out. The child tells her grandmother in her ear; Oryn does not learn the word. Nobody is hurt; nobody dies; she lays hands on nobody without asking. She decides that the mill will not lose its day, whatever the road costs her, and that the reading she owes will be as deep as she goes, asked first. She has heard of no leaf.
    32	18. **The leaf (293rd morning).** Lira and Brom take the carried lamp to the eastern gallery to put back twelve tall tally-books (the last day of his week; his galleries as he found them); Karis keeps Cael at the desk. Cleon unties the bundle. The object first: a leaf of skin the colour of old horn, written with a point and not a pen, in the floors' letters; a place in it scraped thin, four or five letters wide, with one upright stroke left at its edge. The silence is long. Karis reads it aloud once: the Tier A 1 unit is elided: MARKER. She asks that nobody say it back. Her single paraphrase (mine, with the plan's three points): the word at its head is the word on Vastin's page (the page is shown to Cleon now, after he has looked); the §9 fragment "a title, not a name; the name's gone" is a partial quotation: MARKER; by its own count of years it was written about four hundred years ago (the one use in my prose); and it says what the apparatus is for. *Older. Made. Us.*: owner unpinned (the map's heading says "Karis's … finding"; the packet sets it after the paraphrase): MARKER, written so that either could own it. Cael catalogues the room, not himself.
    33	19. **The chronicle.** A second leaf from the bundle, paper, in the old town's ink hand, which Karis reads at sight. One line, whole, placed, read as far as a hole a thumb wide. "Marked." (Karis) / "Marked." (Cleon), placed. A slip laid in it. No gloss by anybody, the narration included.
    34	20. **Vastin reaches.** His sentence is given only as its stopped end ("… the mark that is given at the kindling —"): MARKER. Karis: two pages, in few words, and the ruler line, whole (Tier B), placed. He names what he was doing (drafting) and stops.
    35	21. **The copy.** Karis writes the leaf out fair, once; the Tier A 3 line is placed as she writes it, with the gap ruled as a small empty box. Lira and Brom come back. "Tonight. On paper."
    36	22. **Vastin (cutaway; two scenes; the afternoon and the stair).** *Aimed.* He goes over imperfect, then designed (his Book 6 line, whole, in memory), then the third word. He goes over what he signed. His sentence about the mark stays stopped in his own head: he puts it down each time it rises, by the rule a small grey man gave him at a spring. No sigils, no plates, no dates, no seals named. He asks for a quarter-hour's start on the stair and takes it alone, backward, in the dark.
