   145	## Changes made while drafting (written after the draft)
   146	
   147	- **Scene counts.** Chapter 35 has six scenes (the stair is divided at the last flight); chapter 36 has six (the groove and Seln's finding are divided); chapter 37 has seven (the light is its own scene after the inventory's first three entries). Chapters 34, 38, 39 and 40 have five. Thirty-nine scenes in all.
   148	- **The sixteenth letter** is a number-word after all (the old word for twenty, a score), so that Karis still reads nothing but numbers on the 291st; her first word that is not a number is *and*, on the 292nd.
   149	- **Vastin on the stair at the bell (291st).** He starts a quarter of an hour early and is on the first landing when the others reach the stair's foot; Cael tells Lira on the bottom treads, under his noise; when Cael comes back from the board the old man is three steps above the first landing, and they bring the case up together from there. On the 293rd (his cutaway) he takes the whole stair alone and is three steps from the iron at the bell.
   150	- **The stair is straight** (three flights of nine with two landings, as chapter 28 shows it): nothing in my chapters turns a corner on it.
   151	- **The leaf's physical form (mine):** twenty-six lines on its first face; a list on its back, not gone into; the bundle lay between two thin boards of beech; under the lower board a dozen or more leaves of paper in the old town's ink hand (the chronicle). Cleon's bones of it: one hand, one sitting, ruled first with the same point, a fair copy; "scraped since it was written; I can't tell you by whom."
   152	- **Who heard the reading:** Karis, Cleon, Cael, Vastin. Lira and Brom were in the eastern gallery with the carried lamp, putting back twelve tally-books. Seln was above ground. So three people read the page at the fire.
   153	- **Karis's "two pages" at the fire** stops Brom at "So the floors are —"; she names nothing for the second page.
   154	- **Lira closes the gap beside the blanket** on the night of the 292nd, without looking; there is no line on the boards afterward.
