   316	
   317	The floor was of beaten clay, in the open, with a low kerb of stones about it.
   318	
   319	They were all there by the first grey: the whole hold, thirty or more, standing off beyond the kerb with their breath smoking, and not one of them speaking above a murmur. Oryn sat on the bench by the gate. It was forty yards. She had her hands in her sleeves.
   320	
   321	The old woman drew the ring.
   322	
   323	She walked it backward, bent, with the stick's point in the clay; and when the two ends met she stepped over the line, and held her hand out, and a girl came across the floor to her.
   324	
   325	She was thin, and tall for her age, with her hair in one plait and a sheepskin on her shoulders that was too big. She was frightened, and she was trying to walk as though she were not. Oryn had a square face that showed little, and she kept it so; but she had looked for that walk, and it was there, as it always was. Every one of them came across the clay like a person carrying a full pail.
   326	
   327	The girl stepped into the ring, and the old woman stepped out of it.
   328	
   329	Nothing happened that a watcher could have named.
   330	
   331	There was no light, and no sound, unless it was a ewe somewhere behind the wall. A thin child stood in a scratched circle with her hands shut at her sides, and a hold stood round her and held its breath.
   332	
   333	Then her head turned.
