   267	He sat down against the third box, and took out Hesk's book, and the dividers slid from it into his lap. He set the day at the head of a clean leaf.
   268	
   269	*Two hundred and ninety-third day. Treswick; the yard, and then the loft; one in the stall. A hundred and thirty. A leaf read once, below, by Karis. A copy made. Read at a fire by the three who had not heard it. The reader came up, and ate half a bowl. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
   270	
   271	He held the pencil over the leaf for some time.
   272	
   273	Then he ruled a line under what he had, and wrote nothing beneath it.
   274	
   275	Lira had turned on her pallet. She put out her hand for the book, as she always did, and read the short lines by the last of the lantern; and she turned the leaf over, to see whether the rest was on its back.
   276	
   277	"That's all?"
   278	
   279	"That's all that's for the book."
   280	
   281	"You said it better than that. In the yard."
   282	
   283	"I said it to you. All of you. You've got it." He took the book from her, and shut the brass into it. "It doesn't want keeping. It's not going anywhere."
   284	
   285	She thought about that, with the slow arm uppermost and her eyes on the slates.
   286	
   287	"No," she said. "I don't suppose that it is."
   288	
   289	There was a bearing on a calfskin, in the second box, with four rings on it and a line that ran off the edge. There was a set of marks in a ruled square which nobody could yet read. There was a bundle on a shelf with its tape untied, and the back of a leaf to be gone through in the morning; and after the morning there was a gate, and a bridge on five piers, and a road that went the way they had come and farther.
   290	
   291	He blew out the lantern.
   292	
   293	The blanket lay close to the frame, where a girl's thumb had pressed it. There was no line on the boards.

