    15	He set the middle box against the rock where it went and stood by it, and Brom set the first box down and sat on it with the foot out, and Lira put the third box down and stood, and Vastin set the case down and did not sit, on the knee, because sitting was a thing he had decided a hand did not do until the reader did, and nobody had told him that and he had worked it out from six days of watching Cael, which was, Cael thought, the first thing the man had learned by watching rather than by being told, and was not nothing.
    16	
    17	---
    18	
    19	"Today I'll show you the inside of it," Cleon said, "because when I've finished the week I'm going to tell you a thing you'll not believe from the outside, and I want you to have seen how I see before I tell you what I saw."
    20	
    21	He said it to Karis, over the Hall's ninth sheet, on the sixth day of his week, with the board beside the sheet and the bound leaf of his own notes open on the other side, and Cael stood at the table's corner with the lamp a hand to the left of the sheet and held it still.
    22	
    23	"You've watched me read the bones," Cleon said. "Bearer, carried, ornament. That's the Path from outside — a man pointing at a wall. Now the inside. Sit where you can see the sheet and not my hand, because the hand's a habit and the habit's not the thing."
    24	
    25	Karis moved. Cael did not. He was labour, and labour held the lamp, and the lamp was two feet from the sheet and two feet from the man's hand, and he had held it there all week and was, he found, the person in the room best placed to watch a thing from inside, because the man had stopped seeing him.
    26	
    27	"A record," Cleon said, "sits. Every mark's where it is because a thing happened and somebody put the mark down after. You read it and the marks are *still*. They describe. Nothing in them wants anything." His hand went flat on the board, on his ten-year piece, and his eyes went to the unfocused place between the board and the sheet. "An instruction *leans*. Every mark's where it is because a thing is meant to happen next, and the mark's pointing at the next thing. You put your attention on it and it's not still. It has a direction. It's built so that the eye goes *along* it, bearer to bearer, in an order, and you can feel the order as you can feel which way a stair goes in the dark by where the treads are. That's what I see. Not words. The lean."
    28	
    29	He read the sheet.
    30	
    31	Cael watched him read it, from two feet, holding the lamp, and had, for the length of the reading, the thing he had had beside a plane in a cut: the sense of a craft being done at the edge of his eye by a body that was not hiding it. Cleon's hand did not move. His eyes did not move, either — that was the thing Cael had not seen from the wall on the other days — they held the unfocused place and did not travel the sheet as a reading eye travels, and it was the *attention* that travelled, and the attention went along the sheet's fourth row bearer to bearer, and Cael could not see it go and could see that it went, because the man's breath changed at each bearer, a little, as a man's breath changes on a stair when his foot finds the tread.
    32	
    33	"There," Cleon said, at the row's end. "Left to right. Six bearers, in order. It leans. The whole row goes somewhere. A record doesn't go anywhere; a record's a heap. This is a stair." He took his hand off the board. "That's the inside. That's the whole of the Path, and I've never once shown it to anyone, and I've shown it to you because in a day or two you'll need to have seen a thing lean, so that when I tell you, at the week's end, which way all of it leans you'll believe your own eyes and not mine."
    34	
    35	"Noted," said Karis, and wrote, and Cael saw her hand shake once and steady, because she had seen a thing lean and had not known that a made thing could.
    36	
    37	She sat with it for the time it takes a woman to decide whether a thing she has just seen changes the shape of ten years' work, and decided that it did, and did not say so; she said, instead, in the voice for a finding, "The strata lean too. I've never called it that. The oldest layer under a floor's not a heap either; it's laid so the next one sits on it. I thought that was weight." She looked at the sheet. "It's not weight. It's the same thing you just showed me, in stone, from below." And Cleon looked at her for a moment as a man looks at somebody who has just said a thing he has waited thirty years for someone else to say first, and said, "Yes," and nothing else, because a thing said by somebody else does not need saying twice.
    38	
    39	And Cael stood with the lamp, and had it.
    40	
