     1	# Chapter 14 — Dividers
     2	
     3	The runner came to the street door on the two hundred and ninety-second day with a thing wrapped in oilcloth the size of a hand and a letter folded round it, and said a name at the door that was not the crew's and was, and Brom, who was nearest, paid the runner a copper for the carrying and did not ask where it had come from, because the wrapping said Denvash in a hand Cael would have known across a yard.
     4	
     5	He catalogued the parcel before he opened it, because a thing from Hesk was a thing you owed a description before you owed it a use.
     6	
     7	Oilcloth, waxed, the kind the man wrapped a finished piece in for a customer who had a road to walk it on. Tied with the flat cord he used, in the knot he used, which was a knot that came undone with one pull and did not come undone by accident, and Cael had watched him tie it a hundred times at a bench with his hands going faster than his eyes and had never once managed it himself. A letter round the whole, folded twice, addressed on the outside in the dry square hand to *the boy with the log, at the edge, wherever the edge is this season*, which was the whole of the direction and had been enough, because a courier chain that ran from a bench in Denvash to a bench in Lowmarch and a river ferry and a broker's runner at a street door in the outer district of a city on the Line was a chain of people who knew what an instrument-maker's parcel looked like and passed it along by its shape.
     8	
     9	Karis was not there. She had gone up to the registry at its opening and down with Cleon on the seventh day of the week he had asked for, alone with him by her own asking, and she had said at the yard door, without looking round, "Today he tells me," and had gone down the stair with her bound hand against her coat and the flat under her other arm and had not come up at noon, and nobody had gone to see why, because a woman a day from the thing she had hunted her whole life was not a woman you went to see about.
    10	
    11	So it was five at the inn's long table, and the parcel, and a chair nobody sat in.
    12	
    13	He did not open it at once. He catalogued the five first, because a thing was about to be opened and he wanted the room from before, as he had wanted a back room from before a pack was opened: Brom at the table's end with the foot up on the empty chair, which was what an empty chair was for; Lira across from him with her two hands on the wood, the left one open, because she opened it now without thinking, which was all that twenty-seven days had done; Seln at the door end, facing the street door, because a street door in a city where a team knew where they slept was a thing somebody faced; and Vastin, on the knee, at the table's side, with the stick against the bench and his hands on his knees, at a crew's table on a working day for a thing that was not work, and not knowing what the thing was, and not asking.
    14	
    15	He pulled the cord. It came undone with one pull.
    16	
    17	---
    18	
    19	Dividers.
    20	
    21	He had not known what the parcel was and had known, from its weight in the runner's hand, that it was brass, and had thought *a second edge*, because a second edge was the thing a man might send to a boy who had one, and it was not. Two arms, the length of a hand, hinged at the top on a pin that turned without a sound, tapering to two points fine enough to prick a sheet and not tear it, and a small brass wheel on a screw across the arms' middle that held them open at a width and would not let the width change until the wheel was turned. He knew the tool. He had watched Hesk use one at the bench — walk it along a rule, point over point, to lay a length off a drawing and put the same length on a piece of work, and back, so that a thing built in a shop came out the size the drawing said — and had never once had one in his hand, because a divider was a maker's tool and he had been a boy who watched. He put the points together and there was no light between them, and he entered that, because an instrument that closes true is the first thing you check and the only thing you cannot fix.
    22	
    23	He opened it. The arms went apart without a sound, as a thing goes that has been made by a man who does not tolerate sound in a hinge, and stopped where the wheel held them, and he turned the wheel a quarter, and they went a hair wider, and stopped.
    24	
    25	A second tool. Not a second edge; the thing that went with an edge. An edge told you a line was straight; dividers told you how long it was, and carried the length somewhere else, and put it down again the same. He sat at the long table with the thing in his hand and understood that Hesk had sent him, across a continent, in two parcels a winter apart, the two halves of a single competence, in the order a maker teaches them: first the straight, then the measured.
    26	
    27	He turned the wheel back. The arms closed. He opened them again, to the width of the straightedge's first mark, and walked the points along the table's edge, point over point, as he had watched a man do it at a bench a hundred times — eleven steps to the table's end — and found that a table in an inn on the Line was eleven of Hesk's first marks long, which was a fact about a table that nobody had ever needed and that he now had, and he understood that this was what the tool was for: not the measuring; the *carrying*. A length taken here and put down there, the same. He closed the arms.
    28	
    29	The note was three lines.
    30	
    31	*A rule says a line is true. A divider says how far, and carries it. You've the first. Here's the second, and mind the points; they'll go through a thumb as easy as a sheet. Lines are for finding out how far a thing is from another thing. Everything is. — H.*
    32	
    33	He read it twice. Not the wrong way round; the right way up, as it came, because it was addressed to him and a thing addressed to you is a thing you read as it was written. Then he folded it, and put it in the satchel, flat, against the log, where the others were, and put the dividers in his coat, in the pocket where the straightedge was, and Brom, who had watched all of it with the face he used for a wall somebody else was pricing, said, "He makes good things," which was the whole of Brom's opinion of Hesk and was, from Brom, an essay, and the two brass things lay against each other in the pocket as they had lain, he supposed, in a maker's drawer, and made a small sound when he moved, and he found that he did not mind the sound.
