    70	
    71	That was the custom, and he kept it without the occasion, because the custom had never been the occasion. He had written an inventory at the end of a year on a floor above Ostrand and at the end of a year in a marsh, and at other times when a thing was worth an inventory, and the form was the form: the day, the place, the count, the money, the bodies, the things, and the one line under all of it that the year had earned. He had never written one inside a wall. He wrote this one at the long table, with the door open and the two cups at their places and the dividers in his coat, and it was the first entry in the plain hand that had ever been made in a city, and he noted that, because a first was a thing you noted.
    72	
    73	*Two hundred and ninety-second day. Treswick, the outer district, the inn on the ring road, the long table. Inside walls. Five at the table; one below; one north. A hundred and thirty in hand; the week paid to the day; nothing owed. Brom's foot walks; the shoulder carried, bound fresh two days back; the good one used wrong in a cut and told him so. Lira, two arms, twenty-seven days. Seln, nothing; a slip in the fire. Vastin, the knee bound, a month running; at the table. Karis, the hand bound, below, on the seventh day.*
    74	
    75	*Twelve. Wind, Pressure, Iron, Compression, Ember, Shadow, Storm, Anchor, Blade, Tide; Iron Wall, from a plane in a cut; Rune, from a lamp. The still place at the middle of them, not read, not touched, not the Quiet, not a fragment. A cord that reads everyone but me. A row of sheets that counts one short in my hands. A straightedge and now the thing that goes with it.*
    76	
    77	*Hesk sent dividers. Lines are for finding out how far a thing is from another thing. Everything is. I'll enter that as a rule and see what it's worth.*
