   101	He did not cross. He noted that too, because a healer had asked him for one thing in a log, and here was the first afternoon on which the thing she had asked for had cost him anything — he had wanted, in that count of one, to go up the stair and find out — and he had not gone, because it was written, and because a thing a man writes in a book at a fire is a thing the man keeps in the afternoon or it is nothing, and he was finding out, at a stair's foot, that it was not nothing.
   102	
   103	He turned his back on it.
   104	
   105	It was, he found, walking back along the ring road with the carts, a thing he could do. He had thought, at a fire with a healer's term in his mouth, that the not-going-near would be the easy half and the not-looking the hard, and it had been the other way round for eight days, and now he had both, and the both had cost him one breath at a stair's foot and was going to cost him more, and he could carry it. A tool for finding out how far a thing was from another thing lay in his coat. He did not take it out. He knew how far. Four tiers. He had counted them from below on the first day and he was not going to count them again from any nearer, and that was the measuring of it, and it was done.
   106	
   107	---
   108	
   109	The outer district after the meal, in the afternoon, with the carts on the ring road and the smoke going up from the roofs inside the first wall and the brokers' boards nailed up outside their yards with the day's prices on them, and the stair going up the bluff to the second gate, and above the second gate the second wall, and above that the third, and above the third the crown, and on the crown, pale and steady again, four tiers above where he was allowed to stand, the light.
   110	
   111	Cael, nineteen, keeping a healer's promise, not crossing, with his back to it — turned toward the way down, the carts' street to the landing and the yard door and the cellar stair and the iron door and the dark past it where a woman was reading a thing on Cleon's seventh day, the hole under the city where the machine that made that light could not reach, because everything down there was older than the light — and going down into it, with the straightedge and the dividers in his coat, brass against brass, making their small sound.
   112	
   113	The light behind him, and the count in his head the whole way down, and he counted, and did not hurry, and the stair took him under.

