    19	He had time, in the nothing, to catalogue how she had found it, because she had told him on the stair going down that morning, in eleven words, and eleven words from Karis on a stair was a report. Cleon had pulled it from the galleries thirty years ago — a bundle of leaves in a script he could read the bones of and not the words — and had set it on his own shelf in his own order and had read it, alone, for three decades, as a thing with a lean in it and no language; and she had walked in with three floors and a word, and the floors had given the language its letters and the word had given it a place to start, and on the seventh day the leaf had stopped being a thing with a lean and had started being a thing that said something, and she had read it, and had said nothing to the man beside her, and he had said nothing to her, and they had read it again by lamplight before the bell, and had come down again in the morning to read it a third time, because a thing found on paper is read three times before it is said once. That was the method. It had taken thirty years and a year and a week, and it had taken eleven words to report, and he had them.
