    45	He had not known it in a cut; a cut had been a minute of standing still with a crossbow above him, and the thing had gone in somewhere in the middle of the minute where he was not looking. He had not known it on a table in the sun with a healer's hands on him, because that had been her reading and not his watching. He knew it now, in a chamber under a city, with a lamp in his hand, at the row's end, when the man said *there* and the attention that had gone along six bearers came back to the man's eyes and stopped — because in the breath after it stopped, the row on the sheet, which Cael had looked at since the first row as forty-one kinds of mark, *leaned*.
