    47	Not much. The way a floor leans that you have stood on for an hour before you notice it is not quite level. Six marks in a row that had been six marks, and were now six marks with a direction, left to right, the eye going along them bearer to bearer whether he told it to or not, and the row going somewhere, and the somewhere not a word — he could not have said what the row said; it did not say — but a *lean*, a made thing's lean, the thing a maker had put in it so that the next thing would happen. He looked at the fifth row. It leaned. He looked at the board. It leaned the same way.
    48	
    49	He did not use it. He had not used the eleventh either. He stood with the lamp in his hand and the twelfth thing in him and did not turn it on anything but the sheet it had come off, because a thing that had come to him from a man working in front of him was a thing he owed the man the knowing of before he owed himself the using, and because there was one place in him he was not going to turn it, and he knew that place, and he had been told by a healer not to touch it, and had been told by this man, on the third day down, that the instrument did not work on a man, and he was going to find out which of those two things was true by asking neither of them tonight.
    50	
    51	He held the lamp. Cleon turned his head a finger's width, and the light was there, and Karis, across the table, who had watched him not turn the twelfth thing on anything, looked at him once, as she had looked at him on a road with a cord in his hand, and said nothing, and wrote nothing, because it was not hers to enter and she knew whose it was.
    52	
    53	The rest of the day was the rest of a day in the chamber, and he catalogued it because a day in which a man had come to own a twelfth thing should be catalogued as an ordinary day, so that the twelfth thing would not think itself all of it. Cleon and Karis at the table with the fourth row and the fifth and the board, the pencil going now, Karis's, in the notebook, faster than he had seen it go since a marsh. Brom on the first box with the foot out and the good shoulder held, held like a thing he had used wrong and does not intend to use again for a while. Lira standing, then sitting on the third box, then standing, because a room with three galleries going off it into the dark was a room she did not sit in for long. Vastin on the knee, beside the case, not sitting, being nobody a little better than the day before, which Cael measured by the number of times the man's hand moved toward the lamp and stopped: four, on the fourth day; two, today. And the twelfth thing in him, with nothing to do, leaning at every mark on every sheet on the table whether he asked it to or not, so that the chamber was full of small directions he had not had yesterday and could not now stop having, and he found that a made room was a thing that pointed, everywhere, at what its makers had wanted next, and that he was going to have to learn not to look, as he had learned not to look at a light on a hill.
    54	
    55	---
    56	
    57	He told him at the bell.
    58	
    59	That was the form. The nine had been taken in fights, or in a yard, or on a road, from people who had been using a thing at him or beside him, and the telling had been a thing he did after, when he could — to Lira first, always, and then the circle. The tenth had been given, at a woman's direction, with the cost written down in advance, and he had told her on the table with her hands still on him because that was the covenant and the covenant was the only thing that made the tenth not a theft. The eleventh had been a plane held across a cut for him, and he had not told the man on a step, because the only currency the man took was a ledger, and the entry was made and the telling was not; that debt stood, and the twelfth was told because the eleventh was not yet. And the twelfth was a craft, shown by a craftsman to a researcher with a boy holding a lamp, and it had come to the boy because the boy had stood there, and it was not a reading of a person and it was not a fight, and the covenant for it was lighter than any of the others and it was still a covenant: you tell the man. Honesty was the only strategy that had ever survived contact with what he was, and he had learned that at fifteen from a woman with a ledger and had not once found it wrong.
    60	
    61	He told Lira first, on the stair up at the bell, in three words at her shoulder — *Twelfth. His. Lean.* — because Lira was always first, and had been since a hall, and she said, "Made things?" and he said, "Made things," and she said nothing else, which was how she kept it. Then he went back down.
    62	
    63	So he told him. At the bell, when Karis had gone up the gallery to the stair with Brom and the flat, and Lira had gone with them, and Vastin had picked up the case and gone, slowly, and the chamber was the man and the lamp and the boy, he set the lamp down on the table's corner and said it plainly, in the order he had it.
    64	
    65	"There's a thing you should know before I go up, because it's yours as much as mine and you'd want it in your book." He waited until Cleon looked at him — the first time, he thought, in six days that the man had looked at his face and not at the light he was holding. "I'm not a hand. I'm the boy on the paper. [SHATTERED], from Denvash, five years ago. I've a thing in me that the classification doesn't have a column for: when I stand beside a Path being used, long enough, close enough, I come away with a piece of it. Not the Path. A piece. Partial. Eleven pieces, before today. This morning, when you read the fourth row from inside and said *there* — I have the lean. I looked at the fifth row after, and it leaned. I didn't take it. I stood where you'd put the lamp. But I have it, and you should know that I have it, and that I'll not turn it on anything you wouldn't, and that I'll not turn it on you."
    66	
    67	Cleon did not say anything for a while.
    68	
    69	He looked at the lamp first, which Cael had set on the table's corner, because it was the thing he had been turning his head for all week and it was, Cael understood, the thing he was seeing now — a boy who had stood at his shoulder for six days holding a light, and had been, the whole six days, a thing the classification had no column for, standing in a room the classification had never been lowered into, taking a craft off a man's hands by holding a lamp for them. He looked at the sheets. He looked at the board, and at his own thirty years on the shelf behind him. He did not look at Cael. Not yet.
    70	
    71	Then he said, "You did what."
    72	
    73	It was not a question. It was the sound a careful man makes when a thing he has spent thirty years being careful about has just stood up in front of him and said its name.
    74	
    75	"Twelve," Cael said. "Yours is the twelfth."
    76	
    77	Cleon looked at him. He looked at him properly, for the first time — not at the carvings; at the boy — the way he had looked at Karis on a ring road when she had said *off floors*: as a man looks at the thing that proves the shape he has been right about, and finds that being proved right is a thing with a cost in it. And then he said, slowly, in the voice a man uses for a sentence he is building a word at a time and does not want to finish:
    78	
    79	"That's not in the classification system. That's not *supposed* to be possible. That's — "
    80	
    81	He stopped.
    82	
    83	Cael watched him stop. He had watched Karis stop at a slab, with a sentence about lamps in her mouth, and had watched a marshal stop at a spring three times, and this was neither of those; this was a careful man arriving at the edge of a thing he had read a piece of, on a board, for ten years, and seeing the boy in front of him standing on the far side of it, and choosing, with the whole of thirty years' care, not to say the sentence that would have joined them.
    84	
    85	"No," Cleon said, to himself. "No. I'll not say that one. Not down here. Not to you." He put his hand flat on the board. "I've had a piece of a thing for a decade and I've kept it in a hole because I've learned what happens to a thing the Compact finds interesting, and you are the most interesting thing I have ever had in this room, and I am going to be very careful indeed."
    86	
    87	He looked at the boy a moment longer, and did not say it.
    88	
    89	Cleon had a cup on the table. He had had it there all week — water, from the spring at the gallery's head, in a clay cup that had been a cup for thirty years — and he had not once, in Cael's seeing, drunk from it while he read, because a man who reads with his whole attention does not have a hand to spare for a cup. He picked it up now, and drank.
    90	
    91	"I'm going to pretend you didn't tell me that," he said, "for your sake, not mine. But I'll tell you one thing for free, since you can apparently take my whole trade by watching me work it for a minute." He set down the cup. "Everything I read is *made*. Stone, ink, seal — somebody built it, so I can see how. If that fragment ever reads something for you that shouldn't be *made* and reads it anyway — " He stopped. "Don't tell me. Don't tell anyone. Just remember I said it."
    92	
    93	He went back to the carving.
    94	
    95	---
    96	
    97	Vastin was on the landing when Cael came up to it with the lamp, after, with the case at his feet and the stick, waiting, because a hand who could not carry a case up a stair alone on that knee had learned this week to wait for the hand who could, and had not once said so, and Cael took the case's other strap without a word and they went up the stair together, the two of them, with the thing between them that a plane in a cut had put there.
    98	
    99	The man said, on the fourth flight, without looking round: "You told him."
   100	
   101	"I told him."
   102	
   103	"He looked away."
   104	
   105	"Yes."
   106	
   107	The man nodded, once, as a man nods who has spent forty years watching people be told things and knows what a careful man's looking-away is worth, and they went up.
   108	
   109	Cael took the log out, before he went up the last flight, because a thing that has come should be entered where it came, and he put his attention where the twelfth thing was, and it rendered.
   110	
   111	*Rune-adjacent. Cleon. Partial. Rune family. A made thing's lean — the direction a maker put in it; which mark bears, which is borne, which is nothing; whether a run sits or goes. Structure, not meaning: it will tell me a thing is an instruction and will not tell me what it instructs. Made things only. Not a river. Not a man. Costs the eyes, after. No use in a fight. Engagement field: inscriptive — the second field that is not a fight, after clinical; given, not taken. Tier equivalent unknown. Deployment: none. Twelfth.*
   112	
   113	He read it once, in the plain hand, on a stair under a city, with a man below him at a table who had just given him a thing by working in front of him and had then, deliberately, looked away from him, and he understood that the looking-away was the covenant's other half — that a craftsman who had been told a thing and had chosen not to see the boy who told it was keeping the boy the only way a careful man could — and he entered that too, in four words, under the notice, because it belonged there: *He looked away. Kept.* *Not a man.* He let it stand, and did not look at the place in himself where the eleven went round, and closed the log.
   114	
   115	The eyes paid on the last flight. Cleon had said they would — *costs me my eyes for an hour* — and Cael had entered it as the man's cost and not his, and it was his now: the lamp in his hand went to a smear, and the stair's edge to a darker smear, and the landing above to nothing he could put a step on, and he stopped, and stood, with the case's strap in one hand and the log in the other and the eyes doing what a craftsman's eyes did after a row had been read, and Vastin, a step above him, said nothing, and waited, and did not offer a hand, because the man had learned in a week what a hand was worth to a boy on a stair, and when the smear went back to a lamp they went up.
   116	
   117	Cael did not tell anyone. He remembered.
