     9	Not a board. Not a roll. A leaf — a single leaf, the size of two hands, of a stuff that was not paper and not skin and had been, Cleon said, both once and had stopped being either a long time ago: stiff, the colour of old horn, with a face to it that took a mark and a back that did not, so that it had been written on one side and had lain, for as long as it had lain, with its written face down against the next leaf of a book that had been a book once and was now this leaf and eleven others in a bundle tied with a cord that had gone to dust. The writing on it was not the Compact's and was not the four scripts on the shelves and was not the board's; it was older than the board's, Cleon said, and the board's hand had learned from it as a child's hand learns from a master's — the same lean; the same bearers; the runs going left to right and the bends at the ends of the runs the same shape — and where the board had been cut with a tool, this had been written with a point, in a black that had gone brown, and had been written by somebody who wrote for a living, because every line was the same height and every run started at the same margin, and a hand that does that is a hand that has been paid to.
