    36	
    37	"That's the passage," Karis said. "There's more on the leaf — a list of the measure's parts, the seat's, the tier's; I've the shape of it and not the words yet; it's the same instruction-make Cleon read on the floors, and it's older than the floors, and the floors were cut from it or from a thing like it. But that's the passage. That's what the citation on Vastin's page pointed at across a continent. That's the thing under the registry." She took her hand off the table. "I'll say what it says, once, in my own words, and then I've said it. It says the thing the Compact calls the Path system was *made*. By a person. The record's word for him is the one on Vastin's page — the Architect; it's a title, not a name; the name's gone. It says he made it about four hundred years ago, by the date at the leaf's head, which is a date I can read because the board taught me the numerals. It says he made it *for a reason*, and the reason's the only thing on the leaf the writer cared that anybody remember, and the reason is the un-bound." She looked at the word on the leaf, where it stood, in the antique hand, live, a thing somebody had written down as the name of a thing that walked in the land. "That's the word from the confiscated record. That's the word I've carried since a stratum. It's not retired here. It's not a limit. It's a *kind*. There were people the record calls that, before the measure and the seat and the tier and the mark, and the whole of the apparatus was set over the land so that there would not be any more of them."
    38	
    39	She sat back.
