    49	Karis, with her hand off the table and her face the colour it went when a finding was one she had been right about and had wished to be wrong, sitting very straight in a chair with no cushion on it, not writing, because the thing was said and the writing was after. Cleon, across from her, in the chair with the cushion, with both hands flat on nothing and his eyes on the leaf and not on anyone, a man who had had a piece of this for ten years and had just watched a woman get all of it, and had said one word in the whole of the reading, which was *lamp*, and was not going to say another, because there was nothing to say that the leaf did not say and a man who has not slept well for a decade knows the difference between a thing that needs saying and a thing that has been said. Brom, on the first box, with the foot out, looking at the leaf as he looked at a wall somebody else had priced — respect, and no intention of touching it — and something under the respect that Cael did not have a name for and would have called, in anyone else, fear, and would not call that in Brom. Lira, standing, with her two hands at her sides, not looking at the leaf. Looking at Cael. She had been looking at Cael since the word *kindling* and had not stopped, and he did not look back, because if he looked back he would have to have a face, and he did not have one yet.
