    84	
    85	---
    86	
    87	He had it. He had had it since the leaf, and had held it at a lamp for a day and up a stair and across a yard and through five people, and he had not let himself have it until they were done, because a thing you take should be taken after, and not in the middle of somebody else's taking.
    88	
    89	He took it now. He did not catalogue it.
    90	
    91	He stood up first. He did not know why, and did not ask himself; he stood up from the wall he had been sitting on and stood at the brazier's edge with the fire at his shins, because a thing you are going to take you take on your feet, and the five of them let him stand, and did not look at him, because the family's register for a thing being taken was to leave the taker his face. That was the discipline, and it ran the other way tonight: he had spent a day not letting himself count a thing, and now he let himself have the thing and did not count it, because a man who counts a thing like this has found a way not to have it, and he had promised a healer he would find out what the log did to him, and here was the one thing he was not going to put in the log first.
