books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-11.md:159:Nothing in him changed, and he catalogued it at the run: the stride over the stone, going out, and the nine ringing in him exactly as they had rung a stride inside, and the place where an Arbiter would have been as dark on the road as it had been on the floor. Then he was past Seln.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-12.md:63:She drank from the pan for a while first, and nobody talked to her.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-10.md:13:Lira inside. One stride inside and no more, walking the line's inner edge with her Arbiter gone; if it came back she had strayed out, and she would say *back*, and step in, and say *gone*. Karis on the line, a stride outside Lira, with her own sigil present, laying the cord end over end along the ground and counting cords, and strides within the cords, because she had a number for her stride she trusted. Brom outside Karis, marking: a stone set down at the end of every cord, or a scrub stem broken and turned, whatever the ground gave. Seln ahead, because the ground had not been walked. Oryn at the stone with the mule, because a mule could not be told to keep to a stride. Cael at Karis's shoulder with the book open, writing the counts as she said them, because his hand was the faster and hers would be full.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md:107:"Quiet ground's where the sigil goes to sleep. Yours. Everyone's. You walk a road, and there's a place on it where your Arbiter stops — not slows; stops — and you walk on past and it's back. There's a handful of them. Along the void roads, all of them. Carters walk round them, and have as long as there've been carters. The dogs live in them. Stillhounds. Nobody's asked them why. Why any of it, nobody knows and nobody's needed to, because the rule's *don't go in*, and the rule's never once failed anybody who kept it." He stopped, and the stopping was part of it. "That's what a carter knows. That's what I know. There's not a man on the ferry knows a word more."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md:121:"You'll know," he said. "Your Arbiter tells you. It stops."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md:183:"Everybody knows the quiet ground. Nobody goes in. I've had that under a slip since Thornwater, underlined, filed as a source and a bad one, because it was one woman on a cart and I couldn't ask her the next question. Today a man who has kept the only checkable record in this country for thirty years said the same two sentences, with a name in them, and a road, and a day-count, and a rule, and then a broker's slip put a price on it." She turned the book round on the table so that they could see the page, which was the three leaning sentences copied fair beneath the original in her own present hand, and under them, new: *The Fallow Ring. Fallow road, east of Thornwater, second day. Pike: the sigil stops at a line. The line: 'your Arbiter tells you.'* "That is a primary source — the first I have had on this side of the Line that is a place and not a rumor. I am going to go and stand at the line he says exists and find out whether my Arbiter does what he says it does. I would very much rather do that with the four of you than without. I've said that in the order I mean it, and I'll not say the last part twice."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md:255:*Twelve weeks. Seven contracts. Pike says there's ground out here where the Arbiter goes quiet, and nobody goes in twice. Karis has been waiting her whole life for a sentence like that. Seln says the contract was written by someone from inside. I say it pays. Two days east, through Thornwater, on the day the healer said. For twelve weeks nothing out here has read me. I'd like to see what a place that reads nothing does with that.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:13:Brom had the strap on and the cart's tail a length back from the ledge's root, the three boxes roped at its front, the down-slope wheel a hand from the edge. Seln was not looking at the ledge but back along the bench, at a thing Cael had walked past in the dark and catalogued as the one object on the traverse that was not a plate: a fin of darker stone standing out of the mountain at the bench's inner edge, forty paces behind them, a man high and three carts long. Seln looked at it as he looked at the places they would sleep.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:21:He heard it before he saw it. The whole ledge under her, from her boots to a length ahead, made one dry note, a plate going in a kiln, and then it was not a ledge. It was three carts' length of the mountain's edge going down into the gorge together, slowly and then not, with her on it.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:25:He had watched her burst four hundred times and owned a piece of it. It was not loud to the ear, or not only: air went somewhere all at once, a clap without the hand, and she was not where she had been. This was not that. This was Wind, hers, Iron Rank 1 and everything the rank had, the whole declaration spent at once, upward and back, and it crossed the gorge. He felt it cross. The far wall gave the clap back a breath later, smaller, and by then she was on the bench beside him on her hands and one knee, the ledge gone from under where she had stood.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:31:He did not see them turn. He saw the line be a different line, every back on the shelf pointed away from the ledge, away from the loudest thing that had happened on this mountain in three seasons, and then the line was running, and Karis had been right and wrong in one sentence. A step went uphill. A run did not: the shelf could not hold thirty animals the size of the cart at a run, the outer plates went out from under the first of them, and the herd came off the upper bench down the face in a front two hundred paces wide, on its feet, with the shale coming with it.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:59:The front came down the face at a gallop with the mountain under it and met a line it had to turn from, and it turned, as water turns at a stone, half left and half right, at a distance he measured afterward at eleven paces from the fin's face and could not have measured then, because there was no distance; there was a grey wall going past his shoulder at the length of his arm with plates the size of doors coming off it. The noise was inside the stone at his back. The down-slope half had nothing under its outer feet but the bench and then the edge, and some of it went over. He heard that: a different sound, and the far wall not giving it back. Most of it did not, and turned along the bench, and went.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:71:The cart stood forty paces on at the ledge's root, where the first half had not reached. The second half was coming down behind it at the slant the line on the shelf had run: further along, later, and further along was the cart. He could see them, fourteen or fifteen, a front coming down the face at the cart in a sheet of moving plates, and Brom under them, running down the bench with the bound arm loose, and he could count the seconds.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:85:The shale-back did not see him. It was not built to. It came past the cart at a gallop with its head down and its shoulder came round with it, a plate of armour the size of a tail-board moving at the speed of a falling thing, and took him under the left arm with the box in front of him, and he went where it put him.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:97:He counted the second half past the fin: fourteen. Then the sound going down the mountain toward the fan, and the shale still coming down, plate on plate, settling, and the gorge giving the last of it back, late.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:103:He had lost her at the ledge's root when he ran; everyone had. Now the bench was a different bench, plated over new to the edge, and she was forty paces off where she had landed, coming on her hands.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:107:She had gone for the fin when they went for it, on the ankle that had gone at the ford and gone again when she landed; he pieced that afterward from the marks in the shale. What had come down on her had not been an animal. It had been the bench: a plate going out under her foot as the second half came down and the whole shelf shifted, the leg going into the gap it left, and the plate above coming down on the leg with the mountain behind it. The plate was still there. Seln lifted it off. Under it the leg below the knee was the wrong shape. Not bent; the wrong shape, a line with an angle in it where there was not supposed to be an angle. The foot had no boot. The ledge had taken the boot. A strip of tape was still round the ankle above the bare foot, tied off at the shin.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:125:Lira: the right leg broken below the knee, the shape wrong and the skin whole, the foot bare, the ankle under the break gone twice and no longer the largest thing in the leg. Seln straightened it as far as it would go, with Brom holding her and her own hand in her mouth, and it was not straight. Two plates of the mountain went either side of it, and Karis's cord round the plates from knee to ankle, tight, so that the leg was a thing in a box. She did not faint, and he had never seen her work so hard at anything.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:157:The next day was the one the mountain kept. Seln went at first light with the pack on and came back before the sun was over the gorge: the lip held a walker, and below the lip the fan the herd had taken down was still going, in slides, one every hundred breaths, and a man on it was a stone on it. "Tomorrow," he said, "or it's two of us." The day was rationing and the leg. Karis woke and could not light a lamp and did not try again. Lira's leg went hot under the plates by noon, and the tape came off and went back on wetted, and she said nothing about it, which was how Cael knew. He counted breaths, and the slides on the fan, and the gorge, and did not take the log out.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:177:"Name's Oryn." She said it to the splint. "Tide. Healer." She had the cord off the plates and the tape off the ankle before anyone moved to help her. "A carter came into Oxhollow off the void road two days back at noon and said the shale-backs had come off the mountain onto the low road at a run, and the crew that was up on the traverse was overdue off it. I was at the ford." She looked up, at Lira. "When."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-06.md:209:"That's one," she said. To nobody. She drank from a skin, a long time. Then, to Lira: "Weight on it tomorrow. Not today. The bone's whole; it's new. It'll ache for a season and hold for the rest of your life. The ankle under it I've not touched; I've not got it in me this morning, and it's not going to kill you. Somebody tape it. You'll know how."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-05.md:51:The fan was the worst ground of the route, and Cael catalogued it from the bottom: a spill of broken shale the width of a field, lying at the angle at which shale stops, every plate the size of a hand or a door and none of them fixed to anything. Somewhere on the mountain above it a shelf ran along over the gorge, and the shelf was the traverse, and the fan was where a piece of the shelf had come down.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-05.md:59:He wrote that down first, at the first halt, because it was the fact everything else stood on. A shelf of grey shale along the mountain's face, a cart and a half wide where they came onto it and sloping toward the outer edge as a roof slopes — not steep, but not flat, so that anything set down on it wanted to go one way. On the left the mountain went up in the same stone, plated, and two hundred meters up, by the eye, a second shelf ran along it, greener than theirs: an upper bench. On the right the shelf stopped, and past where it stopped there was nothing for a long way down, and at the bottom of the nothing a white line that was a river.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-05.md:73:He said, at the second halt, that a cart on scree did not roll; it floated. The wheels sat on plates that sat on plates and the whole of it went where the slope wanted, which was over the edge, a finger's width at every turn of the wheel, unless the weight was over the axle and up-slope. He re-roped the boxes at every halt to keep the front one where it was, took the down-slope shaft himself as the counter, and walked the whole day with the cart's want against his chest, and it was — Cael watched for the word — a lock somebody else had built, being held shut by hand.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-05.md:103:The colour of the shale, which was why he had not seen it. Then, because he knew what he was looking for, the next one, and the next, and he counted them as he counted a crowd, left to right and again, and got twenty-nine and then thirty-one and then thirty, and kept thirty. Thirty animals along the upper bench, every one of them with its face down against the rock. Plated across the back in slabs that overlapped like the stone of a roof, grey going to rust as the shale went; the head small and low for the body and the legs short and thick under it; each one, by the eye at that distance, the size of the cart with its load and Brom in the shafts. They were eating lichen — the green was lichen, on the upper shelf where the wind did not scour it — and the sound he had taken for the gorge since the narrowing was them: a grinding, patient and low, thirty sets of teeth on stone, coming down to the lower bench and then back from the far wall, late.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-05.md:131:Karis sat on a flat plate at the camp's edge with the herd above her and the book on her knee and did not move for the first hour except her eyes.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-05.md:145:An animal at the shelf's outer edge had its weight on a plate that had been holding its weight all day, and the plate tilted. Not far. It went a hand's width at the outer side and slid its own length down the shelf's pitch with the animal on it, as a floor-board goes when the joist under it is gone — and the animal walked off it, uphill, without hurry, and the plate stopped against the next plate and lay there.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-05.md:153:"A plate went under one at the outer edge. It stepped off, uphill. The herd went uphill, one length, all at the same time. No leader. I've been looking for one since dawn and there isn't one; there's no animal that moves first. They moved as a unit. Away."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:87:It was hoof-cut and plated for a hundred paces where the herd had come off the fan and crossed it — shale on turf, as if the mountain had walked a piece of the way to Oxhollow and thought better of it — and then it was a road again, and empty. Seln read the ground and said twenty-seven, in a voice that meant the carter had been right. Karis lit the fire that night: one point, on the first try, the first thing she had lit since the road, and sat down after it, not hard. Oryn looked at her and said "Eat," and Karis ate.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-07.md:99:He saw it from the yard, which was as near as he went; it was hers, not a crew's. An old man with a chest that whistled. A girl with a burn up one arm that had been dressed by somebody who did not know how. A carter with a hand under a cloth — the hand, Cael thought, that she had said she would look at again. She took them in the order they were worst, not the order they had come, and nobody in the line argued the order, which told him how long the table had been hers. She went still over the chest for the seconds and then for the quarter-hour, and the whistle stopped. The girl's arm took the time an apple takes. The carter's hand she unwrapped and looked at for a long while and put both hands on, and said something to the man, low, and he nodded and went; and Cael did not know whether that had been the third until she sat down on the bench behind the table and drank, with the grey in her face, and said, to nobody, "That's three," and the line, which knew what that meant, thinned to the ones who only wanted looking at.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md:127:He watched her table that evening from the loft's door, because it was a room with people in it. Nine, as she had said, on the long bench under the lamp, in the order she had put them and not the order they had come: a child with a wrist first, because a child does not wait well; then the three that mattered; then the rest. She sat down once, between the fourth and the fifth, and drank, and did not sit long. Nobody on the bench asked her for paper. Nobody asked her tier. They asked her, one at a time, to put her hands on them, and she did, and that was the whole of what she was to the steading and what the steading was to her. Thornwater fed her. He saw now what it fed her for.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md:177:He had a place he could look. He had had it since he was fourteen, since eleven seconds in a registry room with a sigil in front of him that had come, and had looked at him, and had gone dark and stayed dark, and in four years it had never once had anything in it. He had stopped looking at it the way a man stops checking a pocket he knows is empty. He looked at it now, walking up the rise beside Karis, because a man who had kept a ledger for thirty years had said *your Arbiter tells you*, and he wanted to know what a dark thing would say.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md:197:"My Arbiter's gone," Karis said.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-09.md:229:*Quiet ground. Karis's Arbiter stopped at a line she can mark with a rock. So did Lira's, Brom's, Seln's, Oryn's — same line, same step. Mine didn't. Mine's been stopped since I was fourteen. I don't know what that means. Nobody asked. Tomorrow we go in.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md:45:Cael put that beside the sentence it referred to, in the place he kept such things, and found that the sentence now existed twice — once said, at a gate, and once written, in a book that had no institution above it. Vell had written her first line about him in a bound book at Ardenmere when he was fourteen, and he had walked four years on the strength of it. This was a margin note in a board-keeper's ledger. He did not rank the two. He noticed that he did not.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-02.md:217:*No instrument out here reads me. No Arbiter station, no assessment panel, no registry seat with a seal on it. The board reads what we did. I've spent four years being the case the instruments couldn't parse, and it turns out the cure was a country with no instruments. Write it plainly, because it's true: I'm free. First time.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-01.md:11:*No tier. No rank. No enrollment. And — I checked this morning from the top of the shelf, both directions, every morning for four years now — no file I can see.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-01.md:123:Lira, twenty paces ahead, was walking the way she walked on a floor in the last minute before a bout: everything in her wanting to go, nothing in her going. He knew what she could do to it. One burst — Wind, her own, Iron Rank One by an instrument's account — and the forty meters of scrub between her and it would lie flat, and the thing would either run or be found. She had said as much to nobody in the set of her shoulders. Then she said it in words, low, not turning.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-15.md:57:"Five sigils." Her hand went to five points on the table, one for each. "Wind. Iron Skin. Ember. Shadow. Tide. Iron rank one, Copper, no ranking, Bronze, Iron rank four. Five people, five Paths, five standings, five histories of a thing that showed up at fourteen and has been each of ours since." She looked at the five points. "Every one of them went out at the same stride. Not the same stretch of road — the same *stride*. Lira called it and I called it and I never once had to correct her by a foot. Every one of them went out the same way: not dimming, not failing, not going wrong first — there, and then not there. And every one came back the same: at the stride, in place, the clock started again without having lost the time. Lira's words. Oryn's. Brom's, if Brom used words."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-15.md:65:"You said it at the stone. It's entered as said, and Oryn said it after you, in her own words, with her hands on Brom's arm, and I've hers too." Karis's hand went back to the five points. "Five of them, and no difference among them at all. That's the finding, and here's why I've sat on it two days." She put her pen down, which she did when she wanted a sentence heard as a sentence. "An Arbiter is a personal spiritual entity. That's the registry's phrase, and it's the only phrase anyone has, and every practitioner in Valdris has been raised on it: the thing is *yours*, it came to you at your Kindling, it's as much a part of you as your own hand, and no two are alike. That's what we're taught. And five of them, no two alike, belonging to five people no two alike, went out at one stride, in one instant, in one way, and came back the same." She stopped, and got her breath past the throat, and said the rest in the torn voice as if the tearing were part of the point. "A personal spiritual entity should not fail the way five looms fail when somebody shuts the sluice on the wheel that drives them."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-15.md:131:"Observation. Inside the perimeter, at every trial and in the fight, five practitioners' Paths did not render: Wind, Iron Skin, Ember, Shadow, Tide. Inside the perimeter, at two trials and in the fight, the sixth practitioner's — " she stopped, and started the word again, and it came out level — "the sixth practitioner's *fragments* rendered. All of them. Iron read, the whole day. Wind evasion, a foot's worth, silent, at twenty meters in. In the fight: Shadow, Wind full, Pressure, Ember, Blade, Storm, Compression at contact, Anchor perceiving. Nine of nine. Witnessed by five. His Arbiter unchanged either side of the line, stated by him." She wrote the last word and lifted the pen. "That's the observation, and it's the largest thing on this table, and I have it, and I'm going to enter it exactly like that and not one word further."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-14.md:5:Fourteen. A certification office in Denvash, a sigil that lit and went dark for eleven seconds and came back with a word on it nobody living had seen. A year in an Unranked District with a notebook. A training floor, and a girl with a Wind Path who did not know she was teaching, and a sidestep his body made before his foot decided to, and a page in the log with a question mark on it. He gave her the first notice, and the format of it, and that he had never once received the other kind. He gave her the count — nine before this morning — and the sources, by Path and not by name, because the names were the circle's and she had not been in the circle; and then, because she was a healer and had had her hands on three of them, he gave her three names anyway. Brom's. Karis's. Lira's. He told her what the conditions were as he had them, and that the model said real and not fight, and that until an hour ago nobody had ever tested that sentence.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-14.md:75:"Mm." The lantern's thread did not move. "My father could hear the forge from the house. Every hammer. Thirty years. My mother said she'd never once heard it and he said that was because she'd never once listened." A pause. "He didn't mean it kindly. But it was true."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-14.md:125:"Tenth." She had the sheet on the middle box's lid, turned to face him: the columns, the curve she had been drawing since a training hall in Greyvane, ten points on it now. "First non-combat. First clinical. First one taken lying down." That last was not on the sheet, and she did not write it. "Source-grade Iron. The arithmetic says two days to an idle state and it's been two and a half, and the half's the new register — you've never had one that listens, and there's no comparison class for what it costs to learn to stop hearing something. So. Holding. The curve holds." She put her finger on the tenth point. "For now."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-14.md:169:Seln, at the door end, looked at the satchel, and said, to nobody, in the voice for an inventory: "Six went in. Six came out. He's counted her." He went back to his plate. "So has the broker."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-23.md:79:Lira went with her, one-armed, on the taped foot, down the ridge's near slope, a stride in from where Karis walked, and at forty paces Lira did not say *gone*. At sixty she did not. At a hundred, on a slope where at the Ring and the Stair and the Hall she had gone dark at forty, she was still walking with her hand a little out from her side and her Arbiter with her, and she stopped, and looked back up the slope at the others, and said nothing, and went on.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-23.md:83:Karis paced it in the last light, with Brom marking, because it was what she was for and there was a quarter of an hour of light to do it in. Not the whole circle — there was not the light for the whole circle, and she said so, and did what could be done, which was what she had done at the Ring: Lira on the line with her Arbiter gone, and coming back at a half-stride's stray, and calling it; Karis a stride outside her with her own sigil present, laying the cord end over end along the line westward from the stone; Brom setting a stone at the end of each, the count said aloud at each and Cael at her shoulder writing it; and nobody's boot a hair nearer the shape than the line was, until the light went off the shape at the middle and Karis stopped with the twentieth cord's last knot in her hand and her wrist turned the little it had turned at every cord. And from the first cord on, a bar of iron a hand's height out of the frost beside Lira's boots, and another a pace past it, and another, the whole way, which nobody spoke of until the count was done, because a count was a count and Karis was saying it.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-23.md:95:A line of them. Iron, a hand's height out of the ground, registry-stamped, numbered — thirty-four on the nearest, and thirty-four on the next, and thirty-four on the next, with the small mark beside it, the stroke and the bar — set not a stride apart as at the Stair but close, a pace apart, a pace, a pace, running away along the perimeter's curve east into the dusk as far as the eye went and west along it the other way as far as the eye went, so that the line Lira had called with her Arbiter was drawn on the ground in iron, by somebody who had come with a great many stakes and a great deal of time, and had not measured a circle. Had *marked* one.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-24.md:133:"I have ten things that aren't a Path," Cael said, eventually, to nobody in particular, "no tier, no rank, no file I can see — and five people, one of them on a route, who've walked into the quiet three times and come out." He turned it over once and let it stand. "And a line on a map that somebody drew before there was a registry to draw one."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-22.md:99:He catalogued that, because it was the whole of the chapter and he knew it while it was happening. The right wrist, on the right knee, the coat's cuff back from it, a foot from his own right hand on the track between them. He had the reading. He had had it awake at contact range for three weeks and had put it on one person who had asked him to and on himself, and he knew exactly what a hand on that wrist would give him: at ten, the Path — Force, Bronze, which he already had from a doorway — and nothing more. And past ten, if the man were still and willing, which he was not and had not been asked to be, in the minutes the long one took: what a Force practitioner was built like who had stopped writing to the registry six years ago and whom the registry had stopped asking; whether the not-reporting had left a mark in him the way a resigned ranking had left a mark in Karis, or a Compact left in form had left one in Seln; what the top of a wall was made of, from inside. He could know. Not everything. Enough.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-18.md:23:The carter's hand first — a crushed thing, the fingers gone the wrong colours, a wheel's work — and she had it between her palms already when they came down, and held it there through the count and past the count, and the man stood with his eyes shut and his other hand flat on the table, and when she let go the fingers were straight and the colours were the colours of a hand, and she said, "Don't lift with it for a week. You will. Don't." That was one. She drank from the pan, not much, and put the pan down, and said, "Next," and the next was a child with a fever, carried, and she put her hands on the child's back and ran the count and said, "That's the chest. It's not in the chest yet. It will be by tomorrow if I leave it," and did not leave it, and the child's breath changed under her hands and the mother's face changed above them, and Oryn said, "Warm, not hot. Water. Send for me if it's back in three days, and it won't be." That was two. An old woman's knee — Millrace's, with the ladder in the story before Oryn had asked for it — and Oryn knelt for that one, on the planks, with both hands round the joint, and it took the longest of the three, and when she stood up she stood up as a woman stands who has spent something she keeps count of, and said, "It'll hold to the spring. In the spring I'll do it again. Don't go up the ladder." "I go up the ladder," the old woman said. "I know," Oryn said. "Don't."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-18.md:75:He catalogued, because it was what he did, that no companion in four years had come on their own terms of service, because none of them had had any: Lira had walked out of an academy, Brom out of a family, Karis out of a ranking, Seln out of a Compact, and every one of them had arrived with nothing to bargain with but themselves. Oryn had a route, and a paper going north on a carter, and a sentence with *when I say* in it twice, and she had put the five of them on her circuit as a stop between the third hold and the fourth and had not once used the word *join*. He did not write it at the ford. He wrote it an hour up the road, at the fork above Millrace where her route's road went on west along the ridge toward the fourth hold and the Stair road turned south off it — standing, with the six of them stopped at the fork's stone because Seln stopped at forks, and Oryn's mule with its head toward the west road and Oryn's hand on its rope turning it south.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-18.md:127:A floor. The same grey — he would have known it from a mile — round, level, lipped, the last of the sun going off it white along its whole width at once, as the Ring's had. No ring. No uprights, whole or broken, nothing standing on it at all; the floor lay in the bowl bare as a plate. And in its middle, where the Ring's had had its tallest stone, a hole: square-edged, black, and out of the hole, going down into the ground at the angle of a stair, a stair. The same grey. The top three steps caught the light. The rest did not. He counted what he could see and it was three, and then the dark, and the dark went down further than three.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-20.md:29:Square, or near it: ten strides by ten, walked. Fused stone, floor and walls and ceiling, and the ceiling was the floor — the underside of the plate the six of them had run across in the morning and the bowl's rim had watched all afternoon — one piece of grey from the stair's foot to the far wall, a hand above Brom's head, no join in it anywhere, so that the chamber was not a room under a floor but the floor's *other side*, and he stood under it and put his palm flat on the ceiling, a man touching a ceiling, with nothing running in him but the hollow the rim had left — and it was one piece to the hand as it was to the eye, cold, dry, no join under his palm anywhere, and he did not write the word for that either.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-19.md:5:He catalogued the crossing because it was the second one. Seln first, down the road's cut into the bowl. Karis with the cord and the notebook. Lira, one-armed, on the taped foot. Oryn with the roll on her back and her hands at her sides. Brom, with the bow across his chest and the sleeve of bolts on his belt and the middle box by its strap. Himself last, with the ten held to nothing and the Iron read running under his soles, and the place where an Arbiter would have been as dark on this side of the stone as on the other. Six over. The mules left on the road with the two boxes and their ropes on a scrub root, because a mule could not be told about a line.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-19.md:51:He catalogued it by lantern because it was what he did. Square, or near it. Ten strides by ten. Fused stone, floor and walls, level, seamless. The ceiling a hand above Brom's head, and it was the floor — the underside of the plate they had run across, one piece of grey, no join, so that six people stood under the Long Stair's floor as under a table. The walls carved floor to ceiling in the lines, in bands, regular as a rule, the same kind of line and never the same line, and Karis, with a bolt-wound man beside her and six crossbows forty steps up, looked at the walls for the length of one breath before she looked at anything else, and he saw her see them, and he saw her put it away. At the chamber's middle, shapes: something cased, something laid out on the stone in a line, a cord. Nobody went to look. There was no time to look, and it was not what they had come down for.

