   281	She read it by the lantern, with her lips going. She looked once at the blanket, and at the gap beside it, and then she got up without a word, in her shirt, and crossed the three strides that he had not. She did not lift the corner. She drew the blanket a hand's breadth along its nails, until it met the frame, and pressed the edge flat with her thumb.
   282	
