   291	She came to it at dusk on that same day, two days behind her own reckoning and no longer troubling to count them. It was a larger hold than the others. Its wall was of stone, as high as a man's head, with a fence of split oak above; and the bench under its gate-house eave was long enough for twelve.
   292	
   293	There were nine on it, and she went along them at her stride and a half, with two fingers for each wrist. A herdsman's foot that a beast had trodden. A baby with a rash, and a mother who had not slept. An old woman who wished only to be told that the thing in her chest was the same thing as last year, and it was. She asked each one, and had each one's yes, and it was full dark before she had her hands in the butt.
   294	
   295	The old woman had waited. She was the one who kept that hold's days, as some woman did in each of them: when the rams went in, and when the beans; who was owed what, and whose turn it was at the gate.
   296	
   297	"There's a thing at first light," she said. "I'd take it kindly if you were on that bench for it. Not to do anything. Only to be there."
   298	
   299	"What thing?"
   300	
   301	"My daughter's girl is in her year. Fourteen since the hay. We ring them on the threshing floor, at the first light after the moon's full, and it was full last night." She had her arms folded in her apron. "It's never gone amiss here. But her mother's mother went down like a felled calf, at hers, and lay till noon. It's in the family to take it hard."
   302	
   303	"I'll not lay a hand on her unless she's hurt, and unless she says. She'll have enough that's new without a stranger's fingers."
   304	
   305	"I never asked for your hand. I asked for the bench."
   306	
   307	"Then you've the bench," said Oryn.
   308	
   309	She ate what was brought out, and lay down beside the mule.
   310	
   311	She had seen it done forty times on that road, at one gate or another, and had never got used to how little there was to see. Inside the Line it was a matter of offices. She had stood in one herself: a room with a desk, and a ring chalked on the boards, and a clerk who looked at a plate of brass and not at her. The district had named her day a year beforehand. Out here there was no desk, and no plate, and nobody to write. An old woman scratched a ring on a threshing floor with a hazel stick, on a morning that she chose by the moon; and the child stood in it; and it came.
   312	
   313	Whether it needed the ring, Oryn had never learned. Nobody at any of those gates had been willing to try it without.
   314	
   315	---
   316	
   317	The floor was of beaten clay, in the open, with a low kerb of stones about it.
   318	
   319	They were all there by the first grey: the whole hold, thirty or more, standing off beyond the kerb with their breath smoking, and not one of them speaking above a murmur. Oryn sat on the bench by the gate. It was forty yards. She had her hands in her sleeves.
   320	
   321	The old woman drew the ring.
   322	
   323	She walked it backward, bent, with the stick's point in the clay; and when the two ends met she stepped over the line, and held her hand out, and a girl came across the floor to her.
   324	
   325	She was thin, and tall for her age, with her hair in one plait and a sheepskin on her shoulders that was too big. She was frightened, and she was trying to walk as though she were not. Oryn had a square face that showed little, and she kept it so; but she had looked for that walk, and it was there, as it always was. Every one of them came across the clay like a person carrying a full pail.
   326	
   327	The girl stepped into the ring, and the old woman stepped out of it.
   328	
   329	Nothing happened that a watcher could have named.
   330	
   331	There was no light, and no sound, unless it was a ewe somewhere behind the wall. A thin child stood in a scratched circle with her hands shut at her sides, and a hold stood round her and held its breath.
   332	
   333	Then her head turned.
   334	
   335	It was a small movement, a little to the left, as a person turns toward a voice. Her eyes went with it. They went to a place in the air that was just beyond the corner of her sight, and when they came there the place had moved, and they followed it. Oryn watched a girl of fourteen look at something a hand's breadth from her own cheek that not one of thirty people could see.
   336	
   337	She had seen that forty times as well. It was always the eyes. A child found, on such a morning, that there was a thing in the world which belonged to nobody else, and which nobody else would ever be shown; and the first that she did was to try to look straight at it, and fail, and try again.
   338	
   339	The girl's lips had parted. She was listening now. Whatever it was had said its one word, down at the bottom of the breastbone, where nobody could overhear.
   340	
   341	Her face came open like a door.
   342	
   343	The old woman was over the line before the child's knees had begun to go. They did go, a little. She was caught, and held, with a grey head bent to hers. Oryn, on a bench forty yards off, saw the girl's mouth move against her grandmother's ear, three or four words, quick, and saw the old arms tighten.
   344	
   345	That was the whole of it, and the hold let its breath go. Somebody laughed, and somebody was crying, and a small boy broke away from his mother's skirt and ran across the kerb to see what his sister had got.
   346	
   347	She would not be told what the word had been, and she did not wish to be; it was theirs.
   348	
   349	Oryn sat with her hands in her sleeves, and found that she was not thinking of the girl.
   350	
   351	She was thinking of a boy on a table, under her two hands, a long while since, who had told her a thing with his breath going slow. He had stood in a ring too, and it had been chalk, and there had been a clerk. A small light had come up from behind his breastbone to the edge of his eye, as it had come just now to that child; and it had looked at him, for a count that he still knew; and then it had gone out. She had asked him whether it hurt. He had said no; he had said that it was like a wick being pinched.
   352	
   353	This girl had looked up, when it was over, and found a face. Every one of the forty had. It was the second thing that they did.
   354	
   355	She did not know whose face he had found. There had been a clerk in that room, and a plate of brass; she did not suppose that either had been looking at him.
   356	
   357	She had put her hands on that boy more than once. She had found in him what she had found in nobody, and had told him to write it down and leave it be; and then she had walked north from a fork, owing him the one reading that she had not given.
   358	
   359	She was two days behind, and there was a fifth gate, and a ford, and a mill.
   360	
   361	She made her reckoning there, on the bench, as she would have made it over a wrist. The fifth gate should have its day, since it had waited a cold term. The ford could have an hour. What she would not do was come late to that mill; six people would be there on the day that she had named, and one of them had a thing for her that she had told him to bring. If the road cost her a night's walking, then it cost that. And when she had him on the plank, she would not go round him again as she had gone round before. She would go as deep as she went. She would ask him first, and have his yes; and then she would read what was there, and say what she found, whether she had a word for it or not.
   362	
   363	The old woman had come across the clay. She stood by the bench with her arms folded, and her eyes were wet, and she was not troubling to hide it.
   364	
   365	"You never stirred," she said.
   366	
   367	"She never needed me. That's the best morning I've had on this road since the thaw."
   368	
   369	"She kept her feet. Near enough."
   370	
   371	"She did. Tell her that somebody saw."
   372	
   373	Oryn got up, and went to see to the mule.

