27:Cael gave the rest of his ruling then, with his hair still wet from the pump. He had promised it for the morning, when he could see it; and he had lain awake a good part of the night finding how little there was to see. He had six people, and a hill with two gates in it, and a card that was good for one day; and there was a slot in a wall at the top of that hill, twenty-two steps long, which had been shut on them at both ends like a box.
31:"That leaves the carts' street," said Seln. He stood by the pump, with his hands in his sleeves. "I walked it once, under a keg of oil. It is half a mile, round the back of the crown, and there is not a yard of it that is ever empty by day."
35:"I'd as soon not sit on them a second time," said Brom. "There's a handcart in that stable, with shafts for one. I've asked. She says we may have the use of it while the loft's ours, if it comes back swept."
101:The old keeper at the round arch read the seal from its stamp inward, as he read it every day.
115:He found that his neck had not set, as it had on the first day. He did not make much of that. A person can learn to walk past anything, if it is put in his road twice a day; it does not mean that the thing has grown smaller.
133:He had to put them somewhere. The wall was on his right hand, and above the wall was the crown; and he knew, without having once looked, where on that crown a pale thing stood. He had felt it go round him all that way, like a lamp carried round a room behind a person's back. So he looked left, and down, at a town laid out in three steps to a brown river. There was the bridge. It was small and dark and it stood on five piers; and a square woman had gone north from a fork with a mule, ten days ago, and would be at a mill before he was.
153:"Him," said the man. "Down the hole. I've had this beam eleven years, and I never chalked a thing to him yet." He looked at the boxes. "Came by the slot, the other days, I suppose. On backs."
169:The old man's chin had come up. Cael knew that look. He had seen it in a registry's yard, six days ago, a breath before a name and a rank and an office were laid on a ledge.
181:"It was not done at all. I said one sentence, and it was a foolish one." The old man was looking back at the beam, and at a slate on a knee. "He had the whole of it in him, with no article. They generally have. I begin to think that I was sent for, all those years, chiefly by people who wished to be spared the trouble of deciding."
193:Cael watched him count it. There were Brom's two, and Lira's two, and his own; and beside his own there was one boot standing and one that hung a hand's breadth above the ground, at the end of a leg like a bolster. The eyes went on to the bearer, as the form required. They found a small pale hand, the left, held flat against a coat, in a strip of linen that had not been there the day before.
197:There were three things by now that the man had not asked. Cael had them counted, since he counted anything. Six days ago an old porter had laid a name and a rank and an office on that ledge, and nothing had been said of it since. This morning the same matter had come in by the bottom gate, on wheels, with another man's chalk against it, after seven days on backs. And somewhere in the yard there would be a yard-man who had gone up the cut at first light with a broom, and found a tread lying out of its bed, and two plain bolts, and had brought the bolts to somebody.
237:The eleventh had nothing to do in that gallery, and it did it all day.
239:Cael knelt at the Stair's table from the third hour, a hand to the left, with his forearm along his thigh and the flame standing up. He had been a lamp for six days; he could have done it asleep. That was the trouble. It left the rest of him free, and there was a new thing in the rest of him that had not yet learned the house.
257:The man had seen that, an hour since; he did not look at faces, but he looked at hands all day, since hands were what laid his counters.
277:"I am not stuck. I have a group of nine signs, and I do not know whether it is one word, or two, or the tail of one and the head of the next. If I knew where his words ended, I could lay them against the old books by their length. I have been guessing at the joins for two days, and I do not care for it."
305:"Stand here. Where I stand. No; here." He had taken a pace to his right, and he pointed at the rock where his own boots had been. "I've given you bones for a week, from outside, with pebbles. That's all I've ever given anybody. There's another way that I see it, and I've not found the words for it in thirty years, since nobody wanted them." He rubbed his eyes with the heel of his hand. "You want his joins. I can't hand them to you. I can only show you where I'm standing when I see them."
