5:Cael had the best place in the gallery for both, as he had for everything, and the worst way up. From his knee he saw a page of the cramped brown hand, line under line, with a figure at the end of each: some clerk of the old town had set down what came in at a gate on one day, and what was paid on it. A hand's breadth off lay Karis's hard clean pencil, in rows, with the white pebbles and the black still lying where she had laid them that forenoon.
11:He looked from its foot. Cael had moved the flame before the grey head moved, and it was there. The eyes went up the brown page slowly, without a blink, and the water came into them, and they went up the sheet beside it. Then, which they had never done in eight days, they went back and forth between the two.
31:Cael had the light there before it, and did not have to think of where. He had been looking where that man looked for six days together, a hand to the left of him, and had taken it for a groom's knowledge of a horse. The finger came to the fifth row, and went along the signs, the white and the three black and the red, without touching one of them, and slowed as it went.
51:It was the twelfth. He knew whose it was, and he had been a hand to its left for six days.
59:She had asked for his joins, and he was giving them to her; he could not have done it with a pebble five days ago. He had needed to say aloud, to somebody who stood in his boots, what he had stood in them and seen for thirty years; and it seemed that the saying had loosened the rest.
73:He understood the lamp now. He had put it low and to the left for six days because he was told to, and had moved it ahead of the word because the eyes always went one way. They went one way because the thing itself did. A made thing was looked at from where it stood upon the ground, toward where it was going, and a light set anywhere else lay across the grain of it.
87:There were two reasons, and he made himself set them in order, since a wish is quick and a reason has to be fetched. The man who owned this had told a gallery what it was not for, five days ago, in the voice of a carter giving his rate. It did not work on a man. And a healer had put an order on that place, on a plank, a long way east: *Write it down and stop touching it.* He had read her order once already for the leave that he wanted. He had gone round the place with what he had, at a waypost, and had called it obedience, since he had not gone in. He had been ashamed enough of that to write it, and to owe her the telling.
89:He would not read her twice. He would be at her mill inside of eight days, and she could be asked.
101:A man had shown a stranger the inside of his trade, for the first time in thirty years, because she had asked him honestly for something that he could not hand her. And the boy who held his lamp had come away with a piece of it. He had not reached. He never did reach. But he would kneel at that elbow tomorrow, and the day after, with the man's own way of looking folded behind his eyes; and each time that the finger went along a row, he would be able to see where it was going before it went.
111:"Two days."
151:"That's two in two days," he said. "You'll want a bigger book."
163:"That one came once, and it's shut, and I've not touched it. To tell him that much, I'd have to tell him all of it, and I can't take it back out of him afterward. This one's different, and it's not because I think better of the man. I'm at his elbow every day. He says a word and I'm there before it, and from tomorrow I'd be there because I can see what he sees. He made it a term that nobody tells him what a thing is before he's looked. He's looked for a week at a lamp, and it's not a lamp."
171:"Seln told a clerk the truth in a yard," said Brom. "It cost him his face. He said that it was the only way he'd found that stayed right afterward." He looked at his hands. "The fellow's not a counter. He's kept all that for thirty years with never a ticket on a shelf. Tell him what's his. I'd not tell him ours."
215:His voice had risen. Cael had not heard it rise in eight days, for a dray or a cut hand or a lamp that moved; and it had gone up now like a clerk's who has turned a leaf and found a figure where no figure can be.
237:For eight days those red eyes had gone over him as water goes over a stone in a ford. He had been a hook, and a hook is not looked at; he had asked to be exactly that, and had minded it, and had written that he minded. They had not gone over him tonight. When the cup was set down they had come to the edge of him. They had come as far as a sleeve, and the wrist of the hand that held the light; and there they had stopped, and the man had turned his whole head away to the oak, as a person turns from a lit window that is not his to look in at.
279:*Rune-adjacent. Cleon. Partial. Rune family. A made thing's lean — the direction a maker put in it; which mark bears, which is borne, which is nothing; whether a run sits or goes. Structure, not meaning: it will tell me a thing is an instruction and will not tell me what it instructs. Made things only. Not a river. Not a man. Costs the eyes, after. No use in a fight. Engagement field: inscriptive — the second field that is not a fight, after clinical; given, not taken. Tier equivalent unknown. Deployment: none. Twelfth.*
285:There was a line which he did not need to puzzle at. He had heard it stated like a carter's rate, and he had watched it paid for eight days, in water running down beside a man's nose.
307:Cael had set his boot on the nineteenth tread, with the case, and the lamp was above him, eight steps up, in Lira's hand. It was a small brown flame in a smoked glass. He had knelt beside it for six days. It went into his eyes now like a handful of sand.
319:"I do not ask," said the old man. There was a silence, and a scrape, and the case drew upward by the height of one step. "There are eight more. Each is of the same rise; I have had occasion to learn them today. Put your right foot up. Higher. There."
323:It was given a step at a time, in the level voice in which a clerk reads a column: the right foot, and the left, and that the tread was dry. For eight days Cael had gone two steps below that stick, so that there should be something under it if it fell. He had not thought to find the thing turned about. He bore up his end of the case, and put his boots where he was told, and was brought up out of the dark by a person who could not stand.
