13:Cael ate, since Brom was watching him do it; and then he took out Hesk's book, and held it a long way from his face, and wrote the day.
15:*Two hundred and ninety-first day. Treswick; the loft; one in the stall. A hundred and thirty-four. By the carts' street, both ways, on wheels: a weight chalked at the bottom gate to the name on the card, and to nobody else. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
21:There was a thing that he had not written, and it was the largest thing of the day. A man had said it to a plank of oak, with his back turned, after a cup of water; and he had said what was to be done with it. Cael knew what a line in that book was. Lira read the book; and a thing set down there had been said to her, as surely as if he had leaned across two pallets and put it in her ear.
23:He did not write it. He had kept one other thing out of those pages on purpose, at a spring, eleven days ago; he marked that this made two, and that he could not write that either.
59:"Then it is all." Seln put his hands into his sleeves. "I should have said yes, on that stair. I should have been a good deal longer about it than a big man with a box. He has kept a hundred and ten strides of paper for thirty years, and has not put a ticket on a bundle; I have considered that fact for five days on my own account." He was silent a moment. "It is very nearly the only thing in this town that I trust."
85:He had not known that he would say it until it was out. He had been looked at for nine days, from a doorway and a kerb and the head of a stair, by people who had been told what to look for; and he found that he wished, once, to do the looking.
165:Cael knew where it led before he saw. He had stood in that place with a big man, nine days ago, by daylight, and watched him look into the cut end of a rope as a person looks into a flower. It had been full of people walking backward then.
227:*No*, he said. He had said it to that one twice a day since Ostrand. He said it now as a person leans on a door with somebody leaning on the other side.
273:Cael knew that road. He had walked the whole round of it on his second day in the town, by himself, and had not once lifted his eyes above the top of that wall. He did not lift them now; he looked at a place twenty strides ahead of a long coat, on the cobbles, and went home.
281:"They know that he may be out. It is a different thing, and it is the thing that matters." Seln's voice was as mild as ever. "You and I have gone round this town tonight like mice in a wall. He has gone round it like a man taking the air."
313:Seln looked at him, and did not say that it was foolish. He said, "A person may walk up a street. Do not speak first, and do not stand as though you were waiting to be let in."
337:By day he might go through as the fourth part of a figure on another person's card, for a named door and a stated purpose, and be counted out again by dusk; by right he might not go through at all. Iron walked under that arch, and showed a paper, and was written. There was a colour for Iron, and a paler one for the wall above. A plate had put a word after his name that had no colour. There was no card in the district with an edge for what he was; and a man with his spurs cut off had just put a hand into his coat and brought out the one thing that Cael would never own, which was leave.
357:"So that we should know how much time they have. That is the whole of it. They need not take you in a stair. They may stand under a lamp for a month."
377:*He may go through the blue gate at night. I may not go through it at all, except as a figure. It is a hand wide.*
379:*S.: they have time. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
