5:It was the two hundred and ninety-second morning past the Line, and Cael saw it from the kerb of the carts' street, where the hill dropped away and all the roofs of the district lay below. It stood out on the brown water as it had stood the day before, small and dark, on five piers. He had looked at it then as a person looks at a road that he came in by. Now it was the oldest thing that he could see from that hill which anybody had built. He had that on the word of a small pale woman, who had laid her hand where the masons' pale stone was cut to fit it. And the new thing behind his eyes came round to it, across half a mile of air, like a head lifted from a book.
13:He had gone through this once already, with another matter. For eleven days he had not looked at a light on a crown, and he had not done it by shutting his eyes. He had done it by having somewhere else to put them, and a reason, twice a day, until his neck no longer set at an alley. He did the same now. He looked at a raw hand on a tarred case, which nobody had made, and at the back of a girl's neck, and pushed.
35:He said it to the ring. His red eyes had come to the brass, and to the smear upon it, and they stayed there as though a line had been drawn at the edge of the metal; they did not go on by the width of a finger to the hand that held it. For seven days they had gone over that hand as over a hook. This was not the same. Cael watched a man take care, and he knew what the care was for.
41:It was a long day, and Karis made it longer by being right four times.
51:"It is *and*," she said. "It is only *and*. It is the commonest word that the old clerk has, and it is the commonest on these floors; there are nine of them on this one sheet." She laughed, which she seldom did, and put the back of her bound hand to her mouth. "I have waited a year to read a word of this, and the first word is one that a child has before it can walk."
57:There was a group of six signs on the Stair's long table which stood four times in those twenty-nine, and always after a white. She had not let herself look at it for four days; she had laid a glove across the place whenever her work took her near. She lifted the glove now, by one finger. She had every letter of the six, and she did not read them at once. She looked first at the man with the reed.
67:The man had written his card. He stood by the desk, and his hand went up to the shelf, to the thick bundle tied both ways with tape gone brown; and two fingers rested there, as they had rested on the day when he named what it was made of.
75:Cael knew the hat before he knew the man. He had seen it across a river with ice in it, on the far bank, on the day that the last ferry ran; and there had been a bag under that arm then, as there was now. It was the carrier who took whatever went between the towns of the Line and one plank under an eave, some days east.
139:"Four," said the woman, when he had told her what he wanted. She looked at him as she had looked on the first day, when he asked for a seventh bowl. "In front."
153:He set a saucer over each of the two cups, to keep the dust out, and stood them at the table's end. One was a small pale woman's, who was above their heads with a lantern and twenty-one letters. The other had been carried under the lashing of his box, as an empty bowl, for eleven days; and the person whom it was kept for was a long way north along a hill-foot, with a mule.
157:He made his second mistake of the day over the pie.
197:"One of those is for a lady who is in a hayloft with a pencil," he said. "The other is for a woman who went north eleven days ago, and does not know that there is a table."
215:He had kept his day's lines in that town, as he kept them anywhere. This was the other thing, the long one; and it was the first of its kind that he had ever set down inside a wall.
219:*Treswick; the loft over the stable; one in the stall. The two hundred and ninety-second day. Eight days inside. A hundred and thirty: four to a dinner, on Brom's ruling, and worth it. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
225:*Wind — Lira. Pressure — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron — Brom. Compression — Reydan. Ember — Karis. Shadow — Seln; source protected; sealed; twice in all; it asked for three hours last night and was told no. Storm — Daeva. Anchor — unnamed. Blade — Ephram. Tide — Oryn; ask first, every time; used on nobody this side of the snow, and five times on myself, the last two round the place and not in. Iron Wall — Vastin; held; entered, not told; deployment none. Rune — the reader; a made thing's lean; given; told; deployment none; it looks before I've said that it may, and I am learning to give it nothing to look at.*
229:*Held, with no ruling: a cord, two edges, a hair. A row that counted a sheet short. I have not laid a hand to an instrument since the hundred and sixty-eighth day.*
235:He looked at the brass for a moment. He had a ruling upon himself, a hundred and twenty-four days old, that he was not to try a measure with his own hand; and here was a thing whose whole purpose was to be opened upon a distance and carried. He did not open it. He had a hair in a small pale woman's notebook to remind him why.
265:He did not look again at the line, either; he turned upon the boards, so that the third box was between his shoulder and it, and drew the lantern nearer. He wrote the fourth entry on that page in the form that he had used once before, on the hundred and sixty-eighth day, for a thing that he could neither prove nor put down.
285:"Twenty-seven days out of the wool," she said, of the arm that had done it. "It's good for something."
289:The fourth of the five gates had a child in its fourteenth year, and Oryn was asked to stand by.
291:She came to it at dusk on that same day, two days behind her own reckoning and no longer troubling to count them. It was a larger hold than the others. Its wall was of stone, as high as a man's head, with a fence of split oak above; and the bench under its gate-house eave was long enough for twelve.
293:There were nine on it, and she went along them at her stride and a half, with two fingers for each wrist. A herdsman's foot that a beast had trodden. A baby with a rash, and a mother who had not slept. An old woman who wished only to be told that the thing in her chest was the same thing as last year, and it was. She asked each one, and had each one's yes, and it was full dark before she had her hands in the butt.
295:The old woman had waited. She was the one who kept that hold's days, as some woman did in each of them: when the rams went in, and when the beans; who was owed what, and whose turn it was at the gate.
301:"My daughter's girl is in her year. Fourteen since the hay. We ring them on the threshing floor, at the first light after the moon's full, and it was full last night." She had her arms folded in her apron. "It's never gone amiss here. But her mother's mother went down like a felled calf, at hers, and lay till noon. It's in the family to take it hard."
311:She had seen it done forty times on that road, at one gate or another, and had never got used to how little there was to see. Inside the Line it was a matter of offices. She had stood in one herself: a room with a desk, and a ring chalked on the boards, and a clerk who looked at a plate of brass and not at her. The district had named her day a year beforehand. Out here there was no desk, and no plate, and nobody to write. An old woman scratched a ring on a threshing floor with a hazel stick, on a morning that she chose by the moon; and the child stood in it; and it came.
359:She was two days behind, and there was a fifth gate, and a ford, and a mill.
361:She made her reckoning there, on the bench, as she would have made it over a wrist. The fifth gate should have its day, since it had waited a cold term. The ford could have an hour. What she would not do was come late to that mill; six people would be there on the day that she had named, and one of them had a thing for her that she had told him to bring. If the road cost her a night's walking, then it cost that. And when she had him on the plank, she would not go round him again as she had gone round before. She would go as deep as she went. She would ask him first, and have his yes; and then she would read what was there, and say what she found, whether she had a word for it or not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md
5:The man in the dusty coat had ruled it from his chair, on the two hundred and ninety-third morning, before any of them had a load off. It was the seventh day of his week; there were twelve of the old town's tally-books lying open along a table's edge, and he would not have a tape touched while one of them was out of its bay.
15:Cael had turned to go with them. He had not been four strides from that flame since the third hour of the second day, and his hand felt wrong without it.
21:"I should like one of you here. I do not wish to read it to a chair, and a man who shuts his eyes." She drew a breath. "You have knelt at this for eight days, and been given a sore knee for it. I think that you are owed the end of the week."
47:They were the floors' letters, and he could not read one of them; but he had held a lamp above that woman's column for eight days, and he knew a hooked upright when he saw it, and a gate, and a fork.
93:"I've had that on a shelf ten years, a hand's breadth from my head, and I never knew a word that was in it. I'll hear it before I'm older."
101:[[TIER-B: The leaf (BOOK_MAP §10 Tier A 1), read aloud once by Karis: "In the years of the unmaking, when the un-bound walked in the land and no wall stood against them and no law held, and the cities of the east were emptied and the cities of the west were emptied and the rivers ran wrong" … "there came one who saw the shape of it, and set himself to the ordering. And he made the measure, and the seat, and the tier, and the mark that is given at the kindling, that each should know what he is and none should be what he is not. And he set the apparatus over all the land, and the land was ordered, and the un-bound came no more." / "And the" [the scraped name: four or five letters' width, the first upright stroke showing] "— set the apparatus over all the land, that the un-bound should not come again, who had near unmade the world before the ordering of it. And this is the reason of the apparatus, and this is all of its reason, and let none who come after forget it, though the un-bound be forgotten: that they should not come again." — the plan gives the unit with an elision in its first sentence and a bracketed place for the scraped name; staged as Karis's one reading, standing at Cleon's desk under the fixed lamp, her finger an inch above the skin, with Cleon two paces back with his eyes shut, Cael at the desk's left side and Vastin on a box six strides off; the object (horn-coloured, point-written, the scraped place with its one upright stroke) is described before this line and is not described again; coordinator to supply protected text]]
117:He marked that, and it was done three ways. The red eyes were shut, and had been since before she began. The grey ones had gone to a bay of bundles and were reading the under side of a tag. And the small pale woman, who had asked him to stand there, kept her face to a wall. He had been looked away from by one person in that gallery, two days ago, on purpose and for his own sake. It appeared to have spread.
125:"You made it a term that I should tell you nothing of what I thought until you had looked. I have kept this from you for nine days by that term, and from myself. It is a line that a man copied out of a register, a long way from here, and carried three hundred miles." She did not look toward the box. "It sends its reader to the lower archive at this town for a thing that is held here in full. And it has a word in it, which I had never met."
129:"That word stands here. It is spelled as he has spelled it, letter for letter, in the old signs. *The Architect.*"
131:It was the first time that Cael had heard it spoken. He had seen it written, in fresh ink, on a table at Lowmarch; and his hand had lain a hand's width from it for the whole of a lamp.
135:"It gives its own year, at the head," she said. "It is in the count that the old town's books keep, and I have had twelve of those open for a week. By that count this was written about four hundred years ago. I should want a day with the tallies to say it nearer, and I am not going to be given the day."
139:"The rest is what you heard. It says what the apparatus is for, and it says that there is no other reason. It was set over the land so that a kind of people should not come again." Her voice had dropped. "It calls them the un-bound. I said that word at this table on the first day, as a limit; I had only ever met it as the name of a layer in the ground. I did not know that it had been a people."
157:He knew what he was doing. An old man in Denvash had taught him to write the count first, and he had made a habit of it, and the habit had served him in a stair three nights ago and on a scree before that. He was counting a gallery so that he need not count the one thing in it which the leaf concerned. He let himself. He thought that he would be given the rest of the day to stand in this room; he would not be given it to stand in the other.
167:"I can read this at sight," she said. "It is the old town's hand. It is the hand of the tally-books, or its father's." Her pencil went down the leaf without touching. "It is a chronicle. It has the floods in it, and a murrain among the cattle, and who was reeve. Somebody of this town kept a year-book, and somebody else laid it away with that."
183:"No," she said. "That is another day's work, and it is torn. I am not going to read into a hole."
197:It came down upon the rock, once, six strides away. The old man had sat through a reading and an account with his hands shut upon it, and his face turned to a bay of bundles; and whatever he had been holding off had got past him. He spoke to the shelves, low, and with care, as he had once begun to draft an order at a spring.
199:[[TIER-B: Vastin: "… the mark that is given at the kindling —" — the packet gives only the stopped end of his sentence ("Vastin reaches ('the mark that is given at the kindling —')") and BOOK_MAP §9 says that his sentence stops in the chamber; staged as said from Brom's box by the nearest bay, six strides from the desk, to the shelves and not to any person, and broken off by Karis's next line; coordinator to supply protected text]]
203:She had turned round; it was the first time since the tape was untied that she had looked at anybody, and she looked at him as she had looked across a table at Lowmarch, when a courier's flat lay between them.
205:"There is what that leaf says. It says it in its own words, of its own time, and I shall copy it on one page, and nothing else shall go upon that page." She took a clean leaf from her notebook, and laid it on the desk, bare. "And there is this. What you were about to say is not on the skin. It may be so. I do not know, and nor do you. If it is ever written, it will be written here, when somebody can show it; and until then this page has nothing on it, and nobody is to rule a line from the one to the other because a word stands in both."
215:She had said it at a spring, over six years of an office's sheets, to stop a sentence of her own. She said it now with her bound hand on a bare leaf.
217:"I was drafting," said Vastin. His voice had very little breath behind it. "I was told at that same spring, by another person, what it is when I do so. I was drawing a conclusion, in a room where I have been at some pains for nine days to draw nothing." He looked down at the ash. "I leave it where it stopped."
237:*and the* — there the box stood, with nothing in it — *set the apparatus over all the land, that the un-bound should not come again, who had near unmade the world before the ordering of it.*
275:"That's twice in three days that you've had leave and I've had none." She said it quite low. Her chin was up, and he knew that she was angry, and that she would not be angry for long. "Is it bad?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md
5:He did it on a box, by a bay of bundles, in the afternoon of the two hundred and ninety-third day. Nobody wanted him. The lamp had come back to the long table, and the boy knelt beside it, a hand to the left, as he had knelt on every other afternoon. The small pale woman was making a second copy. The man in the dusty coat sat in his chair with his eyes shut. It was a room with one office in it, and Vastin had not once that day felt his boot begin to move.
21:It was not a fraud. A fraud wants something. It leans the scale so that a house may keep its place, or a seat its revenue, and when the leaning is found there is at least a person behind it who has got richer.
41:It was a draft. A small grey man had told him at a spring what he was when he drafted, and a small pale woman had laid a bare leaf on a desk and asked him to put nothing on it. He did not finish it. He did not let himself hear how it would have ended, even in the place behind his eyes where a man hears his own clauses before he speaks them.
49:Three nights ago, in a stair, six people had come to see what that boy was; and he had stood upon a step with his stick, on the eleventh day of thirty, and put a wall in front of him. He had not known then what stood behind it. He had said so, on a pallet, when he could not hold a bowl. He knew a little more this afternoon. He found that he would do it again, knowing; and that in a day which had taken the floor out from under forty years, it was the single act of his that he could look at without wishing to revise.
55:"I shall want the start," he said to the boy, who had come to see what he needed. "And I should prefer to take it by myself. I have had your hand under my heel, or at the other end of a strap, on every flight for three days. I am obliged for each of them, and I should like to learn whether I can manage one stair without."
61:The eyes that looked back at him were tired. They were nineteen years old, and they had heard what he had heard, and they had gone back to holding a lamp.
67:It was dark from the second tread. He had no need of light; he had learned those steps in three days as a blind man learns a room. He set his two hands on the stone behind him, and thrust with the sound leg, and came up by one. The bound leg went before him, stiff, like a thing that he had been asked to mind for somebody else.
83:He had met the boy across a desk, with a plate between them, on a bluff. He had read him for three days, with every instrument that the seat owned, and had written that the instruments could not reach him. He had declined to read him again. He had told the boy why, on a bank, at dusk: the sound reason, and the poor one beside it.
95:He began, from forty years' habit, to set it in order. There would be three heads. The first would deal with what he had believed; the second with what he had been shown above Ostrand; the third —
109:He set his hands behind him, and thrust, and came up by one. It hurt a good deal. He found that he did not much mind; it was only a knee, and he had been told to the day how long it would take. A healer had been exact with him. He had come to value that above most things.
121:"I said a week. I'll be a day over it. There's the back of that skin to do, and I'll not have it done against a clerk's bell." He pushed the card along the slope. "He'll write *the same*. He'll have to find something else to write the day after."
139:Cael went up the stair a flight behind a sound that scraped, as he had said that he would. He did not go nearer. On the second landing he found that the sound had got above him, higher than it had ever been at a bell; and something in his chest that had been shut all day came a little way open at that, and he let it.
191:"Then I shall read it. I am glad to be spared a telling. I have never yet heard one that was the same size as the thing." The hand went back into its sleeve. "The boots were not on the kerb today, nor in any doorway that I could see. I do not take comfort from it. I only tell you."
233:It was the first fire that they had had in thirteen days. He marked that as the wood caught. They had lain in a loft for eleven nights on another person's hearth, under a term that said no fire of anybody's, in a town where a struck point was a line in a book. He had forgotten how a yard changes when there is a flame in the middle of it: how the dark draws back to the walls, and people's faces come up out of it, one at a time, each lit from below.
267:"I've not been below the second wall after dark since my hair was another colour. I had to ask a keeper which street." The man had put up one square hand between his face and the basket, against the light. "I sat an hour under that skin, when you'd gone. I've sat under it ten years. I found tonight that I didn't care to do it by myself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md
33:Cael waited; he had known that girl since a lane in Ardenmere. He had seen her read a board, and a bracket, and a ladder built out of stone; and he had learned that the thing which she said first, after reading, was generally the whole of the matter with the wrapping off. He found that he was afraid of it; he had spent a day not picking that thing up, and she would do it in one breath.
43:He had been ready for a harder thing and a softer one. He had not been ready for a true one, said plainly, by the one person who had known him when he had nothing in him but a word in square brackets. Every clerk who had ever looked at him over a desk had looked at him as at a thing that might go off. He had taken it so, and had built himself, a year at a time, into somebody who would not: who stood in corners, and kept twelve doors shut, and told a Path no at a tar barrel with a man five yards away.
89:"Nine days," he said, very low. "And a hand sewn. She's earned a bucket."
93:He had read it as a man reads who has copied other people's papers for twelve years: once, from the head to the foot, without a change in his face, and Cael did not think that he would ever need to see it a second time. He folded it in three, on its own creases, and did not give it back at once.
123:"I'll say one thing here, since I'll not say it below with a clerk's bell going. That skin stays where it's lain. There's five storeys of registry on my roof that have never once asked me what's on my shelves, and they'll not learn it from me. It was under my hand ten years when I didn't know what it was. It can lie there now that I do."
143:"You signed the mark at kindlings."
173:Cael had known that it would come round to him. It had come round at a plank in Lowmarch, and at a spring, and in a loft with a blanket on its window; they had looked at him then for a ruling. They did not look at him now: a girl had sat down again on her bucket and was watching the fire, and a big man was scraping a pot. They were giving him the yard, as they had given it to her.
181:It was not a ruling; there was nothing in it to hold, or to try again in daylight. It had been read, once, in a voice that shook; and it was either so or it was not, and every person at that fire had already taken it for so. If he wrote it he would be putting it where he put a cord and a hair. He would be making it a thing that he had noticed; and it was not that. It was the ground that he had been standing on since he was fourteen, shown to him from underneath.
187:[[TIER-B: Pole two, Cael's sentence (BOOK_MAP §10 Tier A 17): "Since my Kindling I thought the system forgot to make room for me," … "It didn't forget. It was *built* so there'd be no room. Four hundred years of a whole world, so that I wouldn't happen." … "I happened anyway." — the plan gives this speech in three pieces with elisions; staged as Cael's, said aloud on his feet at the fire-basket in the inn's yard, to all of them and to nobody in particular, with Hesk's book left in his coat; he does not write it down afterward; coordinator to supply protected text]]
231:He had not looked at Cael, and he did not look now. He had come down through two gates and asked a keeper his road, and sat an hour on a bucket with the fire hurting his eyes; and he had kept one thing the whole of that hour, which was where he did not put them. Cael had seen people look away from him all his life. They had done it at counters, and in the corridors of a house on a bluff, as a person turns from something that may be catching. He had not known that it could be done as a courtesy, and held to, like a term.
267:He sat down against the third box, and took out Hesk's book, and the dividers slid from it into his lap. He set the day at the head of a clean leaf.
269:*Two hundred and ninety-third day. Treswick; the yard, and then the loft; one in the stall. A hundred and thirty. A leaf read once, below, by Karis. A copy made. Read at a fire by the three who had not heard it. The reader came up, and ate half a bowl. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*

