   233	*Hesk sent dividers. Lines are for finding out how far a thing is from another thing. Everything is. I'll enter that as a rule and see what it's worth.*
   234	
   235	He looked at the brass for a moment. He had a ruling upon himself, a hundred and twenty-four days old, that he was not to try a measure with his own hand; and here was a thing whose whole purpose was to be opened upon a distance and carried. He did not open it. He had a hair in a small pale woman's notebook to remind him why.
   236	
   237	---
   238	
   239	The line moved while the brass still lay upon the leaf.
   240	
   241	There was a blanket on the gable window, hung on two nails by a girl on their first night, and it had hung there since. It did not quite meet the frame on the left side. Each night a thread of the crown's light came in by that gap, and lay across the boards a yard from his knee, pale and thin and as straight as a chalk line. He had never looked at it as a light. It was a mark on a floor, as the bars in the lane were marks on the cobbles, and he stepped over it going to his pallet.
   242	
   243	It lay tonight where it always lay. He had the tail of his eye on it, and no more.
   244	
   245	For a count of one, it was not steady.
   246	
   247	It did not go out; it thickened, and thinned, as a lamp's flame does when a hand is put near the wick; and it was as it had been. He had counted it before he knew that he was counting. *One.*
   248	
   249	He sat quite still, with the pencil off the leaf.
   250	
   251	There was a weight on him. He knew it, and he would have given a good deal not to. He had stood in a chalk ring once, in a room with no window, and had felt a thing at the edge of his sight attend to him for eleven seconds while a plate stayed dark. This was fainter than that, and it came from farther off. But it was of the same kind. It was the particular weight of an instrument turning toward him.
   252	
   253	He made himself go on from there, since that was the place where a person stops thinking and begins to be afraid.
   254	
   255	A needle swings to iron. Nobody says that the needle has seen the iron. A cord had read a stride short in his hand, and nobody had said that the cord knew him; a strip of brass had gone a hair past its mark, and it was brass. He had been a thing that instruments went wrong on since a morning in a frozen yard, and for a good while before it. A draught will move a blanket. A lamp-trimmer will put his hand to a lamp. And his own eyes had been paying for another matter since the afternoon.
   256	
   257	It was three strides to that window.
   258	
   259	He looked at the three strides. The old man slept in a stall now. The pallet by the gable had been rolled, and there was nothing between his knee and the blanket but boards. He could cross them. He could lift one corner of a horse-blanket, and see for himself whether the thing on the crown burned as it had always burned.
   260	
   261	He had written a sentence at a fork-stone, in this book, at a square woman's asking, and had read it back to her. It did not say *the steps* and *the square* because those were the only two ways of going near. It said them because she had seen what he did with a question.
   262	
   263	He did not cross.
   264	
   265	He did not look again at the line, either; he turned upon the boards, so that the third box was between his shoulder and it, and drew the lantern nearer. He wrote the fourth entry on that page in the form that he had used once before, on the hundred and sixty-eighth day, for a thing that he could neither prove nor put down.
   266	
   267	*Claim: the light on the crown was not steady, for a count of one. Evidence: a pale line on the boards of a loft, through the edge of a blanket; one witness, with tired eyes. No cause found. Ruling: hold.*
   268	
   269	Under it he put the last, since it was owed in that book and to nobody else.
   270	

