   158	
   159	"That's it."
   160	
   161	"You looked."
   162	
   163	"I looked at how it lay."
   164	
   165	The iron swung inward, and a lantern was waiting in the vault.
   195	"I did. I had rather be wrong."
   196	
   197	They came down the street with the brake on, which was Brom, walking backward into the hill. The roofs of the district went by on the left hand, grey, with their smoke going up straight. Cael looked at them. He had looked at them on every journey by that road, to keep his eyes off a wall; and he found, this evening, that he had no eyes to spare for a wall in any case.
   198	
   199	He was carrying a thing, and it took the whole of him.
   200	
   201	It was not the paper. The paper weighed nothing. It was what he had not let himself pick up in a gallery, at a desk, with a woman's back turned to him and a man's eyes shut. He had counted a room there. He had held a lamp afterward, for five hours, a hand to the left, and counted pebbles. He had the thing now as a person has a bowl that is full to the brim: level, in both hands, with all his care gone into not spilling it before there was a place to set it down.
   202	
   203	He knew that he would have to. He did not think that a cart's tail was the place.
   204	
   205	In the lane the four alleys had lost their white. He pushed through four bars of a paler light, with a shoulder against his own at each; and he did not look at them, and he did not think about them. He looked at a pair of raw hands on a tarred case, a yard from his face.
   206	
   207	They were shut very tight. The old man had not said one word since the bell.
   208	
   209	"He's been like that since noon," said Lira, low. "Brom tried him with a joke, in the yard. He said, *I beg your pardon?* As if Brom had asked him the hour."
   210	
   211	"He was there."
   212	
   213	"I know that he was. I can count who was." She pushed for a few strides. "It's the three of you. You, and her, and him. You've all got the same face on, and it's not a face that I've seen on any of you. I'd give my good arm to know what's on that paper, and I've only the one."
   214	
   215	"You'll have it in an hour."
   216	
   217	"I'd better."
   218	
   219	The old keeper at the round arch counted one, and four, and said that their lame one looked no better for his ride. Nobody answered him. He looked a second time at the cart, and at the faces round it, and made his stroke without another word.
   220	
   221	---
   222	
   223	The stable kept an iron basket on three legs, for the carters, and Brom had it out by the stall door before the cart was swept.
   224	
   225	He laid the fire himself, with ends of split log from the kitchen's heap, and struck it with his own flint. A small pale woman stood by and watched him do it, with her bound hand in her coat, and did not offer. She had not struck a point since a fork on a moor. There was a wall over that yard with a book at its gate.
   226	
   227	"It'll want a quarter of an hour to be worth sitting at," he said. "I'm going for the pot."
   228	
   229	They brought the old man out of his stall between them, and set him on the mounting block, with the leg along it and a blanket folded under the heel. There were buckets in the stable, of oak, with rope handles. Lira turned five of them upside down in a round about the basket. It was nearly dark under the arch, and the lamp over the painted door had been lit; and Seln sat down on the bucket that faced it.
   230	
   231	Cael did not sit. He stood with his back to the stable wall and his hand inside his coat.
   232	
   233	It was the first fire that they had had in thirteen days. He marked that as the wood caught. They had lain in a loft for eleven nights on another person's hearth, under a term that said no fire of anybody's, in a town where a struck point was a line in a book. He had forgotten how a yard changes when there is a flame in the middle of it: how the dark draws back to the walls, and people's faces come up out of it, one at a time, each lit from below.
   234	
   235	Brom came across the cobbles with the pot on a board.
   236	
   237	There were eight bowls on it.
   238	
   239	"She gave me a look," he said. "I told her that there's seven of us, with the one that's owed. She asked what the eighth was for." He set the board down on an upturned bucket. "I said that I'd laid one for a mule before now."
   240	
   241	"He said that he does not come up," said Karis.
   242	
   243	"I heard what he said. I've heard a deal of people say what they don't do." He was ladling. "It costs me nothing to lay it. If nobody comes, there's the more for him on the block; he's eaten nothing since the bread."
   244	
   245	"I have had no occasion for it," said Vastin.
   246	
   247	It was the first thing that he had said since noon, and they all turned to it. He was looking into the basket.
   248	
   249	"I should be obliged if nobody took that as a complaint against the cook. I find that I am not hungry. I expect to be so again." He put out one raw hand, and Brom set a bowl into it. "I shall hold this, in the meantime, since I am now able to."
   250	
   251	The fire had begun to give heat. Karis came and sat on the bucket nearest it, very upright, with her notebook on her knee; and her eyes were shutting, and opening, and shutting.
   252	
   253	Seln lifted his head.
   254	
   255	He had heard something under the arch. Cael had not; he heard it a breath later, when he had been shown where to listen. It was a step on the stones of the carriage-way. It was quick, and flat, and perfectly even, and it did not put out a hand to any wall.
   256	
   257	It stopped.
   258	
   259	A man stood in the mouth of the arch, a full stride inside it, where the lamp of the painted door did not reach and the fire scarcely did. He was not tall. His coat was the colour of dust. He stood for the space of three breaths with his chin down, as he had once stood on a paving outside a cook-house, a step later than a person stops who is used to streets.
   260	
   261	He had not come by the door with the book. He had come by the yard.
   262	
   263	"I said that I don't come up," said the voice like a hinge.
   264	
   265	Nobody answered. Brom had straightened, with the ladle in his fist.
   266	
   267	"I've not been below the second wall after dark since my hair was another colour. I had to ask a keeper which street." The man had put up one square hand between his face and the basket, against the light. "I sat an hour under that skin, when you'd gone. I've sat under it ten years. I found tonight that I didn't care to do it by myself."
   268	
   269	He came forward into the yard.
   270	
   271	He did not look at the fire, which hurt him. He did not look at the boy by the stable wall; he took some trouble about that, and Cael watched him take it. He looked at the cobbles, and at five buckets set in a round, and at the sixth, which a big man had just turned over with his boot.
   272	
   273	"Sit," said Brom. "There's a bowl."
   274	
   275	"I didn't come to be fed."
   276	
   277	"Nobody does. Sit down."
   278	
   279	The man sat. He took the bowl in both hands, and held it without eating, with his eyes shut and the steam going up past his face. That made seven in a stable yard, and eight bowls, round an iron basket, with a pump behind them and a hill over their heads.
   280	
   281	Cael took the paper out of his coat.
   282	
   283	He did not open it. He stepped round the edge of the fire, to the bucket where a girl sat with her left arm across her knees and her face turned up to him, waiting, as she had waited since a gallery.
   284	
   285	"You first," he said, and put it into her hand.

