121-He told it no. And at his knees, where it had stood since the lane under the wall, the other thing lifted its head.
122-
123:Shadow had come up at dusk, as it came every dusk, and he had told it no in a lane while his eyes ran. It had not lain down; it was full night now, in a town of doorways, and he was going to follow a man through it. There was a seal on that thing, and a reason for the seal which had not altered since Ostrand; and he had never felt it lean so against a shut door.
124-
125-The man under the lamp stepped down into the street.
126-
127:He did not look to left or right; he went away up the house-fronts at a stroll, toward the open place, with his hands behind him, like somebody who has dined.
128-
129-"Count thirty," said Seln. "Then follow me, and not him."
130-
131:The open place was empty. There were forty feet of trough, with the pipe running into its upper end, and not a beast at it, and the smith's two fires were banked. The man in the hood went to the pipe, and bent, and drank from his hand. He drank for a long time, and stood afterward with the water dripping from his fingers and looked at nothing, in the middle of a paved space with no cover on it for sixty yards.
132-
133:Cael stood in a doorway and looked at a place twenty strides beyond him.
134-
135:It was a great deal harder than not looking at a crown. The crown did not move.
136-
137-A light came round the corner by the chandler's, low, jogging, with a rumble of small wheels. It was the watch: two men in leather caps, and a handcart between them, and a lantern slung from a pole at its front.
138-
139-Seln's hand came up beside his coat, flat, palm down. *Stand.* Then two fingers.
140-
141:They fell in behind the cart. It was done as a thing is done on a road, at a walk, without any word; and Cael understood it only when he was there. A watch looks where its own lantern falls; and anybody who turned to see that light would see the light, and two caps, and behind them a darkness in which a pair of porters might have been going home.
142-
143-The watchmen did not turn round at all. One of them was telling the other about a goose.
144-
145-They crossed the open place so, twenty strides behind a hood, to the mouth of the brokers' street. There the cart swung right, for the ring road; and the man with the cut boots went straight on.
--
147-It was as wide as a field, and there was nothing in it. Every slate had been wiped, and every bench stood empty against its wall.
148-
149-"In an empty street nobody hides," said Seln, into his collar. "He goes home. You have the right-hand houses; I have the left. Sixty yards. Do not match his step."
150-
151:Cael went down the right-hand side of the brokers' street, in the open, as a porter goes who has been kept late and has a bed at the bottom of the hill. His boots made a noise, and he let them. He looked at a place twenty strides ahead of a long coat, and the coat walked into it, and he chose another.
152-
153-*What does it weigh?* asked the read, of a tread sixty yards in front of him that he could have had through the soles of his feet, heel and toe, for as long as he liked.
154-
155-*No.*
--
161----
162-
163-The lane went down toward the river, and it smelled of tar and new hemp.
164-
165:Cael knew where it led before he saw. He had stood in that place with a big man, nine days ago, by daylight, and watched him look into the cut end of a rope as a person looks into a flower. It had been full of people walking backward then.
166-
167-It was a shed, open all down one side, and it ran away along the inside of the town wall for three hundred yards. There was a great wheel at its head, and beyond the wheel a floor of trodden boards as straight as a ruled line, under a roof on posts, with coils hanging the whole length of the wall like hats on a row of pegs. A lantern burned at the far end, very small, where the tar yard was.
168-
169-The man in the hood walked in under the roof, past the wheel, and went on down the middle of the boards.
--
193-He made one mistake, and it was not with his feet.
194-
195-He glanced along the shed, to judge how much of it was left; he only meant to measure. But it was three hundred yards of a thing that somebody had built to go in one direction, with every post and plank and peg of it laid toward the far end; and the new thing behind his eyes came round to that as a weathercock comes round, before he had told it anything.
196-
197:*Goes*, it said. The whole roof leaned away from him, down the length of the wall, toward a lantern.
198-
199-He shut it, and it had been open for the space of a blink. A grain of sand came into each eye and sat there, and he ran the last hundred yards with his lids half down, and was angry, and had not the time to be.
200-
201-The tar yard was a square of cobbles above the river, with barrels stood on end in rows and a black kettle on a cold hearth. He went in among the barrels and found two that stood a little apart, and put himself between them with his back to the wall.
--
223-And Shadow asked.
224-
225-It had not once left off, and it asked now as water asks at a sluice. He was standing between two tar barrels at night, in a black coat, making himself a part of the dark by holding his breath; and there was a thing under a seal that would have made him so in truth, to any eyes at all, between one heartbeat and the next.
226-
227:*No*, he said. He had said it to that one twice a day since Ostrand. He said it now as a person leans on a door with somebody leaning on the other side.
228-
229-At seven hundred and ten the boards spoke.
230-
231----
--
243-He counted ten. The hood went on round, as slowly as it had come, to the wall and the quay steps; and the man went down the steps.
244-
245-At the foot, for the length of two strides, the lantern was on him from above. Cael saw a pair of horseman's boots go through its light, with a square of paler leather at each heel. He saw gloved hands, clasped behind, and the back of a hood.
246-
247:He did not know whether he had been seen. He thought, afterward, that the man had looked at the place where a follower would be standing if there were one, and had neither found him nor needed to.
248-
249-"You did not come through after him," said a voice at his shoulder. "That was well judged."
250-
251-Cael had not heard a step, and he let his breath go.
252-
253-"He stood in there. I counted seven hundred."
254-
255:"He stood for eleven minutes. I heard him not walking, which is a sound that I know." Seln was looking down at the quay. "He is in no hurry at all. I begin to dislike that more than I should dislike a man who ran."
256-
257-The quay was long and bare; six flat boats lay along it, nose to tail, with their sweeps shipped. Beyond them the great wheel of the hoist stood up against the water, and beyond that again a cable went out over the river into the dark.
258-
259:Cael looked at the cable, and his stomach went cold.
260-
261-"There's a ferry."
262-
263-"There is. It has no crew before first light. But a man who had one waiting would be on the other bank in a quarter of an hour, and we should be standing here."
264-
265:The man in the hood walked the whole length of that quay, and went by the six boats, and laid a hand on the mooring post of each as he passed, as a person touches the heads of children. He stopped at the cable, and looked along it at the far shore; and he stood so long that Cael began again to count.
266-
267-Then he turned his back on the river, and went up a flight of steps between two warehouses, toward the hill.
268-
269-"He has shown us the ferry," said Seln. "He wished us to think of it."
270-
271-They followed by the steps. There was a lane above, and at its head the ring road, lying under the second wall like a ditch under a dyke; and there the man turned right, and went along it at his stroll, with the pale stone going up on his left hand higher than a house.
272-
273:Cael knew that road. He had walked the whole round of it on his second day in the town, by himself, and had not once lifted his eyes above the top of that wall. He did not lift them now; he looked at a place twenty strides ahead of a long coat, on the cobbles, and went home.
274-
275:It was a quarter of the way round the bluff, and it took the better part of an hour, and the man did not once look round. He stopped twice: once to do up a boot, with his back to them, for a long minute; and once for no reason, in the middle of the way, with his hands behind him. Each time one of the two walked on past him, on the far side, with his chin down; and each time the other had him.
276-
277-The watch's cart came toward them, by the chandler's, with its lantern jogging. The man in the hood lifted two fingers to it as it passed, and one of the leather caps nodded.
278-
279-"They know him," said Cael, in the dark of a warehouse door.
--
285-The man crossed the open place, and went by the smith's two cold fires, and set his foot on the one street that climbed.
286-
287-"No nearer," said Seln, and drew Cael into the smith's doorway. "Look where we are standing."
288-
289:It was a deep doorway, well back from the lamp at the corner. A man had stood in it five nights ago, at an hour when nobody has business in a doorway, and looked at the carriage-arch of an inn.
290-
291-At the head of the street, two hundred yards above them, a brazier burned red in the arch of the second wall. The grating was down; it filled the arch from the keystone to the paving, a net of black iron with the fire behind it, and in front of the fire stood two shapes in long coats.
292-
293-The man in the hood walked up the middle of the street to them.
294-
295:He did not show anything at once; he went to the brazier, and held out his gloved hands to it, and stood so, with his back to the whole district, for as long as it takes to say a rhyme. One of the keepers spoke. The man turned his head, and answered, and put a hand into his coat; whatever came out of it was held low, in the palm, and was looked at, and put away.
296-
297-A small door opened in the foot of the grating.
298-
299-He stooped to it. And in it, with one boot through, he turned round.
--
309-"To do what?"
310-
311-"To see where it stops."
312-
313:Seln looked at him, and did not say that it was foolish. He said, "A person may walk up a street. Do not speak first, and do not stand as though you were waiting to be let in."
314-
315-So Cael walked up it, in the middle, as the other had.
316-
317-It was two hundred yards, and he had gone up them on eight mornings with a card in front of him and a box on his back; he had never done it with his hands empty. The brazier grew as he climbed, and the two long coats took on a colour, which was dark blue.
--
321-It had never asked so loud. Every other place where it had asked had been a doorway that he chose to see as a line. This was a line. Somebody had cut a groove across a road, a hand wide, and lined it with iron, and hung a net of bars above it to come down at dusk; and it had come down. The thing in him knew its own kind. It asked in the voice of a bar going home into two sockets, and it very nearly did not need an answer.
322-
323-He stopped a hand short of the iron.
324-
325:The keepers had watched him come. One of them had a pike, grounded, and the other a board on a cord round his neck, and they were not unfriendly. The one with the board looked him over, from the cap to the boots, and saw what there was to see.
326-
327-"First bell," he said, quite kindly. "And you'll want your card. There's no kerb-work to be had up here before the light."
328-
329-Cael nodded. He did not speak, since he had been told not to, and since there was nothing that he could have said to that man which would not have been a lie or a great deal too much.
330-
331-He stood at the groove for as long as a porter might, who has come up early by mistake.
332-
333:He could have crossed it; he looked at that squarely, with the fire on his face. There was a thing at his knees that would have had him through the small door on anybody's heels, and neither of those two would have known that the air had moved. There was a hiss in his feet which made nothing of walls. He had twelve things in him, and not a keeper in that arch could have named one.
334-
335-He could not cross it lawfully, and that was another matter, and it was the matter that the groove was for.
336-
337-By day he might go through as the fourth part of a figure on another person's card, for a named door and a stated purpose, and be counted out again by dusk; by right he might not go through at all. Iron walked under that arch, and showed a paper, and was written. There was a colour for Iron, and a paler one for the wall above. A plate had put a word after his name that had no colour. There was no card in the district with an edge for what he was; and a man with his spurs cut off had just put a hand into his coat and brought out the one thing that Cael would never own, which was leave.
--
347-Seln had not stirred from the doorway.
348-
349-"Well?"
350-
351:"He's Iron, or he's got a paper that does as well. He showed it low. They opened the little door." Cael leaned on the smith's jamb. His legs had begun to tell him how far they had been. "That's all. We walked three hours to learn that a man can go through a gate."
352-
353:"Eleven guards on one broker's doors can go through that gate at night, and every Iron in the district. I do not call it a finding. I call it a wall." Seln was looking up the street at the red point of the fire. "But I shall tell you what I have, since you have earned the half of it. He was not learning whether he was followed. He knew that from the trough. He took us round by the one shed where we must divide, and stood eleven minutes in it; he showed us a ferry; he showed us a watch that nods to him; and then he went to bed, through a thing that we cannot pass, and turned in the door so that we should be sure of having seen."
354-
355-"Why?"
356-
357-"So that we should know how much time they have. That is the whole of it. They need not take you in a stair. They may stand under a lamp for a month."
358-
359-Cael thought about that, with the cold of the doorway at his back.
360-
361:"He wanted me to do something," he said. "At the barrels. Or at that gate. He stood five yards off and looked at where I was, for a count of ten. It'd have been the easiest thing I've ever done, to be gone."
362-
363-"Yes."
364-
365-"I don't think it was the stair they shut, two nights ago. I think it was this. They want it shown, and they'll wait."
--
369-They went back by the muck-lane. The fist came out from under two blankets as they passed the end stall, and opened, palm upward, on the straw. *Nothing?*
370-
371-Cael turned his own hand over, empty, and the old man shut his eyes.
372-
373:There was a greyness over the roofs by then, and he washed at the pump, and the tar would not come off his palms; he had not known that he had leaned upon a barrel. He wrote three lines by the last of the lantern, with the book held at the length of his arm.
374-
375:*Night of the 291st. Followed the boots, with S., round the whole of the lower town. All of them asked; Shadow asked for three hours. One look that I didn't mean, with the twelfth, at the rope-walk: a grain in each eye. Not counted. Nothing opened.*
376-
377-*He may go through the blue gate at night. I may not go through it at all, except as a figure. It is a hand wide.*
378-
379-*S.: they have time. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:19:Then he sat with the pencil over the leaf for some time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:31:Lira took the book across the gap, and read with her chin down; she came to the foot of the leaf, and went on looking at the place below the last line, where there was nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:7:She had turned on her bucket so that the fire came over her shoulder onto the page. It was one leaf, in a small upright hand, folded in three; she held it in her right hand and steadied its foot with the left, which was slow, and which had come to it without being told.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:43:He had been ready for a harder thing and a softer one. He had not been ready for a true one, said plainly, by the one person who had known him when he had nothing in him but a word in square brackets. Every clerk who had ever looked at him over a desk had looked at him as at a thing that might go off. He had taken it so, and had built himself, a year at a time, into somebody who would not: who stood in corners, and kept twelve doors shut, and told a Path no at a tar barrel with a man five yards away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:69:"He gives what he believed, or wished believed. I've only his word, and he's scraped his own name off it; or somebody has." He shook his head slowly. "I'd want to hear the other side before I'd sign for that. There's nobody left to give it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:73:Cael heard that as he would have heard a figure read out in a court, and it was a strange comfort, and he took it for one. Nobody at that fire had said that the leaf was false, since nobody could. But a big man who had been weighed all his life by a house in Velmere had looked at the largest scale that anybody ever built, and had declined to take its own word for what it was worth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:81:"There is what that leaf says. And there is everything else that any of us knows, or thinks that he knows, about floors, or anything." She drew a breath that was half a yawn, and was too tired to be ashamed of it. "I have filled the one. The other is clean. Nobody is to write upon it while I am asleep."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:143:"You signed the mark at kindlings."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:233:Cleon said, "Tomorrow the rest of the leaf. The list of parts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:267:He sat down against the third box, and took out Hesk's book, and the dividers slid from it into his lap. He set the day at the head of a clean leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:269:*Two hundred and ninety-third day. Treswick; the yard, and then the loft; one in the stall. A hundred and thirty. A leaf read once, below, by Karis. A copy made. Read at a fire by the three who had not heard it. The reader came up, and ate half a bowl. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:271:He held the pencil over the leaf for some time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:275:Lira had turned on her pallet. She put out her hand for the book, as she always did, and read the short lines by the last of the lantern; and she turned the leaf over, to see whether the rest was on its back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:289:There was a bearing on a calfskin, in the second box, with four rings on it and a line that ran off the edge. There was a set of marks in a ruled square which nobody could yet read. There was a bundle on a shelf with its tape untied, and the back of a leaf to be gone through in the morning; and after the morning there was a gate, and a bridge on five piers, and a road that went the way they had come and farther.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:71:It leaned at the tall book, when her hand turned a leaf. *Sits*, it would have said of that page, if he had let it: he could feel the word waiting, flat and level, like a board laid on two trestles. It leaned at the lamp-ring, and at the joint of the table's leg, a foot from his knee, where one peg did the work and another had been put in for show. He told it no each time. It was an easier thing to refuse than Shadow, which pulled, or the wall, which asked; this one only offered, like a clerk holding out a sheet that need not be taken.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:93:"Sixteen," said Karis. She had dust on her cheek, and her left hand had come up out of her lap and was holding the tall book's leaf flat, on its bound palm, as though it had forgotten that it was hurt. "It is a score. It is the old word for twenty, and there are a hundred of them on this page."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:201:He told it in the order in which he had it, which was the order in which it would have to be written: what the thing stood beside, and whose it had been, and how much of it he had; and then, since that was what the man would want, how it had come. He said that he had no Path, and that a plate had told him so. He said that pieces of other people's came to him all the same, when he stood near somebody who was using what he had in earnest. He did not reach for them, he said, and could not stop them; and there had been eleven that morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:215:His voice had risen. Cael had not heard it rise in eight days, for a dray or a cut hand or a lamp that moved; and it had gone up now like a clerk's who has turned a leaf and found a figure where no figure can be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:23:He had heard a leaf of skin read out that morning, once, by a woman who would not say it twice. He did not say it over. He had been asked not to, and he was a man who kept to what he was asked; he had learned that lately, and at some cost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:41:It was a draft. A small grey man had told him at a spring what he was when he drafted, and a small pale woman had laid a bare leaf on a desk and asked him to put nothing on it. He did not finish it. He did not let himself hear how it would have ended, even in the place behind his eyes where a man hears his own clauses before he speaks them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:91:There was a matter that he could settle, and he gave the second flight to it. They would sit in a yard tonight. The big young man had said so at noon: there would be a fire by the stall door, since one of them could not climb a ladder; and he knew who the one was. He would be at that fire. The leaf would be read there by those who had not heard it. And the boy would be across the flames from him, with all of it to carry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:131:"We lie at the house with two doors, a street back from the trough. There will be a fire in its yard this evening, since one of us cannot go up to the loft. I say it only so that you should know where your leaf's copy has gone." She took the card from the slope. "I do not ask you to do anything about it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:137:He had not looked at her, or at the boy beside her with a hand inside his coat. He looked at the leaf of horn on its beech board. Then he laid the upper board over it, as a lid is laid, and set the jar upon the board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:139:Cael went up the stair a flight behind a sound that scraped, as he had said that he would. He did not go nearer. On the second landing he found that the sound had got above him, higher than it had ever been at a bell; and something in his chest that had been shut all day came a little way open at that, and he let it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:181:[[TIER-B: "Made." "For a kind." — the packet gives these two lines as how Seln is told on the street ("Seln is told on the street ('Made.' 'For a kind.')") and pins neither speaker; staged on the carts' street at dusk, between the bar and the lane, with Seln walking at the handcart's tail as a hired porter and Cael pushing beside him; nothing of the leaf's wording is said. The lines before and after are written so that either of the two could own either line; coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:41:It was a single leaf, and it was not paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:43:It had the colour of old horn, and it was stiff, like horn, and a little curled at one corner; and where the lamp struck the curl, the light came through it, brown and clouded, as it comes through the pane of a stable lantern. Somebody had made it of a skin, a long while ago, and scraped it thin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:53:The thing behind Cael's eyes had come round to that leaf as it had come round to nothing since a plank of oak, and it leaned as a dog leans on a leash.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:59:"One hand," said the rough voice at last. "One sitting. He ruled it first, with the same point; you can see the lines under the letters. It's a fair copy, and he didn't hurry." The square finger came to rest in the air above the pale place. "That's later. I can tell you that it's been scraped since it was written. I can't tell you by whom."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:101:[[TIER-B: The leaf (BOOK_MAP §10 Tier A 1), read aloud once by Karis: "In the years of the unmaking, when the un-bound walked in the land and no wall stood against them and no law held, and the cities of the east were emptied and the cities of the west were emptied and the rivers ran wrong" … "there came one who saw the shape of it, and set himself to the ordering. And he made the measure, and the seat, and the tier, and the mark that is given at the kindling, that each should know what he is and none should be what he is not. And he set the apparatus over all the land, and the land was ordered, and the un-bound came no more." / "And the" [the scraped name: four or five letters' width, the first upright stroke showing] "— set the apparatus over all the land, that the un-bound should not come again, who had near unmade the world before the ordering of it. And this is the reason of the apparatus, and this is all of its reason, and let none who come after forget it, though the un-bound be forgotten: that they should not come again." — the plan gives the unit with an elision in its first sentence and a bracketed place for the scraped name; staged as Karis's one reading, standing at Cleon's desk under the fixed lamp, her finger an inch above the skin, with Cleon two paces back with his eyes shut, Cael at the desk's left side and Vastin on a box six strides off; the object (horn-coloured, point-written, the scraped place with its one upright stroke) is described before this line and is not described again; coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:105:She took it away, and did not turn the leaf. She stood with both hands flat on the oak, and looked at the wall of pale rock above the desk, where a pick had left its strokes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:119:"I shall say what I take it to be," said Karis. "I shall say it once as well, and plainly, and then I shall stop. It is not the leaf. It is only my account of the leaf, and it is worth less."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:129:"That word stands here. It is spelled as he has spelled it, letter for letter, in the old signs. *The Architect.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:133:[[TIER-B: Karis: "a title, not a name; the name's gone" — BOOK_MAP §9 gives this fragment of Karis's single paraphrase and not its sentence; staged as her next words at the desk, with the pencil's butt on the word at the leaf's head and then on the scraped place; coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:135:"It gives its own year, at the head," she said. "It is in the count that the old town's books keep, and I have had twelve of those open for a week. By that count this was written about four hundred years ago. I should want a day with the tallies to say it nearer, and I am not going to be given the day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:139:"The rest is what you heard. It says what the apparatus is for, and it says that there is no other reason. It was set over the land so that a kind of people should not come again." Her voice had dropped. "It calls them the un-bound. I said that word at this table on the first day, as a limit; I had only ever met it as the name of a layer in the ground. I did not know that it had been a people."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:145:[[TIER-B: *Older. Made. Us.* — BOOK_MAP §10 Tier A 3 heads this "Karis's copy and finding" and prints it after the copy; the packet sets it directly after her single paraphrase and before Cael's catalogue of the room, and pins no speaker or writer; placed here, by the packet's order. The lines before and after are written so that Karis (said, or written at the foot of her notebook's leaf) or Cael (his own reduction) could own it; coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:151:He went over it as he would have gone over a cut on a moor, from the left. There was a desk of black oak, sloped, and a lamp on an iron arm, with the oil a finger down. On the shelf above stood a cup, which he had drunk from, and a jar; and a clean square in the dust. Two lengths of brown tape lay out to either side of a leaf of skin with twenty-six lines on it. There were two chairs, of which one had a cushion that had been carried from the other. A man stood with his eyes shut. A woman stood with a strip of a shirt round her left hand, and the hand was flat on the wood, and it had left off shaking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:157:He knew what he was doing. An old man in Denvash had taught him to write the count first, and he had made a habit of it, and the habit had served him in a stair three nights ago and on a scree before that. He was counting a gallery so that he need not count the one thing in it which the leaf concerned. He let himself. He thought that he would be given the rest of the day to stand in this room; he would not be given it to stand in the other.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:167:"I can read this at sight," she said. "It is the old town's hand. It is the hand of the tally-books, or its father's." Her pencil went down the leaf without touching. "It is a chronicle. It has the floods in it, and a murrain among the cattle, and who was reeve. Somebody of this town kept a year-book, and somebody else laid it away with that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:179:There was a hole in the paper. Cael could see it from where he stood: a place the width of a thumb, a little way in from the edge, where the leaf was simply gone. Its rim was brown and soft. A worm had done it, or damp; the line of ink ran up to that rim, and went over it, and did not come out upon the other side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:185:She took a slip of card from her notebook, and laid it across the leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:199:[[TIER-B: Vastin: "… the mark that is given at the kindling —" — the packet gives only the stopped end of his sentence ("Vastin reaches ('the mark that is given at the kindling —')") and BOOK_MAP §9 says that his sentence stops in the chamber; staged as said from Brom's box by the nearest bay, six strides from the desk, to the shelves and not to any person, and broken off by Karis's next line; coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:205:"There is what that leaf says. It says it in its own words, of its own time, and I shall copy it on one page, and nothing else shall go upon that page." She took a clean leaf from her notebook, and laid it on the desk, bare. "And there is this. What you were about to say is not on the skin. It may be so. I do not know, and nor do you. If it is ever written, it will be written here, when somebody can show it; and until then this page has nothing on it, and nobody is to rule a line from the one to the other because a word stands in both."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:215:She had said it at a spring, over six years of an office's sheets, to stop a sentence of her own. She said it now with her bound hand on a bare leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:227:She would not be hurried over it, and nobody tried. She sat down at last, in the second chair, on the cushion that was not hers, with a board across her knees. She wrote the first face of that skin out fair, in our tongue, in her small upright hand, with the leaf before her and her eyes going from one to the other at every word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:237:*and the* — there the box stood, with nothing in it — *set the apparatus over all the land, that the un-bound should not come again, who had near unmade the world before the ordering of it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:239:"I am not going to put a name where a knife has been," she said, without lifting her head. "I am not going to put a title there either. There was a word, and it is gone; and that it is gone is a part of what the leaf says."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:285:She looked at him a moment longer. Then she turned, and looked for the first time at the leaf of horn on its board, from six feet off. She put her left hand into her coat, slowly, and took it out again, as she did when she wished to be sure that a thing still moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:195:The pen had risen from the leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:297:The afternoon went as the morning had. It was late, and the flame had been trimmed twice, when the chair at the desk scraped upon the rock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:93:A leaf from an order-pad was folded round the legs. It was in a square upright hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:99:An old man in Denvash had cut that leaf from his pad, and had rolled a pair of dividers in it, and tied his knot. He had sent a ruler before the snow, to a boy who was going where there were no instruments. He could not have known that there was a hide with four rings on it, or a line through the rings which ran off the edge of the skin, or that somebody would shortly need to know how far along that line the next thing lay. He had only thought, at his bench, that a person who has a straight edge will want a pair of legs to step it off with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:209:She said so from the boxes, without lifting her head, in what was left of her voice. Brom set the covered cup down by her knee. She drank it cold, in three swallows, with her eyes on a leaf, and Cael did not think that she had tasted it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:225:*Wind — Lira. Pressure — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron — Brom. Compression — Reydan. Ember — Karis. Shadow — Seln; source protected; sealed; twice in all; it asked for three hours last night and was told no. Storm — Daeva. Anchor — unnamed. Blade — Ephram. Tide — Oryn; ask first, every time; used on nobody this side of the snow, and five times on myself, the last two round the place and not in. Iron Wall — Vastin; held; entered, not told; deployment none. Rune — the reader; a made thing's lean; given; told; deployment none; it looks before I've said that it may, and I am learning to give it nothing to look at.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:231:That was two. He took the dividers out of his coat and laid them, shut, on the open leaf, and wrote the third round them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:239:The line moved while the brass still lay upon the leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:249:He sat quite still, with the pencil off the leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:251:There was a weight on him. He knew it, and he would have given a good deal not to. He had stood in a chalk ring once, in a room with no window, and had felt a thing at the edge of his sight attend to him for eleven seconds while a plate stayed dark. This was fainter than that, and it came from farther off. But it was of the same kind. It was the particular weight of an instrument turning toward him.

