   173	Cael had known that it would come round to him. It had come round at a plank in Lowmarch, and at a spring, and in a loft with a blanket on its window; they had looked at him then for a ruling. They did not look at him now: a girl had sat down again on her bucket and was watching the fire, and a big man was scraping a pot. They were giving him the yard, as they had given it to her.
   174	
   175	He had been carrying the thing since a desk, and he had it still, level, in both hands.
   176	
   177	He thought of Hesk's book. It was in his coat, against his ribs, with a pair of brass legs shut in its pages; and ever since a plate lit he had taken whatever was too large for him and put it there, a line at a time, in the order of its coming. He had done so with a word on a plate, and with twelve fragments, and with a place that did not move. He knew exactly how this would go down, if he let it: *Claim. Evidence. Ruling.*
   178	
   179	He did not take the book out.
   180	
   181	It was not a ruling; there was nothing in it to hold, or to try again in daylight. It had been read, once, in a voice that shook; and it was either so or it was not, and every person at that fire had already taken it for so. If he wrote it he would be putting it where he put a cord and a hair. He would be making it a thing that he had noticed; and it was not that. It was the ground that he had been standing on since he was fourteen, shown to him from underneath.
   182	
   183	So he set it down in the only other place there was.
   184	
   185	He came away from the stable wall, and went the three paces to the basket, into the light, and stood there on both heels with his hands at his sides. He was not going to say it sitting; he did not know why that mattered, and it did.
   186	
   187	[[TIER-B: Pole two, Cael's sentence (BOOK_MAP §10 Tier A 17): "Since my Kindling I thought the system forgot to make room for me," … "It didn't forget. It was *built* so there'd be no room. Four hundred years of a whole world, so that I wouldn't happen." … "I happened anyway." — the plan gives this speech in three pieces with elisions; staged as Cael's, said aloud on his feet at the fire-basket in the inn's yard, to all of them and to nobody in particular, with Hesk's book left in his coat; he does not write it down afterward; coordinator to supply protected text]]
   188	
   189	The fire ticked.
   190	
   191	He stood in it, and waited to learn what he felt.
   192	
   193	He had expected one of two things. He had thought that it would break him a little, to hear it in his own mouth; or that it would lift him, as a thing lifts when a person has at last got his shoulder under it. Neither came. He was not glad; and he was not sorry for himself, which surprised him more, since he had had a good deal of practice.
   194	
   195	He was only standing; it was a yard, with a pump in it. There were six people round an iron basket, of whom one was asleep and one would not look at him, for his own sake; and he had said the worst thing that he knew, aloud, and the cobbles were where they had been.
   196	
   197	It had not got lighter for being said. He found that he could carry it standing up, which he had not been sure of in a gallery.
   198	
   199	[[TIER-B: Pole two, Lira (BOOK_MAP §10 Tier A 17): "Yeah." … "Now what." — the plan gives her two lines with an elision between them; staged as Lira's, from her bucket, the first thing said by anybody after Cael's sentence; coordinator to supply protected text]]
   200	
   201	He looked at her.
   202	
   203	She had asked it as she had called a count at the head of a cut. It was not kindness, or it was the only kind that she had ever thought worth giving him: she had heard the thing, and agreed that it was so, and wished to know what was to be carried next and by whom.
   204	
   205	He had an answer, and he had not known that he had, until she wanted one.
   206	
   207	[[TIER-B: Pole two, Cael's answer (BOOK_MAP §10 Tier A 17): "Now we find out how old the oldest one is. Karis has a bearing and the marks, and the records to read them against. There's a site at the end of the line that's been quiet since they built the machine." … "I want to stand in the middle of it." — the plan gives this speech with an elision before its last sentence; staged as Cael's, standing at the fire-basket, in answer to Lira's "Now what."; coordinator to supply protected text]]
   208	
   209	"That's east," said Brom.
   210	
   211	"It's the way we came. And on."
   212	
   213	"Then I'll want that rope bought before we're through the gate." The big man looked at the sleeping woman, and at an old one with a spoon, and at his own two hands. "And two healers at a mill. We're a poor lot to be walking anywhere."
   214	
   215	"We walked here."
   216	
   217	"We did," said Brom. "I'm only pricing it."
   218	
   219	"It will be watched," said Seln. He had not moved upon his bucket. "The road that you speak of. I should be surprised if a pair of cut boots were not upon it before we are."
   220	
   221	"Then he'll see what he saw at the gate."
   222	
   223	"A porter. Yes. I begin to think it the most useful thing that you carry."
   224	
   225	---
   226	
   227	The man in the dusty coat stood up.
   228	
   229	He had eaten the half of his bowl. He set it on the board, among the others, and stood a moment with one hand before him, by habit, though there was light enough in that yard to have read by.
   230	
   231	He had not looked at Cael, and he did not look now. He had come down through two gates and asked a keeper his road, and sat an hour on a bucket with the fire hurting his eyes; and he had kept one thing the whole of that hour, which was where he did not put them. Cael had seen people look away from him all his life. They had done it at counters, and in the corridors of a house on a bluff, as a person turns from something that may be catching. He had not known that it could be done as a courtesy, and held to, like a term.
   232	
   233	Cleon said, "Tomorrow the rest of the leaf. The list of parts."
   234	
   235	He said it to the small pale woman, who did not hear him. He looked at her for a breath, asleep against a big man's coat; and something moved in the red-rimmed face that was not a smile and was nearer one than Cael had yet seen there.
   236	
   237	"Second bell," he said, to the cobbles.
   238	
   239	He went away under the arch, by the yard, as he had come. His step was quick and flat and even. It stopped once, a stride beyond the arch's mouth, for the space of three breaths; and then it went on up the street toward a brazier and a wall, and they did not hear it any longer.
   240	
   241	"He never ate his dinner," said Brom. "Again."
   242	
   243	"He came down a whole hill," said Lira. "To sit with his eyes shut, and say a dozen words to somebody who was asleep."
   244	
   245	"He came so as not to be by himself with it. It's the same as the bowl." Brom was stacking the eight of them on the board. "You lay one. Sometimes a man sits down to it."
   246	
   247	The fire had burned down to red, and nobody fed it.
   248	
   249	They got Karis up the ladder between them, Lira above and Cael below, more asleep than awake, with her notebook held against her in both arms and the bound hand uppermost. She said one thing on the rungs, quite clearly: she said that there were twenty-three, and that she had them all.
   250	
   251	Seln took the ladder's head; he had the folded page yet, and he gave it into Cael's hand as he went by, without a word.
   252	
   253	They put the old man into his stall; he went on the stick, and one shoulder, and let himself down into the straw by his hands; and when he was down he lay looking at the boards over his head, where six people would sleep.
   254	
   255	"Fourteen," he said.
   256	
   257	"Of thirty."
   258	
   259	"I had it as fourteen. I am glad to have it confirmed." He was silent a moment. "You stood up to say it."
   260	
   261	"Yes."
   262	
   263	"I marked that. I sat for mine. I do not think that I could have done otherwise, with this leg; but I should have liked to." He shut his eyes. "Good night."
   264	
   265	Cael went up.
   266	
   267	He sat down against the third box, and took out Hesk's book, and the dividers slid from it into his lap. He set the day at the head of a clean leaf.
   268	
   269	*Two hundred and ninety-third day. Treswick; the yard, and then the loft; one in the stall. A hundred and thirty. A leaf read once, below, by Karis. A copy made. Read at a fire by the three who had not heard it. The reader came up, and ate half a bowl. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
   270	
   271	He held the pencil over the leaf for some time.
   272	
   273	Then he ruled a line under what he had, and wrote nothing beneath it.
   274	
   275	Lira had turned on her pallet. She put out her hand for the book, as she always did, and read the short lines by the last of the lantern; and she turned the leaf over, to see whether the rest was on its back.
   276	
   277	"That's all?"
   278	
   279	"That's all that's for the book."
   280	
   281	"You said it better than that. In the yard."
   282	
   283	"I said it to you. All of you. You've got it." He took the book from her, and shut the brass into it. "It doesn't want keeping. It's not going anywhere."
   284	
   285	She thought about that, with the slow arm uppermost and her eyes on the slates.
   286	
   287	"No," she said. "I don't suppose that it is."
   288	
   289	There was a bearing on a calfskin, in the second box, with four rings on it and a line that ran off the edge. There was a set of marks in a ruled square which nobody could yet read. There was a bundle on a shelf with its tape untied, and the back of a leaf to be gone through in the morning; and after the morning there was a gate, and a bridge on five piers, and a road that went the way they had come and farther.
   290	
   291	He blew out the lantern.
   292	
   293	The blanket lay close to the frame, where a girl's thumb had pressed it. There was no line on the boards.

