    85	"Is a thing a record says a man made four hundred years ago," Karis said. She said it at once, and plainly, and not unkindly, and she said it looking at Vastin and not at the leaf, because it was a thing that had to be said to a face. "That's what the record says. It says he made a thing and set it over the land and it says why. It doesn't say what the thing he made has to do with yours, or with mine, or with the thing on a station's roof four tiers up, and I'm not going to say it either, because I've a leaf and a board and a floor, and a leaf and a board and a floor are facts about a leaf and a board and a floor." She put her hand flat on the notebook. "A measurement is a fact about a ruler. Not about the hand that held it. I've said that at a slab and a marsh and I'll say it in a hole. I've two findings this morning, and they go on two pages, and I'm not drawing a line between them, because I can't see the line and I'm not going to guess one."
    86	
    87	Vastin looked at her for a while. Then he inclined his head, a finger's width — the Compact courtesy — and said, "Two pages," and did not finish the sentence, and Cael watched him not finish it, and understood that he had just watched the Compact's own held line decline to draw a line, at a table, on a researcher's word, and that the man had done it because the researcher was right and not because she was in charge, and that this was the first time in forty years the man had done that, and that it had cost him nothing, which was the strangest part.
    88	
    89	Cael catalogued the two pages, because he could see them from the corner: the notebook open, the bound hand flat on the left-hand page and the pencil on the right, and the left with the leaf's words on it in her small hand and the right with the column's four words and a date, and the gutter between them, a finger wide, with nothing in it. He had watched her not write in a gutter before, at a slab, with a wind lifting the sheets. He watched her not write in it now, in a hole with no wind, with a marshal across the table who had just asked her to, and understood that the not-writing was the same act in both places and had cost more in the second, and that she had paid it without looking up, and he entered the gutter as the thing the morning had found, and did not write in it either.
    90	
    91	Cleon said nothing. He had said *lamp* and *marked*, and that was his whole ledger for the day, and he sat with his hands flat on nothing and looked at the leaf where the name was not, and Cael, who had watched him for nine days, saw that the man was not looking at the scraped place. He was looking at the word that had been left on. And his face was the face of a man who has been right for thirty years and has just found out, at a table, in a hole, with five strangers, that being right was never going to have been the relief he had thought, and that he had known that, and had gone on anyway, and would.
    92	
    93	---
    94	
    95	She copied it before they went up.
    96	
    97	That was the last thing, and Cael held the lamp for it, because a copy wanted light more than a reading did, and because it was the thing the chamber had been for and he wanted to have held the light for it.
    98	
    99	She copied it into the field notebook, in her own hand — the small square hand that had copied fifty-two sheets of a wall and a kit's ledger and a marshal's six years — from the leaf, a run at a time, with the board beside her for the letters she was not sure of and Cleon's finger going above the leaf ahead of her pencil where a bearer was, and she did not copy the whole leaf; she copied the passage, because the passage was the finding and the rest was the work, and the work was for the road. She copied the scraped place as it was. She did not leave a gap on her page and she did not write a word in it; she drew the patch, the width of it, with its rough edge, and the one upright stroke at its left, and the nothing after, so that a reader of her page would see what a reader of the leaf saw, which was a name's length of a thing that had been taken back to the fibre, and would not be able to say, any more than she could, whether a knife had done it or a thousand thumbs.
   100	
   101	And under the passage, when the copy was done and checked against the leaf twice, she wrote three words, in her own hand, smaller, the way she wrote every finding she did not yet dare to interpret, and Cael read them by the lamp he was holding, and did not read them aloud, because she had asked that nobody say it back to her, and a thing written was not a thing said.
   102	
   103	*"and the"* — then the gap: a name's length of the page scraped back to the fibre, four or five letters' worth, the first stroke of the first letter still showing — *"set the apparatus over all the land, that the un-bound should not come again, who had near unmade the world before the ordering of it."*
   104	
   105	*Older. Made. Us.*

