editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:151:He stood on the top step of twenty-seven and looked at that. He had read every sheet that had ever been written about him from the far side of somebody's desk. There had been a file in a district office, and a thicker one on a bluff; there had been a finding under two hands, and a paper with an hour in it. Each had lain with its head toward the person who owned it, and he had learned to take them so, as a matter of course, as a person learns to read a shop's sign from behind the glass.
