editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-70-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:71:It leaned at the tall book, when her hand turned a leaf. *Sits*, it would have said of that page, if he had let it: he could feel the word waiting, flat and level, like a board laid on two trestles. It leaned at the lamp-ring, and at the joint of the table's leg, a foot from his knee, where one peg did the work and another had been put in for show. He told it no each time. It was an easier thing to refuse than Shadow, which pulled, or the wall, which asked; this one only offered, like a clerk holding out a sheet that need not be taken.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-72-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:73:He understood the lamp now. He had put it low and to the left for six days because he was told to, and had moved it ahead of the word because the eyes always went one way. They went one way because the thing itself did. A made thing was looked at from where it stood upon the ground, toward where it was going, and a light set anywhere else lay across the grain of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-74-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-80-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:81:He looked at that squarely, with his breath going long and the flame standing up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-82-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:83:It would take no time; he need not so much as shift his knee. He had only to let the thing come round, as it kept trying to come round to a table's leg, and turn it instead upon the one place in himself that he had never been able to read.
