editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-122-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:123:He had said that he would. A person who must climb as he had to climb did well to begin early. He had gone off down the gallery on the stick, hand over hand along the black uprights, with a civil refusal for each of the three who offered an arm. He had left the tarred case leaning at the foot of the first flight. Cael heard him before he saw the place: a slow scrape, and a breath, and a scrape, somewhere up in the dark of the first flight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-124-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-134-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:135:The lamp did not move; he would have known it if it had, having become a judge of such things. Only her head came round, slowly, and her eyes went over his face in that brown light as they went over a page of his book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-136-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-144-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:145:"I thought you'd gone white. I thought it was your knee." She looked up the stair, at the dark that was scraping, and down again. "Does it ask? Like the other one?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-146-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:147:"It doesn't ask. It leans. It's leaning at this stair now, and I'm not letting it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-148-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-162-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:163:"That one came once, and it's shut, and I've not touched it. To tell him that much, I'd have to tell him all of it, and I can't take it back out of him afterward. This one's different, and it's not because I think better of the man. I'm at his elbow every day. He says a word and I'm there before it, and from tomorrow I'd be there because I can see what he sees. He made it a term that nobody tells him what a thing is before he's looked. He's looked for a week at a lamp, and it's not a lamp."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-164-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-170-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:171:"Seln told a clerk the truth in a yard," said Brom. "It cost him his face. He said that it was the only way he'd found that stayed right afterward." He looked at his hands. "The fellow's not a counter. He's kept all that for thirty years with never a ticket on a shelf. Tell him what's his. I'd not tell him ours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-172-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-186-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:187:The desk's lamp burned at the far end, on its iron arm, as it had burned on the first morning; and for most of the way it showed him nothing but itself. The shelves went by on either hand as walls go by in a dream. The new thing leaned at every bay. He kept his eyes on the flame, and counted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-188-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-196-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:197:"You said no names, and I'll give you none. You said that nobody was to tell you what a thing was until you'd looked. You've looked. There's a thing about the hand that's held your lamp, and I'd sooner you had it from me tonight than found it out tomorrow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-198-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:199:The reed had stopped, and the grey head did not come round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-200-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-202-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:203:"There's a twelfth. It came an hour ago, at the fifth row, when your finger stopped. It's a piece of yours. I can see which way a run goes. I looked at that row, and at your board, and I've looked at nothing since."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-204-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-236-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:237:For eight days those red eyes had gone over him as water goes over a stone in a ford. He had been a hook, and a hook is not looked at; he had asked to be exactly that, and had minded it, and had written that he minded. They had not gone over him tonight. When the cup was set down they had come to the edge of him. They had come as far as a sleeve, and the wrist of the hand that held the light; and there they had stopped, and the man had turned his whole head away to the oak, as a person turns from a lit window that is not his to look in at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-238-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-276-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:277:The lines had stood behind his eyes for an hour, as each of the eleven had stood, and he had not looked at them. He had had a lamp to hold, and then a thing to say. He took them now, on a stair, a tread at a time, as he had taken the last upon a pallet: slowly, from the top, in the plain words in which they would have to go into Hesk's book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-278-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:279:*Rune-adjacent. Cleon. Partial. Rune family. A made thing's lean — the direction a maker put in it; which mark bears, which is borne, which is nothing; whether a run sits or goes. Structure, not meaning: it will tell me a thing is an instruction and will not tell me what it instructs. Made things only. Not a river. Not a man. Costs the eyes, after. No use in a fight. Engagement field: inscriptive — the second field that is not a fight, after clinical; given, not taken. Tier equivalent unknown. Deployment: none. Twelfth.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-280-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-288-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:289:*He looked away. Kept.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-290-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-4-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:5:He had come down half a mile of carts' street with his lids shut and one hand on the tail-board of a handcart, pushing when he was told to push. In the lane a girl's shoulder had gone beside his own through four alleys, as it always went. It was the one evening in that town when he could not have looked at the crown if he had been paid for it; and he had found that funny, under a wall, with the water running off his chin, and had not said so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-6-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-16-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:17:He ruled a line, and wrote the twelfth's lines under it, in their order, as he had got them by heart on a stair, and added the two that were his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-18-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-20-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:21:There was a thing that he had not written, and it was the largest thing of the day. A man had said it to a plank of oak, with his back turned, after a cup of water; and he had said what was to be done with it. Cael knew what a line in that book was. Lira read the book; and a thing set down there had been said to her, as surely as if he had leaned across two pallets and put it in her ear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-22-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-26-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:27:*Twelfth. Rune, from the reader, at his own table, while he showed Karis how a row goes. Told: Lira first; then him. He is the first outside the six who has it from my mouth how one came. Brom and Karis and Lira gave their word for it on the stair. S. and Oryn to be told. V. is not told, and I have now said to a stranger what I could not say to the man it's owed to. That's heavier than it was this morning. It stands.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-28-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-44-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:45:"You have a twelfth," he said. "And you have told a registered practitioner of the Compact, beneath its oldest registry, how you came by it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-46-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-60-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:61:Karis looked up from her notebook, by the boxes. She had seventeen letters, and a strip of linen on her left hand that wanted changing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-62-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-66-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:67:He did not go back to watching the yard; he looked at Cael a little longer, and then at the blanket on the gable, and at the dark under the slates.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-68-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-84-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:85:He had not known that he would say it until it was out. He had been looked at for nine days, from a doorway and a kerb and the head of a stair, by people who had been told what to look for; and he found that he wished, once, to do the looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-86-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-96-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:97:It was at the stable's far end, a low thing on leather hinges, and it gave upon a lane no wider than a barrow. They passed the end stall to reach it. There was a long shape in the straw there, under two blankets, with a bound leg laid out along a plank; and its eyes were open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-98-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:99:The old man did not speak; he looked at the two of them, and at the door, and then one raw hand came out from under the blanket and shut, slowly, into a fist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-100-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-108-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:109:"And if he looks round?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-110-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-112-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:113:That made two people whom Seln had ever taught, and Cael did not say so. He stood in the mouth of a muck-lane and looked along the house-front, at the round of yellow light under a painted door's lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-114-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-116-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:117:He stood on the step, where a person stands who wishes to be seen, beside a book on a stand that he had not written in. His hood was up, and he wore a long coat of no colour. Cael looked at the boots, since he had been told of them four times: a horseman's, cut high, and at each heel a square of paler leather where a spur's rest had been taken off with a knife.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-118-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-130-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:131:The open place was empty. There were forty feet of trough, with the pipe running into its upper end, and not a beast at it, and the smith's two fires were banked. The man in the hood went to the pipe, and bent, and drank from his hand. He drank for a long time, and stood afterward with the water dripping from his fingers and looked at nothing, in the middle of a paved space with no cover on it for sixty yards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-132-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:133:Cael stood in a doorway and looked at a place twenty strides beyond him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-134-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-136-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:137:A light came round the corner by the chandler's, low, jogging, with a rumble of small wheels. It was the watch: two men in leather caps, and a handcart between them, and a lantern slung from a pole at its front.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-138-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-140-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:141:They fell in behind the cart. It was done as a thing is done on a road, at a walk, without any word; and Cael understood it only when he was there. A watch looks where its own lantern falls; and anybody who turned to see that light would see the light, and two caps, and behind them a darkness in which a pair of porters might have been going home.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-142-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-150-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:151:Cael went down the right-hand side of the brokers' street, in the open, as a porter goes who has been kept late and has a bed at the bottom of the hill. His boots made a noise, and he let them. He looked at a place twenty strides ahead of a long coat, and the coat walked into it, and he chose another.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-152-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-164-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:165:Cael knew where it led before he saw. He had stood in that place with a big man, nine days ago, by daylight, and watched him look into the cut end of a rope as a person looks into a flower. It had been full of people walking backward then.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-166-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-194-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:195:He glanced along the shed, to judge how much of it was left; he only meant to measure. But it was three hundred yards of a thing that somebody had built to go in one direction, with every post and plank and peg of it laid toward the far end; and the new thing behind his eyes came round to that as a weathercock comes round, before he had told it anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-196-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:197:*Goes*, it said. The whole roof leaned away from him, down the length of the wall, toward a lantern.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-198-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-226-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:227:*No*, he said. He had said it to that one twice a day since Ostrand. He said it now as a person leans on a door with somebody leaning on the other side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-228-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-246-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:247:He did not know whether he had been seen. He thought, afterward, that the man had looked at the place where a follower would be standing if there were one, and had neither found him nor needed to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-248-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-258-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:259:Cael looked at the cable, and his stomach went cold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-260-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-264-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:265:The man in the hood walked the whole length of that quay, and went by the six boats, and laid a hand on the mooring post of each as he passed, as a person touches the heads of children. He stopped at the cable, and looked along it at the far shore; and he stood so long that Cael began again to count.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-266-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-272-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:273:Cael knew that road. He had walked the whole round of it on his second day in the town, by himself, and had not once lifted his eyes above the top of that wall. He did not lift them now; he looked at a place twenty strides ahead of a long coat, on the cobbles, and went home.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-274-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-288-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:289:It was a deep doorway, well back from the lamp at the corner. A man had stood in it five nights ago, at an hour when nobody has business in a doorway, and looked at the carriage-arch of an inn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-290-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-294-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:295:He did not show anything at once; he went to the brazier, and held out his gloved hands to it, and stood so, with his back to the whole district, for as long as it takes to say a rhyme. One of the keepers spoke. The man turned his head, and answered, and put a hand into his coat; whatever came out of it was held low, in the palm, and was looked at, and put away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-296-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-312-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:313:Seln looked at him, and did not say that it was foolish. He said, "A person may walk up a street. Do not speak first, and do not stand as though you were waiting to be let in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-314-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-324-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:325:The keepers had watched him come. One of them had a pike, grounded, and the other a board on a cord round his neck, and they were not unfriendly. The one with the board looked him over, from the cap to the boots, and saw what there was to see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-326-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-332-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:333:He could have crossed it; he looked at that squarely, with the fire on his face. There was a thing at his knees that would have had him through the small door on anybody's heels, and neither of those two would have known that the air had moved. There was a hiss in his feet which made nothing of walls. He had twelve things in him, and not a keeper in that arch could have named one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-334-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-350-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:351:"He's Iron, or he's got a paper that does as well. He showed it low. They opened the little door." Cael leaned on the smith's jamb. His legs had begun to tell him how far they had been. "That's all. We walked three hours to learn that a man can go through a gate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-352-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-360-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:361:"He wanted me to do something," he said. "At the barrels. Or at that gate. He stood five yards off and looked at where I was, for a count of ten. It'd have been the easiest thing I've ever done, to be gone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-362-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-372-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:373:There was a greyness over the roofs by then, and he washed at the pump, and the tar would not come off his palms; he had not known that he had leaned upon a barrel. He wrote three lines by the last of the lantern, with the book held at the length of his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-374-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:375:*Night of the 291st. Followed the boots, with S., round the whole of the lower town. All of them asked; Shadow asked for three hours. One look that I didn't mean, with the twelfth, at the rope-walk: a grain in each eye. Not counted. Nothing opened.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-376-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-14-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:15:He knew how she read, having handed her a book across two pallets on most nights of three hundred. She took a line as she took a wall: she found the first hold, and did not go on to the second until the first had been tried with her whole weight; and when a word was strange to her she said it over under her breath, twice, until it had left off being strange. She did that four times in the first reading, and he saw her lips make the same shape each time, and he knew which word it was, and he looked at the fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-16-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-28-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:29:Lira said nothing more, and she finished. She laid the page on her knee, face upward, and put her hand flat upon it, as a person keeps a paper from the wind. She looked into the basket for as long as it takes a log to settle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-30-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-34-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:35:She lifted her head, and looked across the fire at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-36-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-42-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:43:He had been ready for a harder thing and a softer one. He had not been ready for a true one, said plainly, by the one person who had known him when he had nothing in him but a word in square brackets. Every clerk who had ever looked at him over a desk had looked at him as at a thing that might go off. He had taken it so, and had built himself, a year at a time, into somebody who would not: who stood in corners, and kept twelve doors shut, and told a Path no at a tar barrel with a man five yards away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-44-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-54-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:55:He did it with the page held a long way off, and his head on one side, as he looked at a wall that he had been asked to buy. When he had done he did not read it again; he handed it up to Seln, across the fire, and sat for some time with his forearms on his knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-56-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-64-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:65:"He was as frightened as that. You can read it off the bill; it's the only part of him that's left." Brom looked into the fire. "I'm not saying that he'd cause. A price isn't a verdict. I've known a man pay a great deal for a bad horse, and swear by it after, because of what he'd paid."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-66-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-72-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:73:Cael heard that as he would have heard a figure read out in a court, and it was a strange comfort, and he took it for one. Nobody at that fire had said that the leaf was false, since nobody could. But a big man who had been weighed all his life by a house in Velmere had looked at the largest scale that anybody ever built, and had declined to take its own word for what it was worth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-74-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-98-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:99:"I came into this town carrying a thing that I had not decided," said Seln. "I looked at it from a roof. I looked at three doors, and at a shelf with no ticket on it, and I ruled nothing. I had supposed that it was the largest thing that I should ever be asked to hold." He looked down at the folded page. "I find that it is not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-100-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-114-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:115:"I read how a thing's made," said the voice like a hinge. "I'll tell you that, and no more, since it's all I have. It's one hand. He ruled it before he wrote it. He pressed like a mason and he didn't hurry, and it's as old as it says it is; I'd put my name to that at any counter." The square thumbs went round the bowl's rim. "Whether he's told the truth in it, I can't say. A man can write a lie in a fair hand. I've a hundred and ten strides of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-116-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-126-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:127:It was Brom, and it was all that he said; he had the ladle in his fist, and he leaned across, and filled the man's bowl to the brim, though it had not been emptied.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-128-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-130-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:131:He had held it since the pot came, and had not once lifted it. He put it on the stone beside his hip, carefully, as he did everything; and he laid both hands upon the head of the ash, and looked into the fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-132-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-138-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:139:On the sixth bucket the red eyes had shut again, and the grey head had turned a little farther from the flame, and Brom was looking at his boots. Cael stood where he was, with the heat of the basket on his shins; and he found that he could be looked at by that man, across a fire, and stand under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-140-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-152-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:153:She had not softened it by the width of a hair. She looked at him a moment longer, across the flames, as she would have looked at a figure that she had been asked to check and had found right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-154-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-158-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:159:The old man looked at the bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-160-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-166-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:167:Cael had watched the whole of that from the wall. He had believed, for the whole of his time on a bluff, that the worst thing which one person could do to another was to write him down. He had now heard a man say across a fire that he would have signed the sheet. He had seen a girl agree that it was so, to his face, and then feed him; and he looked in himself for the wish that she had done otherwise, and it was not there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-168-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-172-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:173:Cael had known that it would come round to him. It had come round at a plank in Lowmarch, and at a spring, and in a loft with a blanket on its window; they had looked at him then for a ruling. They did not look at him now: a girl had sat down again on her bucket and was watching the fire, and a big man was scraping a pot. They were giving him the yard, as they had given it to her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-174-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-200-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:201:He looked at her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-202-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-212-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:213:"Then I'll want that rope bought before we're through the gate." The big man looked at the sleeping woman, and at an old one with a spoon, and at his own two hands. "And two healers at a mill. We're a poor lot to be walking anywhere."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-214-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-230-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:231:He had not looked at Cael, and he did not look now. He had come down through two gates and asked a keeper his road, and sat an hour on a bucket with the fire hurting his eyes; and he had kept one thing the whole of that hour, which was where he did not put them. Cael had seen people look away from him all his life. They had done it at counters, and in the corridors of a house on a bluff, as a person turns from something that may be catching. He had not known that it could be done as a courtesy, and held to, like a term.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-232-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-234-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:235:He said it to the small pale woman, who did not hear him. He looked at her for a breath, asleep against a big man's coat; and something moved in the red-rimmed face that was not a smile and was nearer one than Cael had yet seen there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md-236-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-38-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:39:Cael looked at what lay beneath, since he had been told to stand there, and since it was the thing that all of them had walked to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-40-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-52-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:53:The thing behind Cael's eyes had come round to that leaf as it had come round to nothing since a plank of oak, and it leaned as a dog leans on a leash.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-54-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-56-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:57:The man had bent over the skin. He looked at it from its foot, without a blink, and the water ran; and Cael, who had no lamp to move, found his empty hand going left of its own accord.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-58-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-62-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:63:"I've looked," he said. "It's yours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-64-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-78-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:79:Cael counted, since there was nothing else for him to do. He counted the lines on the skin, by the pale place, and made them twenty-six; he counted the breaths of the man beside him, which were slow. He looked at the lamp on its iron arm, and saw the oil in the glass stand a finger lower than when the tape came off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-80-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-104-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:105:She took it away, and did not turn the leaf. She stood with both hands flat on the oak, and looked at the wall of pale rock above the desk, where a pick had left its strokes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-106-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-114-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:115:Nobody looked at Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-116-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:117:He marked that, and it was done three ways. The red eyes were shut, and had been since before she began. The grey ones had gone to a bay of bundles and were reading the under side of a tag. And the small pale woman, who had asked him to stand there, kept her face to a wall. He had been looked away from by one person in that gallery, two days ago, on purpose and for his own sake. It appeared to have spread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-118-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-124-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:125:"You made it a term that I should tell you nothing of what I thought until you had looked. I have kept this from you for nine days by that term, and from myself. It is a line that a man copied out of a register, a long way from here, and carried three hundred miles." She did not look toward the box. "It sends its reader to the lower archive at this town for a thing that is held here in full. And it has a word in it, which I had never met."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-126-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-180-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:181:Karis looked at the hole, and did not put her pencil near it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-182-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-192-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:193:He shut them again. Neither of them said one word more about that line, then or afterward, and Cael did not go over it. He looked at the rim of the hole, and at a slip of card; and he put them where he put a hair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-194-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-202-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:203:She had turned round; it was the first time since the tape was untied that she had looked at anybody, and she looked at him as she had looked across a table at Lowmarch, when a courier's flat lay between them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-204-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-216-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:217:"I was drafting," said Vastin. His voice had very little breath behind it. "I was told at that same spring, by another person, what it is when I do so. I was drawing a conclusion, in a room where I have been at some pains for nine days to draw nothing." He looked down at the ash. "I leave it where it stopped."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-218-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-234-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:235:She looked at the pale place on the skin for some time. Then she took up the brass strip from Ternhall, which she had not had out of its cloth since the hide was rolled, and ruled a small box in the line that she was writing. It was four letters wide, by her own letters; she ruled it on all four sides, and left it empty, and went on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-236-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-246-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:247:She finished the face. She laid the pencil down across the board, and flexed her fingers, and looked at what she had done as a mason looks along a course.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-248-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-264-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:265:Lira had come to his shoulder. She did not look at the skin; she looked at him, as she had looked on a stair by a lamp, going over his face a line at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-266-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-284-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:285:She looked at him a moment longer. Then she turned, and looked for the first time at the leaf of horn on its board, from six feet off. She put her left hand into her coat, slowly, and took it out again, as she did when she wished to be sure that a thing still moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-286-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-2-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:3:The bridge was a made thing that morning, and it had never been one before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-4-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:5:It was the two hundred and ninety-second morning past the Line, and Cael saw it from the kerb of the carts' street, where the hill dropped away and all the roofs of the district lay below. It stood out on the brown water as it had stood the day before, small and dark, on five piers. He had looked at it then as a person looks at a road that he came in by. Now it was the oldest thing that he could see from that hill which anybody had built. He had that on the word of a small pale woman, who had laid her hand where the masons' pale stone was cut to fit it. And the new thing behind his eyes came round to it, across half a mile of air, like a head lifted from a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-6-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-8-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:9:He put his eyes on the tail-board of the handcart. That was no better. The tail-board had a hinge, and the hinge leaned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-10-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:11:It was the same wherever he turned. A river is not made, and a man is not; but a town is nothing else, from the ditch to the weathercock, and he had been given a thing that inclined toward every peg and course of it as a dog's nose goes to every post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-12-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:13:He had gone through this once already, with another matter. For eleven days he had not looked at a light on a crown, and he had not done it by shutting his eyes. He had done it by having somewhere else to put them, and a reason, twice a day, until his neck no longer set at an alley. He did the same now. He looked at a raw hand on a tarred case, which nobody had made, and at the back of a girl's neck, and pushed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-14-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-28-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:29:There was tar on the lamp's ring before the first sheet had been looked at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-30-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-48-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:49:Cael saw her find it. It was a group of three signs, and it stood so often on the Stair's long table that she had passed it over as packing; and she went back to it, and looked from the pencil to the brown book, and sat quite still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-50-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-56-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:57:There was a group of six signs on the Stair's long table which stood four times in those twenty-nine, and always after a white. She had not let herself look at it for four days; she had laid a glove across the place whenever her work took her near. She lifted the glove now, by one finger. She had every letter of the six, and she did not read them at once. She looked first at the man with the reed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-58-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-90-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:91:They were of brass. There were two legs, each as long as his hand, flat on the inner side so that they lay close. At the foot of each was a point of blued steel, as fine as a thorn, the pair of them thrust into a round of cork. The legs met at the head in a joint with a broad flat rivet, and above the rivet stood a little turned stalk for the fingers. They had been rubbed with something, so that the last of the light ran down them like water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-92-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-108-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:109:He ought not to have looked at the joint. He had been not looking at things since the second bell; but this lay in his own two hands, and it was a gift, and for the space of a breath he was only a boy with a new tool.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-110-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:111:*Bears*, said the twelfth, of the rivet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-112-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:113:It had come round before he could stop it. Both legs hung from that one pin; he saw the whole of the thing lean inward to it, the two brass arms and the steel at their feet, as everything on a gate leans to its hinge. It was true, and it was no more than his eyes could have told him. The sand came into them all the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-114-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-118-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:119:"It looked. I didn't tell it to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-120-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-138-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:139:"Four," said the woman, when he had told her what he wanted. She looked at him as she had looked on the first day, when he asked for a seventh bowl. "In front."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-140-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-160-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:161:He looked away, at the candle, and that was worse. He looked at his own hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-162-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-164-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:165:He had spoken with his mouth full. He leaned back, and knocked the far leg with the side of his boot, twice, and it gave a little dry rattle in its socket.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-166-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-176-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:177:The dividers went round the table once, shut, with the cork on. Seln turned them over in his fingers and gave them back without a word, which was praise. The old man held them for some time. He opened the legs a little way, and set the two points upon the board, an inch apart, and looked at the inch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-178-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-190-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:191:Nobody asked him what; he had not offered it to be asked. Cael counted the word, as he counted anything, and put it beside three others that the same man had given him at a pump; and he understood this one no better, and found that he had not expected to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-192-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-194-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:195:He had looked at them a good deal, through the goose and the pie, as he looked at an entry that he could not at once place. He had sat at more boards than anybody in that room. At every one of them a cover had been laid for whoever was present, in the order of the roll, and a person who was not in the room had no place in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-196-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-202-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:203:Nobody added to that. The old man turned his own cup a quarter round upon the board, and looked into it; and Brom reached over with the jug, since he could reach, and filled it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-204-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-212-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:213:He had not meant to make the inventory that night. He made one at the start of a thing; and he could not have said, climbing the ladder, what was starting. But he had a twelfth, which was not yet in any count. He had a pair of brass legs in his coat from a man who had taught him to write the count first. And there was a bundle on a shelf, two hundred yards over his head, which a pair of square pale fingers would untie in the morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-214-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-224-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:225:*Wind — Lira. Pressure — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron — Brom. Compression — Reydan. Ember — Karis. Shadow — Seln; source protected; sealed; twice in all; it asked for three hours last night and was told no. Storm — Daeva. Anchor — unnamed. Blade — Ephram. Tide — Oryn; ask first, every time; used on nobody this side of the snow, and five times on myself, the last two round the place and not in. Iron Wall — Vastin; held; entered, not told; deployment none. Rune — the reader; a made thing's lean; given; told; deployment none; it looks before I've said that it may, and I am learning to give it nothing to look at.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-226-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-234-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:235:He looked at the brass for a moment. He had a ruling upon himself, a hundred and twenty-four days old, that he was not to try a measure with his own hand; and here was a thing whose whole purpose was to be opened upon a distance and carried. He did not open it. He had a hair in a small pale woman's notebook to remind him why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-236-
