editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md-240-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:241:There was a blanket on the gable window, hung on two nails by a girl on their first night, and it had hung there since. It did not quite meet the frame on the left side. Each night a thread of the crown's light came in by that gap, and lay across the boards a yard from his knee, pale and thin and as straight as a chalk line. He had never looked at it as a light. It was a mark on a floor, as the bars in the lane were marks on the cobbles, and he stepped over it going to his pallet.
