editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:311:She had seen it done forty times on that road, at one gate or another, and had never got used to how little there was to see. Inside the Line it was a matter of offices. She had stood in one herself: a room with a desk, and a ring chalked on the boards, and a clerk who looked at a plate of brass and not at her. The district had named her day a year beforehand. Out here there was no desk, and no plate, and nobody to write. An old woman scratched a ring on a threshing floor with a hazel stick, on a morning that she chose by the moon; and the child stood in it; and it came.
