editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:351:She was thinking of a boy on a table, under her two hands, a long while since, who had told her a thing with his breath going slow. He had stood in a ring too, and it had been chalk, and there had been a clerk. A small light had come up from behind his breastbone to the edge of his eye, as it had come just now to that child; and it had looked at him, for a count that he still knew; and then it had gone out. She had asked him whether it hurt. He had said no; he had said that it was like a wick being pinched.
