editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:53:The thing behind Cael's eyes had come round to that leaf as it had come round to nothing since a plank of oak, and it leaned as a dog leans on a leash.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:75:He had been a wall. He did not say that with any pity for himself; it was the plainest account of Iron Wall, and of his office, and of what a seat had used him for. A crowd came to a gate, or a hearing drew too many, and he was sent for. He struck his stick upon the ground, and whatever stood on the far side of the line stayed there. He had done it eleven times for a seat, and twice since for no seat at all. He had believed, each of the eleven times, that the thing behind him was the law, and that it was imperfect, and that it was a great deal better than what would come through if he lifted his heel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:87:He stopped there, on the dark stone, with one hand lifted to the next tread. He could feel that the thought had a second half; and the second half was the sentence again, come round by another door. He saw it for what it was. *Draft nothing. Just look.* He put the hand down upon the stone, which was cold and dry and laid without mortar, and looked at that instead until the thing had gone by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:5:It was the two hundred and ninety-second morning past the Line, and Cael saw it from the kerb of the carts' street, where the hill dropped away and all the roofs of the district lay below. It stood out on the brown water as it had stood the day before, small and dark, on five piers. He had looked at it then as a person looks at a road that he came in by. Now it was the oldest thing that he could see from that hill which anybody had built. He had that on the word of a small pale woman, who had laid her hand where the masons' pale stone was cut to fit it. And the new thing behind his eyes came round to it, across half a mile of air, like a head lifted from a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:11:It was the same wherever he turned. A river is not made, and a man is not; but a town is nothing else, from the ditch to the weathercock, and he had been given a thing that inclined toward every peg and course of it as a dog's nose goes to every post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:111:*Bears*, said the twelfth, of the rivet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:113:It had come round before he could stop it. Both legs hung from that one pin; he saw the whole of the thing lean inward to it, the two brass arms and the steel at their feet, as everything on a gate leans to its hinge. It was true, and it was no more than his eyes could have told him. The sand came into them all the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:157:He made his second mistake of the day over the pie.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:159:He had his elbows on the board, and his eyes had gone down to the wood, idly, as a person's do who is full. It was an old table. It had been mended. *Bears*, the thing said, of the two trestles in the middle; and of the leg at the far end, by the old man's stick, it said *nothing*, as flatly as it had said so of a red square of tile.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:213:He had not meant to make the inventory that night. He made one at the start of a thing; and he could not have said, climbing the ladder, what was starting. But he had a twelfth, which was not yet in any count. He had a pair of brass legs in his coat from a man who had taught him to write the count first. And there was a bundle on a shelf, two hundred yards over his head, which a pair of square pale fingers would untie in the morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:225:*Wind — Lira. Pressure — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron — Brom. Compression — Reydan. Ember — Karis. Shadow — Seln; source protected; sealed; twice in all; it asked for three hours last night and was told no. Storm — Daeva. Anchor — unnamed. Blade — Ephram. Tide — Oryn; ask first, every time; used on nobody this side of the snow, and five times on myself, the last two round the place and not in. Iron Wall — Vastin; held; entered, not told; deployment none. Rune — the reader; a made thing's lean; given; told; deployment none; it looks before I've said that it may, and I am learning to give it nothing to look at.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:33:[[TIER-B: "there" — the packet names this as the word at which the twelfth arrives ("the acquisition at 'there'") and gives neither its sentence nor its speaker; staged as the next thing said over the Stair's ninth sheet, at the moment when Cleon's finger comes to rest above the end of the fifth row. The paragraphs after this line are written so that Cleon or Karis could own the word. If the word falls inside Cleon's speech above, strike this marker and let Karis's "The strata lean too." follow the acquisition; coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:41:Nothing on the paper had stirred; the signs stood where her pencil had put them, square and small, each in its place. But the row had a way to it now, as a field of barley has a way when the wind has gone over it and every stalk still bows a little toward where the wind went. The white sign at the head stood up straight, and all that followed it hung from it, and inclined, each toward the next. At the row's end the lean came down flat, like a foot arriving on a floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:43:*Goes*, it said, of that row. It said it without any voice at all; it was only there, as the weight of a stone is there in the hand that lifts it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:49:There was a table under that sheet, with pegs in it. There was a ring of brass in his fingers, and a desk six strides off, and a stair of dark stone that somebody had laid. The new thing inclined toward each of them in turn, mildly, like a head coming round. He did not let it look. He had been given a pair of eyes that were not his, in a room that belonged to the man they came from, and he did not know yet what they cost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:51:It was the twelfth. He knew whose it was, and he had been a hand to its left for six days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:71:It leaned at the tall book, when her hand turned a leaf. *Sits*, it would have said of that page, if he had let it: he could feel the word waiting, flat and level, like a board laid on two trestles. It leaned at the lamp-ring, and at the joint of the table's leg, a foot from his knee, where one peg did the work and another had been put in for show. He told it no each time. It was an easier thing to refuse than Shadow, which pulled, or the wall, which asked; this one only offered, like a clerk holding out a sheet that need not be taken.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:83:It would take no time; he need not so much as shift his knee. He had only to let the thing come round, as it kept trying to come round to a table's leg, and turn it instead upon the one place in himself that he had never been able to read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:91:*Not until she's heard it*, he said, to the thing behind his eyes; and it lay down, having only ever offered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:135:The lamp did not move; he would have known it if it had, having become a judge of such things. Only her head came round, slowly, and her eyes went over his face in that brown light as they went over a page of his book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:187:The desk's lamp burned at the far end, on its iron arm, as it had burned on the first morning; and for most of the way it showed him nothing but itself. The shelves went by on either hand as walls go by in a dream. The new thing leaned at every bay. He kept his eyes on the flame, and counted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:199:The reed had stopped, and the grey head did not come round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:203:"There's a twelfth. It came an hour ago, at the fifth row, when your finger stopped. It's a piece of yours. I can see which way a run goes. I looked at that row, and at your board, and I've looked at nothing since."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:279:*Rune-adjacent. Cleon. Partial. Rune family. A made thing's lean — the direction a maker put in it; which mark bears, which is borne, which is nothing; whether a run sits or goes. Structure, not meaning: it will tell me a thing is an instruction and will not tell me what it instructs. Made things only. Not a river. Not a man. Costs the eyes, after. No use in a fight. Engagement field: inscriptive — the second field that is not a fight, after clinical; given, not taken. Tier equivalent unknown. Deployment: none. Twelfth.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:173:Cael had known that it would come round to him. It had come round at a plank in Lowmarch, and at a spring, and in a loft with a blanket on its window; they had looked at him then for a ruling. They did not look at him now: a girl had sat down again on her bucket and was watching the fire, and a big man was scraping a pot. They were giving him the yard, as they had given it to her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:17:He ruled a line, and wrote the twelfth's lines under it, in their order, as he had got them by heart on a stair, and added the two that were his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:27:*Twelfth. Rune, from the reader, at his own table, while he showed Karis how a row goes. Told: Lira first; then him. He is the first outside the six who has it from my mouth how one came. Brom and Karis and Lira gave their word for it on the stair. S. and Oryn to be told. V. is not told, and I have now said to a stranger what I could not say to the man it's owed to. That's heavier than it was this morning. It stands.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:45:"You have a twelfth," he said. "And you have told a registered practitioner of the Compact, beneath its oldest registry, how you came by it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:137:A light came round the corner by the chandler's, low, jogging, with a rumble of small wheels. It was the watch: two men in leather caps, and a handcart between them, and a lantern slung from a pole at its front.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:193:He made one mistake, and it was not with his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:195:He glanced along the shed, to judge how much of it was left; he only meant to measure. But it was three hundred yards of a thing that somebody had built to go in one direction, with every post and plank and peg of it laid toward the far end; and the new thing behind his eyes came round to that as a weathercock comes round, before he had told it anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:197:*Goes*, it said. The whole roof leaned away from him, down the length of the wall, toward a lantern.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:331:He stood at the groove for as long as a porter might, who has come up early by mistake.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:375:*Night of the 291st. Followed the boots, with S., round the whole of the lower town. All of them asked; Shadow asked for three hours. One look that I didn't mean, with the twelfth, at the rope-walk: a grain in each eye. Not counted. Nothing opened.*

