editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:15:He knew how she read, having handed her a book across two pallets on most nights of three hundred. She took a line as she took a wall: she found the first hold, and did not go on to the second until the first had been tried with her whole weight; and when a word was strange to her she said it over under her breath, twice, until it had left off being strange. She did that four times in the first reading, and he saw her lips make the same shape each time, and he knew which word it was, and he looked at the fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:89:"Nine days," he said, very low. "And a hand sewn. She's earned a bucket."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:105:Nobody asked him how long a while was; he had given a great many words, for that purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:117:"You said that she'd not like it. A week ago."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:173:Cael had known that it would come round to him. It had come round at a plank in Lowmarch, and at a spring, and in a loft with a blanket on its window; they had looked at him then for a ruling. They did not look at him now: a girl had sat down again on her bucket and was watching the fire, and a big man was scraping a pot. They were giving him the yard, as they had given it to her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:207:[[TIER-B: Pole two, Cael's answer (BOOK_MAP §10 Tier A 17): "Now we find out how old the oldest one is. Karis has a bearing and the marks, and the records to read them against. There's a site at the end of the line that's been quiet since they built the machine." … "I want to stand in the middle of it." — the plan gives this speech with an elision before its last sentence; staged as Cael's, standing at the fire-basket, in answer to Lira's "Now what."; coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:249:They got Karis up the ladder between them, Lira above and Cael below, more asleep than awake, with her notebook held against her in both arms and the bound hand uppermost. She said one thing on the rungs, quite clearly: she said that there were twenty-three, and that she had them all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:269:*Two hundred and ninety-third day. Treswick; the yard, and then the loft; one in the stall. A hundred and thirty. A leaf read once, below, by Karis. A copy made. Read at a fire by the three who had not heard it. The reader came up, and ate half a bowl. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:289:There was a bearing on a calfskin, in the second box, with four rings on it and a line that ran off the edge. There was a set of marks in a ruled square which nobody could yet read. There was a bundle on a shelf with its tape untied, and the back of a leaf to be gone through in the morning; and after the morning there was a gate, and a bridge on five piers, and a road that went the way they had come and farther.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:5:The man in the dusty coat had ruled it from his chair, on the two hundred and ninety-third morning, before any of them had a load off. It was the seventh day of his week; there were twelve of the old town's tally-books lying open along a table's edge, and he would not have a tape touched while one of them was out of its bay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:21:"I should like one of you here. I do not wish to read it to a chair, and a man who shuts his eyes." She drew a breath. "You have knelt at this for eight days, and been given a sore knee for it. I think that you are owed the end of the week."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:47:They were the floors' letters, and he could not read one of them; but he had held a lamp above that woman's column for eight days, and he knew a hooked upright when he saw it, and a gate, and a fork.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:117:He marked that, and it was done three ways. The red eyes were shut, and had been since before she began. The grey ones had gone to a bay of bundles and were reading the under side of a tag. And the small pale woman, who had asked him to stand there, kept her face to a wall. He had been looked away from by one person in that gallery, two days ago, on purpose and for his own sake. It appeared to have spread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:125:"You made it a term that I should tell you nothing of what I thought until you had looked. I have kept this from you for nine days by that term, and from myself. It is a line that a man copied out of a register, a long way from here, and carried three hundred miles." She did not look toward the box. "It sends its reader to the lower archive at this town for a thing that is held here in full. And it has a word in it, which I had never met."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:135:"It gives its own year, at the head," she said. "It is in the count that the old town's books keep, and I have had twelve of those open for a week. By that count this was written about four hundred years ago. I should want a day with the tallies to say it nearer, and I am not going to be given the day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:157:He knew what he was doing. An old man in Denvash had taught him to write the count first, and he had made a habit of it, and the habit had served him in a stair three nights ago and on a scree before that. He was counting a gallery so that he need not count the one thing in it which the leaf concerned. He let himself. He thought that he would be given the rest of the day to stand in this room; he would not be given it to stand in the other.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:197:It came down upon the rock, once, six strides away. The old man had sat through a reading and an account with his hands shut upon it, and his face turned to a bay of bundles; and whatever he had been holding off had got past him. He spoke to the shelves, low, and with care, as he had once begun to draft an order at a spring.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:215:She had said it at a spring, over six years of an office's sheets, to stop a sentence of her own. She said it now with her bound hand on a bare leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:217:"I was drafting," said Vastin. His voice had very little breath behind it. "I was told at that same spring, by another person, what it is when I do so. I was drawing a conclusion, in a room where I have been at some pains for nine days to draw nothing." He looked down at the ash. "I leave it where it stopped."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:275:"That's twice in three days that you've had leave and I've had none." She said it quite low. Her chin was up, and he knew that she was angry, and that she would not be angry for long. "Is it bad?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:5:It was the two hundred and ninety-second morning past the Line, and Cael saw it from the kerb of the carts' street, where the hill dropped away and all the roofs of the district lay below. It stood out on the brown water as it had stood the day before, small and dark, on five piers. He had looked at it then as a person looks at a road that he came in by. Now it was the oldest thing that he could see from that hill which anybody had built. He had that on the word of a small pale woman, who had laid her hand where the masons' pale stone was cut to fit it. And the new thing behind his eyes came round to it, across half a mile of air, like a head lifted from a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:13:He had gone through this once already, with another matter. For eleven days he had not looked at a light on a crown, and he had not done it by shutting his eyes. He had done it by having somewhere else to put them, and a reason, twice a day, until his neck no longer set at an alley. He did the same now. He looked at a raw hand on a tarred case, which nobody had made, and at the back of a girl's neck, and pushed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:35:He said it to the ring. His red eyes had come to the brass, and to the smear upon it, and they stayed there as though a line had been drawn at the edge of the metal; they did not go on by the width of a finger to the hand that held it. For seven days they had gone over that hand as over a hook. This was not the same. Cael watched a man take care, and he knew what the care was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:57:There was a group of six signs on the Stair's long table which stood four times in those twenty-nine, and always after a white. She had not let herself look at it for four days; she had laid a glove across the place whenever her work took her near. She lifted the glove now, by one finger. She had every letter of the six, and she did not read them at once. She looked first at the man with the reed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:75:Cael knew the hat before he knew the man. He had seen it across a river with ice in it, on the far bank, on the day that the last ferry ran; and there had been a bag under that arm then, as there was now. It was the carrier who took whatever went between the towns of the Line and one plank under an eave, some days east.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:127:"I should like the loan of those," she said. "Not now. When I have a hide in front of me again, and a point upon it to carry from. I have been stepping that line off with a strip of brass and my thumbnail." Her eyes had not left the points. "I am going up. I have twenty-one, and I have found the twenty-first only once."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:135:It was Brom's ruling, and he made it at the half-door with the purse in his fist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:137:"A parcel's come the breadth of the country to a hayloft, and found it. That's eaten on. We've had the pot for ten nights and said nothing against it. Tonight we'll have what she'd give a carter with money."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:141:He counted four marks onto the ledge, and she swept them off it with the side of her hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:143:"A hundred and thirty," he said, to the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:153:He set a saucer over each of the two cups, to keep the dust out, and stood them at the table's end. One was a small pale woman's, who was above their heads with a lantern and twenty-one letters. The other had been carried under the lashing of his box, as an empty bowl, for eleven days; and the person whom it was kept for was a long way north along a hill-foot, with a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:197:"One of those is for a lady who is in a hayloft with a pencil," he said. "The other is for a woman who went north eleven days ago, and does not know that there is a table."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:219:*Treswick; the loft over the stable; one in the stall. The two hundred and ninety-second day. Eight days inside. A hundred and thirty: four to a dinner, on Brom's ruling, and worth it. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:235:He looked at the brass for a moment. He had a ruling upon himself, a hundred and twenty-four days old, that he was not to try a measure with his own hand; and here was a thing whose whole purpose was to be opened upon a distance and carried. He did not open it. He had a hair in a small pale woman's notebook to remind him why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:241:There was a blanket on the gable window, hung on two nails by a girl on their first night, and it had hung there since. It did not quite meet the frame on the left side. Each night a thread of the crown's light came in by that gap, and lay across the boards a yard from his knee, pale and thin and as straight as a chalk line. He had never looked at it as a light. It was a mark on a floor, as the bars in the lane were marks on the cobbles, and he stepped over it going to his pallet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:285:"Twenty-seven days out of the wool," she said, of the arm that had done it. "It's good for something."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:291:She came to it at dusk on that same day, two days behind her own reckoning and no longer troubling to count them. It was a larger hold than the others. Its wall was of stone, as high as a man's head, with a fence of split oak above; and the bench under its gate-house eave was long enough for twelve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:295:The old woman had waited. She was the one who kept that hold's days, as some woman did in each of them: when the rams went in, and when the beans; who was owed what, and whose turn it was at the gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:359:She was two days behind, and there was a fifth gate, and a ford, and a mill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:15:*Two hundred and ninety-first day. Treswick; the loft; one in the stall. A hundred and thirty-four. By the carts' street, both ways, on wheels: a weight chalked at the bottom gate to the name on the card, and to nobody else. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:23:He did not write it. He had kept one other thing out of those pages on purpose, at a spring, eleven days ago; he marked that this made two, and that he could not write that either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:59:"Then it is all." Seln put his hands into his sleeves. "I should have said yes, on that stair. I should have been a good deal longer about it than a big man with a box. He has kept a hundred and ten strides of paper for thirty years, and has not put a ticket on a bundle; I have considered that fact for five days on my own account." He was silent a moment. "It is very nearly the only thing in this town that I trust."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:61:Karis looked up from her notebook, by the boxes. She had seventeen letters, and a strip of linen on her left hand that wanted changing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:85:He had not known that he would say it until it was out. He had been looked at for nine days, from a doorway and a kerb and the head of a stair, by people who had been told what to look for; and he found that he wished, once, to do the looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:165:Cael knew where it led before he saw. He had stood in that place with a big man, nine days ago, by daylight, and watched him look into the cut end of a rope as a person looks into a flower. It had been full of people walking backward then.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:289:It was a deep doorway, well back from the lamp at the corner. A man had stood in it five nights ago, at an hour when nobody has business in a doorway, and looked at the carriage-arch of an inn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:357:"So that we should know how much time they have. That is the whole of it. They need not take you in a stair. They may stand under a lamp for a month."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:365:"I don't think it was the stair they shut, two nights ago. I think it was this. They want it shown, and they'll wait."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:375:*Night of the 291st. Followed the boots, with S., round the whole of the lower town. All of them asked; Shadow asked for three hours. One look that I didn't mean, with the twelfth, at the rope-walk: a grain in each eye. Not counted. Nothing opened.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:5:He did it on a box, by a bay of bundles, in the afternoon of the two hundred and ninety-third day. Nobody wanted him. The lamp had come back to the long table, and the boy knelt beside it, a hand to the left, as he had knelt on every other afternoon. The small pale woman was making a second copy. The man in the dusty coat sat in his chair with his eyes shut. It was a room with one office in it, and Vastin had not once that day felt his boot begin to move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:41:It was a draft. A small grey man had told him at a spring what he was when he drafted, and a small pale woman had laid a bare leaf on a desk and asked him to put nothing on it. He did not finish it. He did not let himself hear how it would have ended, even in the place behind his eyes where a man hears his own clauses before he speaks them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:49:Three nights ago, in a stair, six people had come to see what that boy was; and he had stood upon a step with his stick, on the eleventh day of thirty, and put a wall in front of him. He had not known then what stood behind it. He had said so, on a pallet, when he could not hold a bowl. He knew a little more this afternoon. He found that he would do it again, knowing; and that in a day which had taken the floor out from under forty years, it was the single act of his that he could look at without wishing to revise.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:55:"I shall want the start," he said to the boy, who had come to see what he needed. "And I should prefer to take it by myself. I have had your hand under my heel, or at the other end of a strap, on every flight for three days. I am obliged for each of them, and I should like to learn whether I can manage one stair without."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:61:The eyes that looked back at him were tired. They were nineteen years old, and they had heard what he had heard, and they had gone back to holding a lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:67:It was dark from the second tread. He had no need of light; he had learned those steps in three days as a blind man learns a room. He set his two hands on the stone behind him, and thrust with the sound leg, and came up by one. The bound leg went before him, stiff, like a thing that he had been asked to mind for somebody else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:77:He was not sure that he had been wrong in every case. He would not let himself off so cheaply as to say that he had been wrong in all of them, either. He would have to go over the eleven, one at a time, when he had a table and a month. It was the kind of work that he was good at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:83:He had met the boy across a desk, with a plate between them, on a bluff. He had read him for three days, with every instrument that the seat owned, and had written that the instruments could not reach him. He had declined to read him again. He had told the boy why, on a bank, at dusk: the sound reason, and the poor one beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:121:"I said a week. I'll be a day over it. There's the back of that skin to do, and I'll not have it done against a clerk's bell." He pushed the card along the slope. "He'll write *the same*. He'll have to find something else to write the day after."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:233:It was the first fire that they had had in thirteen days. He marked that as the wood caught. They had lain in a loft for eleven nights on another person's hearth, under a term that said no fire of anybody's, in a town where a struck point was a line in a book. He had forgotten how a yard changes when there is a flame in the middle of it: how the dark draws back to the walls, and people's faces come up out of it, one at a time, each lit from below.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:11:He looked from its foot. Cael had moved the flame before the grey head moved, and it was there. The eyes went up the brown page slowly, without a blink, and the water came into them, and they went up the sheet beside it. Then, which they had never done in eight days, they went back and forth between the two.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:15:[[TIER-B: Cleon: "A record sits … An instruction *leans* … This is a stair." — the plan quotes this speech in three pieces with elisions (packet, "The reader (291st)"; BOOK_MAP §10 Tier C S13 gives the second and third pieces); staged as Cleon's, said to Karis while his eyes go between the open tally-book (a record of loads) and the Stair's ninth sheet laid beside it foot to foot, with Cael on one knee a hand to the left holding the lamp; coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:31:Cael had the light there before it, and did not have to think of where. He had been looking where that man looked for six days together, a hand to the left of him, and had taken it for a groom's knowledge of a horse. The finger came to the fifth row, and went along the signs, the white and the three black and the red, without touching one of them, and slowed as it went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:51:It was the twelfth. He knew whose it was, and he had been a hand to its left for six days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:59:She had asked for his joins, and he was giving them to her; he could not have done it with a pebble five days ago. He had needed to say aloud, to somebody who stood in his boots, what he had stood in them and seen for thirty years; and it seemed that the saying had loosened the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:73:He understood the lamp now. He had put it low and to the left for six days because he was told to, and had moved it ahead of the word because the eyes always went one way. They went one way because the thing itself did. A made thing was looked at from where it stood upon the ground, toward where it was going, and a light set anywhere else lay across the grain of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:87:There were two reasons, and he made himself set them in order, since a wish is quick and a reason has to be fetched. The man who owned this had told a gallery what it was not for, five days ago, in the voice of a carter giving his rate. It did not work on a man. And a healer had put an order on that place, on a plank, a long way east: *Write it down and stop touching it.* He had read her order once already for the leave that he wanted. He had gone round the place with what he had, at a waypost, and had called it obedience, since he had not gone in. He had been ashamed enough of that to write it, and to owe her the telling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:89:He would not read her twice. He would be at her mill inside of eight days, and she could be asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:107:"Of twenty-three," said the man.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:111:"Two days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:127:She had the lamp, and she had set her foot on the bottom tread. Brom was behind Cael, under his box, and Karis behind Brom, with her notebook shut against her chest and seventeen letters in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:151:"That's two in two days," he said. "You'll want a bigger book."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:163:"That one came once, and it's shut, and I've not touched it. To tell him that much, I'd have to tell him all of it, and I can't take it back out of him afterward. This one's different, and it's not because I think better of the man. I'm at his elbow every day. He says a word and I'm there before it, and from tomorrow I'd be there because I can see what he sees. He made it a term that nobody tells him what a thing is before he's looked. He's looked for a week at a lamp, and it's not a lamp."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:177:He slid the beckets off. Brom shifted along, and laid one forearm across the lid of the third box, with a purse in it under five thumbs, as a man lays his arm along a gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:215:His voice had risen. Cael had not heard it rise in eight days, for a dray or a cut hand or a lamp that moved; and it had gone up now like a clerk's who has turned a leaf and found a figure where no figure can be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:237:For eight days those red eyes had gone over him as water goes over a stone in a ford. He had been a hook, and a hook is not looked at; he had asked to be exactly that, and had minded it, and had written that he minded. They had not gone over him tonight. When the cup was set down they had come to the edge of him. They had come as far as a sleeve, and the wrist of the hand that held the light; and there they had stopped, and the man had turned his whole head away to the oak, as a person turns from a lit window that is not his to look in at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:285:There was a line which he did not need to puzzle at. He had heard it stated like a carter's rate, and he had watched it paid for eight days, in water running down beside a man's nose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:307:Cael had set his boot on the nineteenth tread, with the case, and the lamp was above him, eight steps up, in Lira's hand. It was a small brown flame in a smoked glass. He had knelt beside it for six days. It went into his eyes now like a handful of sand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:323:It was given a step at a time, in the level voice in which a clerk reads a column: the right foot, and the left, and that the tread was dry. For eight days Cael had gone two steps below that stick, so that there should be something under it if it fell. He had not thought to find the thing turned about. He bore up his end of the case, and put his boots where he was told, and was brought up out of the dark by a person who could not stand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:19:"I shall not go up that again," he said. "I say it now, so that it need not be argued at dusk by people who are tired. The stall at the end has nothing in it. I have looked at it through the boards for nine nights. I will not say that I looked forward to it; but it has straw, and its floor is level with the yard."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:133:He had to put them somewhere. The wall was on his right hand, and above the wall was the crown; and he knew, without having once looked, where on that crown a pale thing stood. He had felt it go round him all that way, like a lamp carried round a room behind a person's back. So he looked left, and down, at a town laid out in three steps to a brown river. There was the bridge. It was small and dark and it stood on five piers; and a square woman had gone north from a fork with a mule, ten days ago, and would be at a mill before he was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:135:*Bring me whatever walks out of that archive with you.* He had brought fourteen letters, as yet, in another person's notebook.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:153:"Him," said the man. "Down the hole. I've had this beam eleven years, and I never chalked a thing to him yet." He looked at the boxes. "Came by the slot, the other days, I suppose. On backs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:165:"That's a hand," said Lira. "He's on the card. He's not walking, this week."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:169:The old man's chin had come up. Cael knew that look. He had seen it in a registry's yard, six days ago, a breath before a name and a rank and an office were laid on a ledge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:197:There were three things by now that the man had not asked. Cael had them counted, since he counted anything. Six days ago an old porter had laid a name and a rank and an office on that ledge, and nothing had been said of it since. This morning the same matter had come in by the bottom gate, on wheels, with another man's chalk against it, after seven days on backs. And somewhere in the yard there would be a yard-man who had gone up the cut at first light with a broom, and found a tread lying out of its bed, and two plain bolts, and had brought the bolts to somebody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:239:Cael knelt at the Stair's table from the third hour, a hand to the left, with his forearm along his thigh and the flame standing up. He had been a lamp for six days; he could have done it asleep. That was the trouble. It left the rest of him free, and there was a new thing in the rest of him that had not yet learned the house.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:273:It was dull work for a lamp, and slow for a researcher. She had fourteen letters, and she wanted nine, and they would not be hurried. Twice in the forenoon Cael saw her glove go down over a group of signs, and stay there, and come away again with nothing written.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:277:"I am not stuck. I have a group of nine signs, and I do not know whether it is one word, or two, or the tail of one and the head of the next. If I knew where his words ended, I could lay them against the old books by their length. I have been guessing at the joins for two days, and I do not care for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:305:"Stand here. Where I stand. No; here." He had taken a pace to his right, and he pointed at the rock where his own boots had been. "I've given you bones for a week, from outside, with pebbles. That's all I've ever given anybody. There's another way that I see it, and I've not found the words for it in thirty years, since nobody wanted them." He rubbed his eyes with the heel of his hand. "You want his joins. I can't hand them to you. I can only show you where I'm standing when I see them."

