./books/book-04-copper-crown/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:24:Plants required for future reveals, plus locked threads this book must NOT resolve:
./books/book-04-copper-crown/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:188:Karis, six weeks into observing the counter-surveillance study, names the possibility nobody has said aloud: every acquisition condition her Book 3 model established is assembling itself around Seln. Sustained close observation — running for weeks. Genuine stakes — the operation is aimed at Cael's enrollment, his file, his future; nothing in the building is *more* adversarial. The stakes rule never required combat. It required earnest engagement against him, and a fifteen-year intelligence officer running a live operation on Compact instruction is the purest case the model has ever seen. Her conclusion, delivered with the formality she reserves for findings she doesn't like: "The conditions are met. If you directed it, I believe it would complete. Which is why we have to talk about whether you may."
./books/book-04-copper-crown/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:361:*Key beat (the half-second):* one moment, unplanned, that the chapter stages with total precision — a staged exercise's apparatus overloads, a mis-set resistance the panel didn't intend, and Cael's correction is instantaneous, minimal, and *perfectly sized*: no fragment surfaced, no capability revealed, just a control adjustment executed faster and cleaner than the demonstrated tier should allow, gone before the instruments resolve it. Gault notes an apparatus fault. The faculty seats note nothing. Vastin's gaze, which has not moved all session, does not move now either — and that, Cael understands with cold clarity, is itself the tell: the man saw it, sized it, and required no second look.
