./v3-runs/book-08/editor-wrapper.template.md:7:Additional required checks for this series (craft/VOICE_CHARTER.md is in the context): run the §9 tic greps against the draft and report counts; run the §8.4 metadata grep; check §8.5 attribution runs; check the fragment inventory and every injury against the state snapshot and the previous chapter; check every name against craft/NAME_REGISTRY.md by ear; confirm nothing in the packet's prohibited_outcomes appears; confirm the chapter's final beat type differs from the previous chapter's; and answer in strengths/taste: is the worldbuilding done through mechanism and use, and is any fight or set piece choreographed memorably (terrain load-bearing, win writable as "X removed Y's ability to Z")?
./books/book-02-iron-circuit/chapters/chapter-08.md:31:He recognized the method itself before he understood its results, which was its own point of interest: the boy prepared. The bout Brom watched him win that first week had been won days earlier, somewhere in the audience, in the pages of the notebook that never left his hands — Brom had asked around, casually, and learned that the unranked one watched every prospective opponent for hours before agreeing to terms, and that the circuit had stopped finding this strange. Brom found it strange in the best sense. He did his own preparation through the pressure-read, passively, a capability doing his watching for him. The boy did it with nothing but eyes and ink, manually, hour over hour, and arrived somewhere Brom's instructors would have insisted required a Path to reach.
./books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-08.md:135:Which meant the seeding was not a starting position. Past a certain depth it was a ceiling. From twenty-second, the top was six wins away — six perfect afternoons, demanding but entirely available. From fiftieth it was thirteen, which a good season could still reach. From the high sixties the arithmetic simply closed — more wins required than bouts available — and it closed well before that for anyone who missed a single session, which everyone did. A quarter of the Copper column had been eliminated from the crown before the boards went up, by a scheduling constraint, and every one of them was down there fighting for it anyway.
./books/book-04-copper-crown/chapters/chapter-08.md:155:Ephram at Iron Rank 6 was the top-ranked enrollee at Halcenvane and behaved like it, which was to say that he did not perform any of it. He came to the yard, he fought when the ladder required it, he won, and between bouts he stood at the rail with four or five of Rooke's cohort around him and dissected whatever had just happened on the floor in a low, level voice that carried further than he probably intended. Cael listened to it whenever he could get within range, and found — irritatingly — that the analysis was good. Not as good as it thought it was. But good.
./books/book-02-iron-circuit/chapters/chapter-03.md:83:He'd seen two of the sweeps himself, from the middle distance he preferred for watching officials work. They had a choreography as readable as any fighter's: always two officers, never one, entering the district in the slack mid-morning hours when the market was staffed but not crowded; always the same route inward, main street to the notice boards to the boarding-house row, because the route was written in a procedure somewhere and procedure didn't know the district had four better streets; always the credential cases carried in the left hand, leaving the writing hand free. The district's response had its own choreography, older and better rehearsed. Vendors didn't close — closing looked like guilt — but transactions slowed to exact-change purchases, nothing that required a ledger-book to come out under an official eye. Children evaporated from the sweep's path a street ahead of it, reappearing a street behind. And the Ironyard, both times, had gone quiet in a precisely calibrated fashion: training continued in the side alcoves, audible and innocent, while the main floor's schedule board — Cael had checked, both times — spent the sweep's duration wiped clean, rebuilt from Dace's memory within an hour of the officers clearing the district gate. Nothing hidden, exactly. Just nothing left lying where a procedure could trip over it.
./books/book-02-iron-circuit/chapters/chapter-03.md:209:He watched her pull the volume she actually needed, watched her check its spine against some private catalogue she kept entirely in memory, and lingered a moment longer near the oldest section than the errand strictly required, studying the cracked bindings from a respectful distance without touching them. Even the physical objects told a story, read carefully. The old volumes weren't uniform like Vell's thirty-eight — they varied in height and thickness, bound in whatever leather their decade had afforded, and two of the oldest had been rebound at some point, newer spines grafted onto older bodies, which meant someone between then and now had judged their contents worth the cost of a binder when a binder cost real money. Records survived by being carried, and carrying was a choice, made fresh by each keeper in turn. Somebody had chosen these, every time, for longer than the Compact's current registry had existed.
./books/book-01-the-shattered/chapters/chapter-08.md:13:That got her to look up. She studied Cael for a moment with an expression that wasn't quite concern and wasn't quite skepticism — something more practiced, more neutral, the look of someone whose job required her not to have opinions about the people who walked in front of her, even when a number like his made the job briefly harder to keep neutral about. Her gaze moved once, quickly, over his build — not unkindly, just assessing, the way Cael assessed opponents from the crowd — and then returned to the ledger.
./books/book-03-no-path-given/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:22:Plants required for future reveals, plus locked threads this book must NOT resolve:
./books/book-03-no-path-given/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:98:Lira's re-certification track is harder than advertised. The provision grants academic access but marks her file at every turn — separate sign-in on the training floor, an assessor's initial required beside her bout results. The academy is not cruel about it; the *procedure* is cruel on its own, without needing anyone's help. The chapter gives her one scene alone with the notice board, looking at her name with the provisional marker beside it, and one scene refusing to let it matter: she takes the floor against Greyvane's second-ranked student and wins with the not-holding-back circuit style that no academy taught her. The floor goes quiet. Fenmark's records say Copper-tier. The floor just watched something else.
./books/book-03-no-path-given/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:111:The format: a closed session in the training hall. Panel of three — Quenna presiding, Wray, one rotating faculty seat. Cael must demonstrate "consistent, observable practice." He is not required to name his Path. He is required to show that what he does is real, repeatable, and controlled. The tension of the chapter is calibration: every capability he shows becomes institutional record, and institutional records travel. He demonstrates the Wind-adjacent evasion framework and the Iron-adjacent surface read — his two most stable fragments — at deliberately conservative deployment, and holds back the Pressure-adjacent, the Compression-adjacent, and everything about acquisition. Showing enough to pass while banking everything that matters: the skill Vell's circuit spent a year teaching him, transplanted into a room with higher stakes.
./books/book-03-no-path-given/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:199:The file is worse than she expected in a way she didn't expect: it's *thin*. The insubordination finding that ended her academic life runs four paragraphs. No hearing transcript, because there was no hearing — Fenmark's disciplinary provision for Copper-tier scholarship students required none. Three years of her life redirected by four paragraphs and a signature. The chapter sits with her in that — the discovery that the institution that broke her didn't even do it *carefully* — and then watches her put it down and walk onto the assessment floor. The reassessment itself is the answer: she demonstrates advancement so far beyond her file's Copper ceiling that Quenna's panel logs the session with the notation "advancement inconsistent with recorded tier; recommend continued track with priority review." It is the academy system, for the first time in her life, writing down that her file might be wrong.
./books/book-03-no-path-given/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:278:He comes through Greyvane's gate mid-morning, with an aide and a document case, and presents credentials the gate clerk has never seen in person: not a local warden's writ — a **Guilds Compact field commission**. The reveal is procedural before it is dramatic, which is exactly Coss: *Warden* was never a town title. It is a Compact field-agent grade, and he has held it since before Denvash. The man who has held the file on him since the morning of his Kindling, who filed the reports, who stood down Havel's questions — he has been the Compact's hand on the file the entire time, patient at a distance because patience at a distance was what the assignment required. Greyvane changed the assignment.
./books/book-03-no-path-given/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:436:- [x] **Continuity note — RESOLVED:** Book 2's actual drafted prose (Ch23–24, verified directly, not just the architecture doc's old 14–16 arc-range note) confirms Cael is **fifteen** at Book 2's close, with no time skip into Book 3 Ch1. This matches the series bible's Book 3 hearing line ("a fifteen-year-old") exactly. All three "sixteen" references in this document (Ch1 intake, Ch21 hearing, Ch21 close) have been corrected to fifteen — no bible change needed, no birthday scene required in Book 3.
./books/book-02-iron-circuit/chapters/chapter-18.md:25:By what eventually reached Ardenmere, he wasn't here for money. His background was professional, comfortable, the kind of fighter who could have spent his entire career inside sanctioned guild competition without ever needing an unsanctioned circuit's modest purse to survive on. He'd trained at a proper academy from age eleven, advanced cleanly through every formal evaluation his tier required, and built, by twenty-two, a reputation solid enough that most guild-track opponents at his level had either already fought him or actively arranged not to. That was, as it turned out, the actual problem. The challenge available inside sanctioned competition had dried up years before he was ready for it to. Guild matchmaking, careful and conservative by design, simply couldn't keep producing opponents worth his genuine attention once word of his skill had spread far enough to make people cautious around him.
