editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-01.md:143:His preliminary assessment had been in the spring. Every child in the district had one. You sat in a chair at the district office while an assessor laid two fingers on your wrist and her other hand flat on your chest and frowned at nothing for a minute, and then she wrote something down. Most children got *standard coupling*, but Cael's assessor had looked at him a moment longer than she had looked at the boy before him, and written *irregular coupling patterns*, and under it, kindly, *uncommon variation, no action required.* He had looked up *irregular coupling patterns* that afternoon, and there had not been much, except those four lines a page further on in the same summary.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-05.md:209:It stopped a little short of a hug, and the little did not matter. The hand was hard, an instrument-maker's hand that had spent forty years closing on tools and had never been taught to close any more gently than the job required. Through it Cael learned what Hesk could never have told him in words and been believed: that the old man was holding on to him exactly as hard as he was holding on back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-23.md:203:It also had to be somebody who would not hurt a fourteen-year-old more than a bout required. That mattered to her more than anything else on the list, and it shortened the list a great deal.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-04.md:37:The seal was dark wax pressed with the Compact's mark, a hand holding a balance, so cleanly that a machine must have done it. Cael thought, for no good reason, of the brass plate on Pellin's desk, and all that care poured into the outside of things. He broke the seal and unfolded the paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-04.md:169:Cael found, a little to his surprise, that he did not think it was wrong, or not completely. It was the choice of a man trying to carry two loads in one pair of hands: the job of getting Cael ready for what was coming, and the instinct not to hand him more weight than the day required, and those two things did not fit together neatly. They hardly ever did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-02.md:119:There was a pause, in which Cael learned that he had no idea how his grandfather spent his Tuesdays. He had thought Hesk went to the guild house on Tuesday evenings to look over the trade notices, and he had never asked, because it had never occurred to him there was anything to ask. The notebook came open beside his plate, and *ask Hesk about Tuesdays* went into the margin with two lines under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:81:A year of being ready, and he was not ready, and it turned out you could be both. He knew the procedural summary by heart: *The practitioner stands within the marked circle. No external device is required. Every practitioner's Arbiter is their own, dormant since birth, and Kindling is the moment it wakes.* What the waking felt like, nobody had managed to tell him; only that it came.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-30.md:29:He had done what the brief required of him with any exactness. He had confirmed the location. He had confirmed the status. He had walked the district for two days and seen nothing he could write down as practice in the sense the certification offices meant it, and he was honest enough with himself to add that he was no longer sure he would have known it if he had. There remained the one instruction in the brief that was not vague. *Summon the subject for compliance evaluation as procedure requires.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-30.md:149:*You are required to present yourself at the district office, the post at the head of the fish steps, Ardenmere, for a standard compliance evaluation, within forty-eight hours of the delivery of this summons, during the hours the post is open.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-32.md:175:"Now I stamp the summons closed, pending, and I apply to regional for a senior evaluator, because that's what the regulation asks for in your case." Coss picked up a stamp from the corner of the desk. He held it a moment, longer than stamping a paper required, and Cael understood that he was weighing something that had nothing to do with stamping. Then he brought it down on the summons with a good deal more force than the job needed. The desk rocked. *Tock.* "Senior authorization takes time. The file goes up to regional to be read, somebody qualified has to be found to evaluate it, and then they've to be sent all the way out to a district at the far end of everything. Six weeks, as a rule. Longer if the evaluators are busy, and they usually are."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-36.md:159:There were three of them at the table: the reviewing officer from the guild's own office, a thin careful man with a portfolio, whom Hesk had met once when he came to look at the workshop and ask about the drawer of springs. The other two were members chosen by lot, as the guild's rules required. One was a lens-grinder from the Inner District whom Hesk knew only by sight. The other was a woman he had sat near at thirty years of guild suppers without ever once learning her name, which he realized only as he sat down. She was a gauge-maker with a shop on the canal, a small fierce woman with a magnifier on a ribbon round her neck. She looked at his bound hand and raised her eyebrows and said nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-33.md:171:It was not long. He set down that he had attended for the compliance evaluation as summoned, and that before it could begin, the subject had presented a written procedural objection. He gave the citation, in full: the part, the section, the note, the register, the statute, the appendix. He set down that he had checked the citation against the regulation and found it correct. He set down that the standard evaluation could not lawfully proceed, and that he had marked the summons closed, pending, and filed for senior evaluation authorization as the regulation required.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-13.md:93:That was the result. There was no name in it. The book did not carry the names of unrated debuts, only what they were; a name went into the book when the yard had a reason to need it, and most debuts never gave it one. It was two lines. It was all the book required, and it was all anybody coming to the book in a year, or ten years, would need in order to know what had happened in the yard tonight and who owed whom what.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-13.md:159:There was one more line to write, and it was not one the book required, and she wrote it anyway, at the bottom of the entry, smaller, in the space she used for things that belonged to her and not to the circuit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-48.md:127:*You are required to attend a senior evaluation, convened under the District Handbook, Part Six, at the post, Ardenmere, on the Thursday of this week, at the second hour after noon.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-48.md:223:It was dull, and it was long, and he understood very little of it on the first reading and a good deal more on the second. There were definitions, and who might ask for a senior evaluation, and how an assessor was to conduct one, and what happened when none of the rest applied. Section four was a long dry paragraph about practitioners whose classification could not be assessed by ordinary means, and a temporary category they could be entered under, and a facility they could be required to attend. He copied it line by line like all the rest, and thought, *that's for people the Arbiter couldn't read*, and went on to five.
