editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:179:*The traverse holds. The map's good. Karis says the herd moves off declarations, which means the loudest thing on this mountain is us, and we've been quiet for five days. One more day. First honest job I've ever had that didn't have a fight at the end of it. Sleep.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:7:There were fifty-two marks in the purse, and the name at the top of the sheet was still up the north water on two holds' slips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:31:"It's the same thing with a wheel on it." Pike laid his one hand on the paper. "Oxhollow's six days north-west of this plank, and Thornwater's two days east of it. A cart from the one that wants grain from the other comes round by my ferry, and it's eight days, and eight home. There's high ground between the two holds. The salt crews say there's a shelf along the top of it, a bench, with a gorge down one side. A mule string went over it once, before I'd this book, and came down in three days." He looked at the horned head, twice. "Somebody's told the ford so. The ford wants to know whether it's true before it sends a wheel."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:49:Cael read the slip through once more, for the thing that was not in it. There was no name on it but the hold's. Nobody had asked to have it kept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:61:The second was that nobody's finger was on it. The last large slip they took had carried a captain's name in capitals. A one-armed man had struck that name with his own pen before a meadow, and they had gone down to a river under the weight of it. This one had been posted for anyone at all, with no hold on it. If they took it they would take it off nobody, and the captain at the top of the sheet could read it when he came down and find nothing in it that had been his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:69:It was work, besides, that four half-mended people could do. A man with a bitten calf could pace a level shelf and write. A girl with a stiff ankle could tell him whether a hundred yards of it would bear her, and a researcher who could hold a pencil for a quarter of an hour had a cord, and a clock, and a head that kept numbers as a jar keeps water. Nobody would have to be fast, or hard, or to set anything alight; the job asked for no Path from any of them, and for no fragment of his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:77:"You want to tell a ford it holds a cart, you'd better have had one on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:177:"The hold's woman said not to lift."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:197:*Seventy-first day. The east road, above the wash. Taken at the plank yesterday: a hold's slip, Oxhollow's, for the high traverse between that hold and Thornwater. Walked with a cart and mapped. Seventy marks, paid at Oxhollow on the map. We walk it from this end and come down on his.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:19:She said it quite quietly. Two lines that had stood over theirs since the day they came now stood under them, and the shingle pair were at the foot still. Cael looked for what the rung was made of, since he had learned in this room that it was always made of something. It was a mountain, less a cart. The wall had no place to say so. It said only that a hold which asked Pike who ought to walk a bad hill would now be given five names before it was given two others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:63:He stood in front of them for as long as it took to be sure what he felt. He had asked for that word once, on a ford, and had it struck with one line while he was on a ledge with a string of salt; and he had never grudged the striking, since a hold cannot wait on a captain's convenience. Now two holds had come down to a ferry and asked a one-armed man who they ought to have. That was the board doing its work. If he minded it, he would have to mind arithmetic.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:77:He was given it, as he had been given a struck slip on the fifty-seventh day. It was good paper, heavier than anything the holds wrote on. He read it from the first word to the seal, and then he began again at the top, more slowly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:99:It was all either of them said about it. There was a fence on the oak, four slips along: the axe-head hold's longer fence on the east road, forty marks, HOLD. Teague drew its tack and held it out, and Pike wrote his name to it before he shut the book and tucked it under what was left of his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:123:"He says that there is a place on a road where an Arbiter stops. He says it stops at a line, and that a person knows the line by her own. If that is so, then it can be found with nothing but feet, by anybody, and found again in the same place the next day by somebody else. I have been taught that an Arbiter cannot be tested at all; that it is the one thing every instrument we own takes for granted. I should like to stand where one fails." She drew a breath. "I would go for nothing. I say so in order that nobody should count my hand as a vote for the hundred."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:147:He had a good many reasons. He could have said what he had said to Lira under the eave, that he wished to see it, and it would have been true. He could have said that a hold had asked for them by name, and that a crew which was asked for ought to find out what it was made of. There was a rope with no figure against it. There was a cart in a gorge, and a line in a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:167:"There is a margin. It has been pricked, here and here, at even distances, with a wheel; that is done so that every sheet from a desk is ruled alike. No hold has such a wheel. The broker's clerk writes on whatever comes to hand." His finger moved to the head of the sheet. "*Recovery.* One word, set by itself, with a stop after it. That is how a class of business is named at the top of a paper that will afterward be put away with others of its class. A carter would have begun, *I have lost.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:215:*Ninetieth day. Lowmarch. The seventh line is said: seventy in the purse, sixty-four on the wall. Tenth. The smith is paid, and we have a hundred and eight. A rope is owed to the ferryman, with no figure. Two holds have asked for us by name, and will wait a fortnight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:217:*Twelve weeks. Seven contracts. Pike says there's ground out here where the Arbiter goes quiet, and nobody goes in twice. Karis has been waiting her whole life for a sentence like that. Seln says the contract was written by someone from inside. I say it pays. Two days east, through Thornwater, on the day the healer said. For twelve weeks nothing out here has read me. I'd like to see what a place that reads nothing does with that.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:231:The hide-woman had her arms folded. Under the ash the old carter had lifted his chin at the first word and was holding it there. Two men in tarred aprons had stopped with a bundle of shingles between them; and the boy who took the standing-money stood on one leg beside a tent-rope, with his bag forgotten in his hand. That was ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:253:"The two holds," he said to Pike. "With our names. We'll be nine days on this, if it goes as it's written. If we're not down in their fortnight, don't keep a bull waiting on us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:11:The six on the rim were where they had been all day: two on the brow above him, two away to the south, one north, and one far off across the low ground against the last of the light. The two on the brow were on their feet; the one with the bow had it up across his chest, spanned, and the other was holding his own hands out before him and looking into them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:81:He saw the fingers want to. He saw them hold their stillness for one breath, and for a second, with the heat coming up into them. Then they closed, slowly, and the hands drew back, and went down to the man's sides.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:123:"There. Back," she said. It came out of her on one breath, hoarse, as a person speaks who has been holding it since the morning. She opened her eyes and looked at her own left hand in the lamplight, back and palm. "The clock started again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:127:"Down. Here, on the blanket, on your left side. You, bring that light and hold it where I point. You, the pannier, the one that's on the grey." She had Brom on the turf before any of them had moved. "And somebody give me a knife that'll cut ash."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:129:It was Seln's. She had him kneel behind Brom's shoulder and cut through the shaft below the iron head, a notch at a time, while she held the wood still in both her fists so that it should not turn. It took as long as it takes to say a line over twice; then the head came away, and she threw it on the grass, and put one hand flat on the front of the shoulder and took hold of the feathered end with the other.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:149:"It held a stair. It's not going to hold anything else this season."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:151:"It did hold it," said Lira. Her voice was small. "I'd have gone down the other twenty on my head."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:173:"A fence that failed at dusk, at its knot, which was set on an iron bar that stands upon the very edge of the place; and directly afterward a boy, who stood up out of the old road beside that bar and declared. Nobody on that rim saw anything between the stair and the bar. I asked Lira what she saw from the thirty-fourth step, and she says that she saw a stair. They will write that he crept up a rut in bad light while they were watching a hole, and that a knot tied a hand's breadth from such an edge is a knot which nobody should have trusted. A man who lays fences for his living would far sooner put that on paper than the other thing, and he has no cause to think of the other thing at all. It holds."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:199:*Shadow: once, from the fourth step to the old bar. Lira saw it go, from below. Seln turned his back. Three were round the door. The six were two hundred and sixty yards off, with a lip between; not one had a line to me. Let go in the rut. It holds. Dead to the elbows; I had to watch my own hand.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:13:"Then you'll be an hour or two with your backs to the sky. I'd not care to be, after yesterday." His grey eyes came round. "We'll hold it till you're up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:35:"As you found it last night: your Arbiter will tell you which foot has gone over."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:85:She had said at the stone that she would not sit outside a second day and listen. Four people who knew what a crossbow was were holding the rim, and she meant to be where a certain shoulder was; if anything opened, it could be carried up forty steps and two hundred yards with her walking beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:215:It was so unlike him that all five of them looked up. He was holding the book quite still, a hand's breadth from the horn of the lantern, and his eyes were going along one line of pencil from its beginning to its end and back again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:227:Karis had let go of it. She had been kneeling at the lowest course, with her left hand spread to hold the sheet and her right about to begin; and she had come up off her knees as if somebody had laid a cold hand between her shoulders.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:245:Lira had picked up the fallen one already, and was holding it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:255:It was there, as it was every time. Lira could not hold a sheet against a wall and rub it; that wanted two hands. But she had the roll under her right arm and the pebbles in her belt, and before the first hour was out she had learned the walls better than their reader. She had counted the courses, and had seen that a group which stood in the second course of the east wall came again in the fourth course of the south; and she was standing at the south wall with a sheet torn off and ready while Karis was still on her knees at the east.
