+19. `manuscript/chapter-35.md`
+   old: `It leaned at the tall book, when her hand turned a leaf. *Sits*, it would have said of that page, if he had let it: he could feel the word waiting, flat and level, like a board laid on two trestles. It leaned at the lamp-ring, and at the joint of the table's leg, a foot from his knee, where one peg did the work and another had been put in for show. He told it no each time. It was an easier thing to refuse than Shadow, which pulled, or the wall, which asked; this one only offered, like a clerk holding out a sheet that need not be taken.`
+   new: `The tall book, the lamp-ring, and the joint of the table's leg were all made things he could turn it upon. He did not. He kept it to the fifth row and the board, and kept the light where it was wanted.`
+
+20. `manuscript/chapter-35.md`
+   old: `It would take no time; he need not so much as shift his knee. He had only to let the thing come round, as it kept trying to come round to a table's leg, and turn it instead upon the one place in himself that he had never been able to read.`
+   new: `It would take no time; he need not so much as shift his knee. He had only to turn the thing upon the one place in himself that he had never been able to read.`
